values
  ('control-plane-hosting:render', 'control-plane-hosting', 'Render', 'Render Services, Inc.', 'Render is a full-stack PaaS — git push (or Docker) to a managed web service, with a dashboard/YAML blueprint for config, that''s already running this API today.', '$7/mo Starter (512MB RAM, 0.5 CPU); $25/mo Standard (2GB/1CPU); $85/mo Pro; up to $450/mo Pro Ultra. Free tier exists but sleeps.', '["Already running this exact API — render.yaml, health check and build command are all proven in production", "$7/mo Starter is always-on, no cold start, and is the tier already budgeted", "SOC 2 Type II and ISO 27001 certified, and Render''s own pricing page mentions HIPAA BAA availability at higher tiers", "IaC via render.yaml blueprint is already the repo''s source of truth for the service"]'::jsonb, '["No FedRAMP / GovCloud offering at any tier — no mention on Render''s own pricing/compliance page", "IPv4/IPv6 outbound specifics are not published; the DATABASE_URL landmine (IPv6-only direct Supabase host failing as ENETUNREACH) was discovered by trial, not documented by Render", "Single region (this app: Virginia) — no built-in multi-region failover on the Starter/Standard tiers", "A slow agent invoke launched from the browser hits the Netlify proxy''s ~30s ceiling even though Render itself allows 60s — a self-inflicted architecture cost, not Render''s, but only visible once deployed"]'::jsonb, 'When the honest answer is ''nothing is broken here'' — Render is proven, cheap, and already wired into render.yaml, netlify.toml and DEPLOY.md.', 'Strong incumbent fit: zero migration cost, and the one real gap (FedRAMP) was already named in project docs as requiring a separate deployment regardless of host chosen.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://render.com/pricing', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 7, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Starter plan: $7/mo (512MB RAM, 0.5 CPU) — already the plan this API runs on today (render.yaml).", "caveat": "Standard ($25/mo), Pro ($85/mo), Pro Ultra ($450/mo) tiers for higher RAM/CPU not modeled; a free tier exists but sleeps on inactivity."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('control-plane-hosting:fly-io', 'control-plane-hosting', 'Fly.io', 'Fly.io, Inc.', 'Fly.io runs your app as lightweight Firecracker VMs (''Machines'') close to users, deployed via a Dockerfile and a fly.toml, with a real CLI-driven IaC story.', 'Pay-as-you-go by the second: a shared-cpu-1x/256MB machine runs ~$2-3/mo, 1GB ~$6-9/mo depending on region; legacy plans keep up to 3 free 256MB VMs; dedicated static IPv4 costs $2/mo extra, IPv6 is free and unlimited.', '["Cheapest true always-on compute in this set for a service this small (256MB-1GB) — often under $10/mo", "fly.toml is real IaC, checked into the repo like render.yaml already is", "SOC 2 Type II certified and runs in ISO 27001 datacenters, confirmed on Fly''s own compliance page", "IPv6 is free and unlimited, useful if a future dependency ever requires it the way the retired Supabase direct host did"]'::jsonb, '["No FedRAMP/GovCloud — not mentioned anywhere in Fly''s own compliance documentation", "New pay-as-you-go accounts get zero free compute allowance, unlike the older Hobby/Launch plans", "A full migration off Render: new Dockerfile, fly.toml, volumes, and a from-scratch cutover of DNS/proxy wiring in netlify.toml", "Smaller company than Render with a more DIY operational model — more knobs to get wrong for a one-person shop"]'::jsonb, 'When raw compute cost matters more than anything else and the owner is comfortable with a slightly more hands-on CLI/Dockerfile workflow.', 'Technically compatible (long-lived process, stdio MCP server both work fine) but the migration cost is real and not offset by a compelling capability gain over the incumbent.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://fly.io/docs/about/pricing/', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 2.5, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Smallest shared-cpu-1x/256MB machine runs approximately $2-3/month pay-as-you-go by the second; midpoint used.", "caveat": "1GB machines run $6-9/mo; dedicated static IPv4 adds $2/mo; legacy free tier (up to 3 small 256MB VMs) not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('control-plane-hosting:railway', 'control-plane-hosting', 'Railway', 'Railway Corp.', 'Railway is a git-push PaaS with per-second usage billing and TOML/JSON config-as-code, popular for hobby-to-small-team Node apps.', 'Hobby plan: $5/mo minimum usage; Pro: $20/mo minimum. Usage billed by the second (~$0.0000039/GB-sec RAM, ~$0.0000077/vCPU-sec) — a small always-on Node service typically lands in the $5-15/mo range.', '["Usage-based billing means a small idle-mostly service can cost less than Render''s flat $7/mo Starter", "SOC 2 compliant on all tiers per Railway''s own pricing page", "Config-as-code (TOML/JSON) exists for a git-based deploy, similar in spirit to render.yaml", "Simple, modern dashboard UX, well liked for small Node/Express services"]'::jsonb, '["No free-tier always-on option — the Free plan caps at $1 minimum and effectively requires a paid plan for anything persistent", "HIPAA BAA only appears at the $1,000+/mo Pro commitment tier — a serious jump from the $5-20/mo range this system needs", "No FedRAMP/GovCloud mentioned anywhere in Railway''s public materials", "Full migration cost, same as Fly: new deploy config, no render.yaml equivalent already written"]'::jsonb, 'When usage is genuinely spiky rather than steady, so per-second billing beats a flat monthly tier.', 'A steady, always-on Express API with a long-lived pg.Pool doesn''t especially benefit from usage-based billing over Render''s flat Starter tier — the win is marginal and the migration cost is not.', 'n/a', 'growing', 'https://railway.com/pricing', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 5, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 5, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Hobby plan: $5/month minimum usage charge; a small always-on Node service typically lands $5-15/mo (Railway pricing, Aug 2026).", "caveat": "Actual cost scales with per-second GB-RAM/vCPU usage; $5 is used as the floor, not a guaranteed final bill; Pro plan ($20/mo minimum) not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('control-plane-hosting:azure-container-apps', 'control-plane-hosting', 'Azure Container Apps', 'Microsoft Corporation', 'Azure''s serverless-containers product: deploy a container, set min replicas ≥1 for always-on, pay per vCPU-second and GiB-second of actual usage.', 'Consumption plan: first 180,000 vCPU-seconds, 360,000 GiB-seconds and 2M requests/month free; exact per-second rates beyond the free grant were not shown on the public pricing page in this research pass (region/calculator-dependent) — plan on roughly $15-30/mo for a small always-on service as an unverified estimate, not a vendor-quoted figure.', '["Free monthly grant (180k vCPU-sec / 360k GiB-sec) may cover a genuinely light always-on workload close to $0", "Native to Azure, which is this project''s own documented eventual production target (''production target = Azure'')", "Min-replica=1 configuration keeps the container warm, avoiding cold starts on the primary always-on path", "Azure Government regions are named on Microsoft''s own Postgres Flexible Server pricing page, a signal (not confirmed specifically for Container Apps) that a government-cloud path exists somewhere in the platform"]'::jsonb, '["Exact per-second consumption rates are not published on the public pricing page — real cost requires the interactive calculator, a bad sign for confident budgeting", "A real migration: containerizing the API (a Dockerfile doesn''t exist yet), plus Azure account setup, resource group, and a new CI/CD path replacing render.yaml entirely", "Azure''s console complexity is a real operational-burden cost for a one-person shop, even before FedRAMP questions", "FedRAMP/Azure Government eligibility for Container Apps specifically was not confirmed in this pass"]'::jsonb, 'If and when the project''s own stated ''production target = Azure'' actually gets scheduled — this would be the natural landing spot for a containerized API.', 'Directionally the right vendor (matches the project''s own long-term stack target) but not yet worth the migration cost while Render is working and no Azure-specific need is active.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/pricing/details/container-apps/', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 22.5, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Free grant covers 180,000 vCPU-seconds, 360,000 GiB-seconds and 2M requests/month; beyond that, an unverified estimate of roughly $15-30/mo for a small always-on service (midpoint used), since exact per-second rates were not shown on the public page in this pass.", "caveat": "This range is explicitly flagged as an unverified estimate, not a vendor-quoted figure."}'::jsonb, 'chosen', 'Josh 2026-08-05: web app, API and jobs all run in Azure Container Apps, one environment per env.', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('control-plane-hosting:azure-app-service', 'control-plane-hosting', 'Azure App Service', 'Microsoft Corporation', 'Azure''s traditional managed web-app PaaS (App Service Plans, e.g. B1/S1 tiers) — an always-on VM-backed model rather than Container Apps'' scale-to-zero model.', 'Basic B1 (Linux): third-party cost breakdowns report roughly $12-13/mo (1 core, 1.75GB RAM); Microsoft''s own interactive pricing page would not render a static dollar figure without selecting a region in this research pass, so treat the exact number as unverified.', '["Simple, VM-backed always-on model — no scale-to-zero cold-start risk the way Container Apps or Cloud Run have on an idle tier", "B1 tier price is in the same ballpark as Render''s Starter ($7/mo) based on third-party aggregator figures", "Mature product with 15+ years of operational track record", "Azure Government region names appear on a sibling Azure product''s (Postgres Flexible Server) pricing page, suggesting a government-cloud path likely exists somewhere on the platform"]'::jsonb, '["Microsoft''s own pricing page would not display a concrete number in this session — a bad sign for a small shop trying to budget without a sales/calculator session", "No confirmed FedRAMP authorization specific to App Service — general Azure Government eligibility does not automatically mean this specific product is FedRAMP Moderate authorized", "Same full-migration cost as any non-Render option: new deploy pipeline, Azure account, resource group setup", "''Always-on'' is itself a setting that must be explicitly enabled on lower tiers — a real gotcha only found by reading Azure''s docs closely"]'::jsonb, 'Same as Container Apps — worth it once the project actually moves toward Azure, not as a speculative switch today.', 'A workable long-term Azure landing spot, but the un-quotable public pricing page is itself a bad omen for a small shop that dislikes the kind of ambiguity that already cost hours on the DATABASE_URL landmine.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/pricing/details/app-service/linux/', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 12.5, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Basic B1 (1 core, 1.75GB RAM): third-party cost breakdowns report roughly $12-13/mo; Microsoft''s own interactive pricing page requires a region selection and did not render a static figure in this pass.", "caveat": "Treat as an unverified estimate, not a vendor-quoted number."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('control-plane-hosting:aws-app-runner', 'control-plane-hosting', 'AWS App Runner', 'Amazon Web Services, Inc.', 'AWS''s simplest ''push code or container, get a running web service'' PaaS, sitting between raw ECS/Fargate and nothing at all.', '$0.064/vCPU-hour + $0.007/GB-hour while actively processing requests, plus a lower provisioned/idle rate to keep the container warm; AWS''s own worked example shows ~$4.80/mo for a 1vCPU/2GB service with only 2 active hours/day, rising toward $25+/mo with heavier active use.', '["Provisioned-instance model keeps low-latency always-warm behavior without full 24/7 active-rate billing, potentially cheaper than Render''s flat $7/mo for a genuinely low-traffic control plane", "Sits inside the AWS ecosystem, useful if any consumer app ever standardizes on AWS", "AWS''s enterprise-grade reliability and networking track record", "AWS GovCloud exists as a platform generally, though App Runner''s specific availability there was not confirmed"]'::jsonb, '["No FedRAMP/GovCloud availability was found for App Runner specifically in this research pass", "AWS''s dual provisioned/active billing model is the least intuitive of any option researched — genuinely hard for a non-architect owner to predict a bill from", "A full migration: containerizing the app, setting up an AWS account/IAM, and replacing render.yaml with App Runner''s own service definition", "AWS''s general reputation for a steeper learning curve and easier-to-fumble IAM/networking configuration than Render''s dashboard"]'::jsonb, 'If the consumer-app ecosystem already leans AWS and low, spiky traffic makes the provisioned/active split genuinely cheaper than a flat monthly host.', 'No AWS dependency exists elsewhere in this project today, so the ecosystem argument doesn''t apply yet, and the billing complexity cuts against the project''s own ''operational burden is a first-class cost'' rule.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://aws.amazon.com/apprunner/pricing/', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 4.8, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "AWS''s own worked example: roughly $4.80/mo for a 1vCPU/2GB service processing requests only 2 active hours/day, at $0.064/vCPU-hour + $0.007/GB-hour.", "caveat": "Cost rises toward $25+/mo with heavier active use; a lower idle/provisioned rate applies when not actively processing, not itemized separately here."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('control-plane-hosting:google-cloud-run', 'control-plane-hosting', 'Google Cloud Run', 'Google LLC', 'Google''s serverless-containers platform — deploy a container, optionally set min-instances=1 for always-on, pay per vCPU-second/GiB-second.', '$0.000018/vCPU-sec and $0.000002/GiB-sec (instance-based); with min-instances=1 (request-based idle billing) a 1vCPU/512MB always-on service works out to roughly $10/mo before the free tier, which covers about 50 hours of that workload — realistically $8-10/mo net.', '["Free tier (180k-240k vCPU-seconds + 360k-450k GiB-seconds/month) meaningfully offsets a small workload''s bill", "Google''s global network and container-native model is a solid operational fit for a stateless-ish Express app, once containerized", "Comparable real-world price to Render''s $7/mo Starter once the free tier is applied", "Mature, widely-used product with strong documentation"]'::jsonb, '["No FedRAMP/compliance information was found on Cloud Run''s own pricing page in this research pass — needs a direct compliance-page check before ruling in or out for the regulated question", "Full migration: containerizing, gcloud CLI/Google Cloud project setup, and a new CI/CD path replacing render.yaml", "Min-instances=1 avoids cold starts but forfeits scale-to-zero savings — this system''s long-lived pg.Pool and stdio MCP server require paying the always-on idle rate, not the cheaper burst-to-zero pattern Cloud Run is famous for", "No existing Google Cloud footprint anywhere else in this project, so there''s no ecosystem synergy to offset the migration cost"]'::jsonb, 'When a consumer app already lives on Google Cloud, or free-tier headroom genuinely covers a very light workload''s compute needs.', 'Workable but not compelling: the free tier helps, but Cloud Run''s real strength (scale-to-zero) is exactly the thing this system''s long-lived pool and stdio server can''t use.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://cloud.google.com/run/pricing', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 9, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "With min-instances=1 (always-on), a 1vCPU/512MB service works out to roughly $10/mo before the free tier (which covers about 50 hours), netting roughly $8-10/mo; midpoint used.", "caveat": "Actual cost scales with vCPU-seconds/GiB-seconds if instance-based billing or traffic varies; based on Google''s own worked shape, not an exact quote."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('control-plane-hosting:digitalocean-app-platform', 'control-plane-hosting', 'DigitalOcean App Platform', 'DigitalOcean, LLC', 'DigitalOcean''s git-push PaaS for containerized or buildpack-detected apps, with straightforward flat per-container pricing (no usage metering).', '$5/mo for a 1 vCPU / 512MB shared-CPU container with 50GB transfer — DigitalOcean removed its old Basic/Professional tiers in favor of this simpler, cheaper modular model.', '["Cheapest flat always-on price researched in this set — $5/mo undercuts Render''s $7/mo Starter for a comparable 512MB container", "DigitalOcean holds SOC 2 Type II certification and Cloud Security Alliance STAR Level 1, confirmed via their own investor/trust pages", "Simple, predictable flat pricing (no per-second usage math to explain to a non-developer owner)", "DigitalOcean''s reputation for straightforward, small-shop-friendly UX is well-established"]'::jsonb, '["No FedRAMP/GovCloud availability found anywhere in DigitalOcean''s public compliance materials — this is a firmly commercial-only cloud", "Full migration cost identical to Fly/Railway: new deploy config, DO account, and rewiring the Netlify proxy target", "No IaC/blueprint equivalent to render.yaml was confirmed on the App Platform pricing page in this pass", "Smaller platform ecosystem than AWS/Azure/GCP if this project''s compliance needs ever require more specialized government-cloud options"]'::jsonb, 'When the goal is simply ''cheaper than Render, just as simple'' with no other requirement in play.', 'A legitimate cost-savings option ($5 vs $7/mo) with comparable simplicity to Render, but the savings ($24/yr) are too small to justify migration effort absent another reason to move.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://www.digitalocean.com/pricing/app-platform', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 5, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "$5/month flat for a 1 vCPU/512MB shared-CPU container with 50GB transfer, no usage metering (DigitalOcean pricing).", "caveat": "Larger container sizes cost more; this is the smallest tier only."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('control-plane-hosting:hetzner-vps', 'control-plane-hosting', 'Hetzner Cloud VPS', 'Hetzner Online GmbH', 'A plain, unmanaged Hetzner Cloud VPS (e.g. CX22) that you SSH into and run Node/PM2/systemd on yourself — the ''just a box'' option, no PaaS layer at all.', 'CX22: about €4.35/mo (~$4.70/mo) for 2 shared vCPU, 4GB RAM, 40GB NVMe — by far the cheapest raw compute of any option here, though this is a bare VM, not a managed deploy target.', '["Cheapest compute per dollar of any option researched — 4GB RAM/2vCPU for under $5/mo, several times what Render''s $7/mo Starter gives", "Full control: no PaaS constraints, run the stdio MCP server and pg.Pool exactly as built with zero platform-imposed limits", "No usage-based billing surprises — a flat monthly VM price", "Hetzner has a strong reputation for cheap, reliable hardware"]'::jsonb, '["This is the daemon-to-operate the project explicitly wants to avoid: OS patching, process supervision (systemd/PM2), TLS, firewall, deploy scripting, and monitoring are now entirely the owner''s job, with zero managed layer", "No compliance certifications were found for Hetzner in this research pass — would need a direct check before any regulated use", "No IaC/blueprint parity with render.yaml exists unless the owner builds one (e.g. a small Ansible/shell script) — that is itself new engineering work", "A single VM with no managed health-check/restart/auto-redeploy story is a worse operational-burden trade than every PaaS option in this set, for a one-person shop"]'::jsonb, 'Only if compute cost is the dominant constraint and the owner is willing to accept full ops ownership — a poor match for ''operational burden is a first-class cost'' as stated in this project''s own rules.', 'Cheapest in dollars, most expensive in the operational-burden currency this project explicitly weighs — the wrong trade for a control plane an owner needs to trust unattended.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://www.hetzner.com/cloud/', '{"kind": "byo", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 4.7, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "CX22: about EUR4.35/mo (~$4.70/mo) for 2 shared vCPU, 4GB RAM, 40GB NVMe (Hetzner Cloud pricing).", "caveat": "Unmanaged bare VM — no PaaS layer, deploy tooling, or managed backups included; operator time to run Node/PM2/systemd is not priced."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 80),
  ('control-plane-hosting:cloudflare-workers', 'control-plane-hosting', 'Cloudflare Workers', 'Cloudflare, Inc.', 'Cloudflare''s V8-isolate serverless compute platform — genuinely stateless, request-scoped functions, not a container or VM.', 'Free: 100k requests/day, 10ms CPU/invocation. Paid: $5/mo minimum, 10M requests + 30M CPU-ms included, then $0.30/million requests + $0.02/million CPU-ms.', '["Cheapest nominal price in this set ($5/mo minimum) if the workload actually fit the model", "Massive global edge network and effectively zero cold-start latency for the parts of an app that ARE request-scoped", "Hyperdrive exists specifically to let Workers reach a Postgres database without holding their own connection pool", "Confirmed directly from Cloudflare''s own docs: execution terminates at the end of each request — this is architecturally explicit, not ambiguous"]'::jsonb, '["Does not fit this system at all: Workers cannot run a long-lived pg.Pool or a stdio MCP server — both require a persistent process, which Workers'' execution model structurally forbids", "Even with Hyperdrive, every request pays a per-connection setup cost that a real pool amortizes away — the wrong trade for a control plane doing many small queries", "A stdio MCP server requires a long-running process attached to a stdio stream; Workers has no equivalent primitive at all", "Adopting Workers would mean rewriting the API''s connection and MCP-transport model from scratch, not just redeploying it — a rewrite, not a migration"]'::jsonb, 'For a genuinely stateless HTTP API or edge-caching layer — never for a long-lived process holding a database pool or a stdio server.', 'Ruled out on architecture, not price: confirmed directly from Cloudflare''s own docs that neither a long-lived pg.Pool nor a stdio MCP server can run here.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/pricing/', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "api_calls", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 3e-07, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free: 100k requests/day (~3M/mo), 10ms CPU/invocation. Paid: $5/mo minimum includes 10M requests + 30M CPU-ms, then $0.30/million requests + $0.02/million CPU-ms.", "caveat": "CPU-ms overage ($0.02/million) is not modeled separately from the request-based per_unit rate.", "tiers": [{"upto": 3000000, "monthly": 0}, {"upto": 10000000, "monthly": 5}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 90),
  ('postgres-host:supabase', 'postgres-host', 'Supabase', 'Supabase, Inc.', 'Supabase is a managed Postgres platform (plus optional auth/storage/edge functions this project deliberately doesn''t use for its own auth) — the dev database this system already runs on.', 'Pro: $25/mo base, including $10/mo compute credit (covers one Micro instance: 1GB RAM, 2-core ARM), 8GB disk, 250GB egress, 100GB file storage, daily backups with 7-day retention. Free tier: 500MB DB, 2 projects, pauses after a week of inactivity.', '["Already the live dev database — zero migration cost, and every landmine (Supavisor session pooler on 5432, direct host retired) is already documented and worked around", "Plain Postgres 17 under the hood — the project''s own ''plain SQL, standard Postgres'' rule is satisfied by construction", "Supavisor connection pooling is built in and already the required path for the long-lived pg.Pool", "SOC 2 Type 2 and ISO 27001 certified per Supabase''s own pricing page footer"]'::jsonb, '["This project is explicitly on the dev tier today with no staging and no second copy to restore from — a real risk independent of vendor, but the current plan level compounds it", "No FedRAMP/GovCloud status found on Supabase''s pricing page in this pass", "Daily backups with only 7-day retention on Pro — point-in-time recovery is a separate paid add-on not itemized in the base $25/mo", "Compute credit model means real cost can exceed $25/mo the moment usage grows past the Micro instance included in the credit"]'::jsonb, 'Right now, for exactly the reason it''s already the host: proven, plain Postgres, and the known landmines are already documented rather than waiting to be rediscovered on a new vendor.', 'The clear incumbent-stays case: this project''s own hard rule (''plain SQL and standard Postgres only, so the host is a connection string'') is best honored by not moving at all absent a specific unmet need.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://supabase.com/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "storage_gb", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Pro: $25/mo base including $10/mo compute credit (one Micro instance, 1GB RAM/2-core ARM) and 8GB included disk; Free: 500MB DB (supabase.com/pricing).", "caveat": "Storage beyond 8GB, egress beyond 250GB, and file storage beyond 100GB are billed as separate line items not modeled here.", "tiers": [{"upto": 0.5, "monthly": 0}, {"upto": 8, "monthly": 25}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('postgres-host:neon', 'postgres-host', 'Neon', 'Neon, Inc.', 'Neon is a serverless Postgres platform built around branching and scale-to-zero autosuspend, popular for dev/preview environments and increasingly for production.', 'Free: 100 CU-hours/project, 0.5GB storage. Launch: $0.106/CU-hour compute + $0.35/GB-month storage, pay-as-you-go, no monthly minimum. Scale: $0.222/CU-hour, same storage rate.', '["Genuinely plain Postgres (not a proprietary flavor) — satisfies the project''s ''standard Postgres only'' rule the same as Supabase does", "Connection pooling is included on every tier per Neon''s own pricing page, addressing the long-lived pg.Pool need directly", "Pay-as-you-go with no monthly minimum could be cheaper than Supabase''s $25/mo Pro floor for a genuinely small workload", "Database branching (a full copy-on-write branch) is a real capability Supabase doesn''t offer at this price point — useful for testing migrations against production-like data"]'::jsonb, '["Autosuspend is the default behavior on paid tiers too (configurable 1 minute to always-on) — a control-plane API with a long-lived pg.Pool needs to explicitly disable autosuspend or accept reconnection latency on the first query after idle", "PITR retention is time-based (7 days Launch, 30 days Scale) rather than itemized as a clean backup guarantee the way this project''s own docs describe Supabase''s", "A full migration: new project, new connection string, re-verifying every DATABASE_URL landmine (pooler mode, IPv4/IPv6) from scratch on a different vendor''s pooler implementation", "No FedRAMP/GovCloud mention found; SOC 2 and HIPAA (BAA, no extra cost) are confirmed on Scale only, not Launch"]'::jsonb, 'When workload is genuinely spiky (dev/staging, low-traffic personal tools) and pay-as-you-go beats a flat monthly minimum, or when database branching for safe migration testing is worth paying for.', 'A credible peer to Supabase on the ''plain Postgres'' requirement, but autosuspend behavior needs a deliberate always-on configuration to avoid surprising latency on this system''s long-lived pool — not a drop-in win over the incumbent.', 'n/a', 'growing', 'https://neon.com/pricing', '{"kind": "usage_passthrough", "driver": "storage_gb", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.35, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Launch plan: pay-as-you-go, no monthly minimum — $0.106/CU-hour compute + $0.35/GB-month storage (Neon pricing).", "caveat": "Compute-hour charges ($0.106-$0.222/CU-hour depending on plan) are not captured by the storage_gb driver; Free tier (100 CU-hours/project, 0.5GB) not modeled as an included allowance."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('postgres-host:azure-postgres-flexible', 'postgres-host', 'Azure Database for PostgreSQL Flexible Server', 'Microsoft Corporation', 'Azure''s managed Postgres offering — the natural database counterpart if the API side ever moves to Azure Container Apps/App Service.', 'Burstable B1ms (1 vCore, 2GiB) is the smallest production tier; Microsoft''s own pricing page would not render a static dollar figure without a region selection in this research pass — third-party estimates commonly cite $12-25/mo depending on storage and region, unverified from a primary source.', '["Same ecosystem as this project''s own stated ''production target = Azure'', so choosing it would be a step toward, not away from, the documented plan", "Plain, standard Postgres under the hood — same ''connection string, not an architecture'' property Supabase and Neon share", "Azure Government regions (Arizona, Texas, Virginia) are explicitly named on this exact pricing page, the strongest signal of a real government-cloud path of any Postgres host researched", "Microsoft''s enterprise support and SLA track record"]'::jsonb, '["Public pricing page displays no concrete number without using the interactive calculator — the same transparency problem found on Azure''s compute products", "Backup/PITR retention specifics on the base tier were not confirmed in this pass and need a direct doc check", "A full migration: new Azure Postgres instance, re-verifying pooler behavior (does Azure''s pooler handle a long-lived pg.Pool the same way Supavisor does?) from scratch", "No existing Azure footprint anywhere in this project today, so this is a two-vendor migration (API AND database) if pursued in isolation from a broader Azure move"]'::jsonb, 'If and when the API side actually moves to Azure Container Apps/App Service — pairing both halves on one cloud simplifies networking and billing.', 'The database counterpart to the Azure compute options — same logic applies: right direction, wrong time absent an active Azure migration already underway.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/pricing/details/postgresql/flexible-server/', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 18.5, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Burstable B1ms (1 vCore, 2GiB) is the smallest production tier; third-party estimates commonly cite $12-25/mo depending on storage/region (unverified from a primary source); midpoint used.", "caveat": "Microsoft''s own pricing page requires region selection and did not render a static figure in this pass."}'::jsonb, 'chosen', 'Josh 2026-08-05: Azure Database for PostgreSQL Flexible Server is the system of record, with row-level security doing the tenant isolation.', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('postgres-host:aws-aurora-serverless-v2', 'postgres-host', 'AWS Aurora Serverless v2 (PostgreSQL)', 'Amazon Web Services, Inc.', 'Aurora Serverless v2 for PostgreSQL — AWS''s auto-scaling, Postgres-compatible managed database, billed by Aurora Capacity Unit (ACU).', '$0.12/ACU-hour (Aurora Standard); minimum capacity is 0.5 ACU, so a continuously-running minimum instance costs roughly $44/mo (0.5 x $0.12 x 730 hours) before storage/IO — notably more than Supabase''s $25/mo Pro floor.', '["Aurora''s storage layer auto-scales and is generally regarded as one of the most durable, battle-tested managed-database backends in the industry", "AWS''s compliance program is deep, with GovCloud and FedRAMP authorizations for AWS overall, though not independently confirmed for Aurora Serverless v2 specifically in this pass", "Fine-grained auto-scaling (ACU steps) could in principle track real usage more precisely than a fixed compute tier", "Mature, extremely well-documented product"]'::jsonb, '["Aurora PostgreSQL is Postgres-*compatible*, not stock Postgres — a proprietary storage engine behind a Postgres wire protocol, in real tension with this project''s explicit ''plain SQL and standard Postgres only'' rule", "$44/mo realistic floor is materially more expensive than Supabase''s $25/mo Pro tier for a workload this small", "No AWS footprint exists anywhere else in this project, so this is a from-scratch vendor relationship, IAM setup, and networking model to learn", "FedRAMP/GovCloud availability for Aurora Serverless v2 specifically was not confirmed in this pass and needs a direct compliance-page check"]'::jsonb, 'When true elastic scaling to real traffic spikes matters and the team already lives in AWS — not this project''s situation today.', 'Poor fit on the project''s own explicit rule: Aurora is not ''plain Postgres,'' and at $44+/mo minimum it''s also the most expensive option in this set for a workload this small.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://aws.amazon.com/rds/aurora/pricing/', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 44, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "derived", "basis": "Minimum capacity 0.5 ACU continuously running: 0.5 x $0.12/ACU-hour x 730 hours ~ $44/month, before storage/IO (AWS pricing).", "caveat": "Storage and I/O charges are additional and not included; scales up automatically (and in cost) under load."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('postgres-host:crunchy-bridge', 'postgres-host', 'Crunchy Bridge', 'Crunchy Data Solutions, Inc.', 'Crunchy Bridge is a managed Postgres company built entirely around Postgres itself — no proprietary wire-protocol compatibility layer, just Postgres with managed ops.', 'Hobby-0: $9/mo (2 burstable vCPU, 512MB RAM) but no PgBouncer connection pooling and no HA — not production-grade per Crunchy''s own docs. Standard-4: $70/mo (1 core, 4GB RAM) is the first tier with PgBouncer and standard support; storage billed separately at $0.10/GB/month.', '["Genuinely plain, unmodified Postgres — Crunchy Data is a long-standing pure-Postgres company, arguably the strongest ''standard Postgres only'' fit in this set", "SOC 2 Type 2 report and HIPAA compliance support confirmed on Crunchy''s own product page", "Fully-managed backups/PITR included in the base price at no extra cost, per their own pricing language", "Connection pooling and backup storage explicitly called out as included in the base price on paid production tiers"]'::jsonb, '["The genuinely cheap tier (Hobby-0, $9/mo) has NO connection pooling — a real problem for this system''s long-lived pg.Pool pattern, which needs PgBouncer or equivalent to avoid exhausting Postgres''s own connection limit", "The tier that does include pooling (Standard-4, $70/mo) is roughly 3x Supabase''s $25/mo Pro floor for comparable specs", "No FedRAMP/GovCloud mention found on Crunchy''s pricing or product pages in this pass", "A full migration off Supabase: new provider relationship, re-verifying every connection-string landmine from scratch"]'::jsonb, 'When ''as close to upstream Postgres as a managed vendor gets'' is the top priority and the $70/mo Standard tier''s price is acceptable for that assurance.', 'Philosophically the purest match for ''plain SQL and standard Postgres'' of any vendor researched, but the price for a pooling-capable tier is a real step up from the incumbent with no corresponding capability this system currently lacks.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://docs.crunchybridge.com/concepts/plans-pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "storage_gb", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.1, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Hobby-0: $9/mo (2 burstable vCPU, 512MB RAM) — cheapest tier, though not production-grade (no PgBouncer/HA per Crunchy''s own docs); storage billed separately at $0.10/GB/month.", "caveat": "Standard-4 ($70/mo, the first tier with PgBouncer and standard support) is the realistic production floor and is not modeled as the base; Hobby-0 lacks HA.", "tiers": [{"upto": 0, "monthly": 9}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('postgres-host:timescale-cloud', 'postgres-host', 'Timescale Cloud (Tiger Data)', 'Tiger Data, Inc. (formerly Timescale)', 'Timescale Cloud (rebranding toward ''Tiger Data'') is managed Postgres with built-in time-series extensions — usable as plain Postgres even if the time-series features go unused.', 'Performance plan: compute from $30/mo, 3-day PITR. Scale plan (the first genuinely production-grade tier): compute from $36/mo, 14-day PITR, HA replicas with automated failover; storage at $0.212/GB-month ($0.0424/GB effective after compression).', '["Scale plan''s 14-day PITR is the longest point-in-time-recovery window of any option in this set at a comparable price point", "Automated HA failover on the Scale tier is a genuine production-safety feature this project doesn''t currently have with the dev-tier Supabase project", "Still standard Postgres underneath — the time-series (hypertable) features are opt-in extensions, not a replacement for the base engine", "Hourly billing with no lock-in contracts, per their own pricing page"]'::jsonb, '["$36/mo Scale-plan floor is meaningfully above Supabase''s $25/mo Pro floor for a workload that doesn''t need Timescale''s actual specialty (time-series)", "Connection pooling behavior with a long-lived pg.Pool was not documented on the pricing page in this pass and needs a direct check", "No FedRAMP/GovCloud mention found anywhere in this research pass", "Paying for a time-series-optimized platform when the system has no time-series workload is buying a capability this project won''t use"]'::jsonb, 'If this control plane ever ingests genuinely time-series-shaped data at volume (e.g. dense agent-run telemetry) where hypertables'' compression and continuous aggregates would pay for themselves.', 'A real production-hardening upgrade (HA, 14-day PITR) over the incumbent''s current dev-tier posture, but the vendor''s core specialty (time-series) is unused here, making it a slightly odd fit versus a plainer general-purpose Postgres host at a similar price.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://www.tigerdata.com/pricing', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "storage_gb", "base_monthly": 36, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.212, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Scale plan (the first genuinely production-grade tier per the vendor): compute from $36/mo, 14-day PITR, HA with automated failover; storage at $0.212/GB-month ($0.0424 effective after compression).", "caveat": "Performance plan ($30/mo, 3-day PITR, no HA) not modeled as it is described as not fully production-grade."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('postgres-host:railway-postgres', 'postgres-host', 'Railway Postgres', 'Railway Corp.', 'A Postgres instance provisioned inside Railway, billed under the same per-second usage model as Railway''s compute — not a separately priced product.', 'No separate database price sheet; billed under the same Hobby ($5/mo minimum)/Pro ($20/mo minimum) usage model as Railway compute — a small always-on Postgres instance realistically lands in a similar $5-15/mo range as the compute side.', '["If the API also moved to Railway, database and compute live in one dashboard, one bill, one vendor relationship to manage — a real operational simplification for a one-person shop", "Standard Postgres image under the hood, not a proprietary fork", "Usage-based pricing could undercut Supabase''s flat $25/mo Pro floor for a genuinely light workload", "SOC 2 compliant per Railway''s own pricing page, same as its compute product"]'::jsonb, '["No managed connection pooler (PgBouncer/Supavisor equivalent) was confirmed for Railway''s Postgres offering in this research pass — a real risk for a long-lived pg.Pool pattern if unconfirmed", "Backup/PITR details for Railway Postgres specifically were not found in this pass and need direct verification before trusting it with the only copy of this data", "Only compelling as a bundle with Railway compute — evaluated as a standalone database swap (keeping Render for the API) it offers no benefit over Supabase", "No FedRAMP/GovCloud mention anywhere in Railway''s materials"]'::jsonb, 'Only as part of a full Railway migration (API and database together) — not worth adopting for the database alone while staying on Render for compute.', 'Weak standalone fit: the appeal is bundling with Railway compute, which this research set didn''t find a compelling independent reason to adopt either.', 'n/a', 'growing', 'https://railway.com/pricing', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 10, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 5, "