  ('postgres-host:digitalocean-managed-postgres', 'postgres-host', 'DigitalOcean Managed PostgreSQL', 'DigitalOcean, LLC', 'DigitalOcean''s managed PostgreSQL product — a straightforward, flat-priced managed database matching the simplicity of its App Platform.', '$15.15/mo for the smallest tier: 1 vCPU, 1GiB RAM, 10GiB storage (expandable at $0.215/GiB/month in 10GiB increments).', '["Flat, simple pricing with no usage-metering math, consistent with DigitalOcean''s App Platform pricing philosophy", "Standard Postgres, not a proprietary variant", "Pairs naturally with DigitalOcean App Platform if that were chosen for compute — one vendor for both halves", "DigitalOcean holds SOC 2 Type II and CSA STAR Level 1 certification, confirmed via their own trust pages"]'::jsonb, '["Backup/PITR retention policy and PgBouncer connection-pooling support were not documented on the pricing page itself in this research pass — both need direct verification before trusting this with the system of record", "$15.15/mo for 1GiB RAM is a downgrade in headline specs from Supabase''s Pro tier (1GB RAM but with pooling, backups and egress bundled into the $25/mo base)", "No FedRAMP/GovCloud availability found anywhere in DigitalOcean''s public materials", "Only clearly compelling if compute also moves to DigitalOcean App Platform — otherwise it''s a lateral, not obviously better, move from Supabase"]'::jsonb, 'As the database half of a DigitalOcean App Platform migration, if the compute side ever moves there for its lower flat price.', 'A reasonable, simple option but with two real unverified gaps (pooling, PITR) that would need closing before trusting it as the sole copy of this system''s control-plane data — a bar the incumbent already clears with documented answers.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://www.digitalocean.com/pricing/managed-databases', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "storage_gb", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.215, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "$15.15/mo for the smallest tier: 1 vCPU, 1GiB RAM, 10GiB storage; expandable at $0.215/GiB/month in 10GiB increments (DigitalOcean pricing).", "caveat": "Storage increments are sold in blocks of 10GiB, not smoothly per-GiB as modeled here.", "tiers": [{"upto": 10, "monthly": 15.15}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('postgres-host:self-managed-vps', 'postgres-host', 'Self-managed Postgres on a VPS', 'n/a — in-house, e.g. on Hetzner Cloud', 'Run Postgres yourself on a plain VPS (e.g. the same Hetzner CX22 considered for compute) — full control, zero managed-service fee, entirely your own operational responsibility.', 'No separate database fee beyond the VPS itself — e.g. Hetzner CX22 at ~EUR4.35/mo (~$4.70) already covers both a small Express API and a small Postgres instance on one box, if colocated.', '["Cheapest possible option in raw dollars — effectively free once a VPS is already paid for", "Zero vendor dependency for the database specifically — the connection string points at infrastructure the owner fully controls", "No usage-metering, egress fees, or per-GB storage surprises", "Full control over Postgres version, extensions, and configuration"]'::jsonb, '["This is exactly the daemon-to-operate this project''s own rules flag as expensive even at $0 license cost: backups, PITR, failover, security patching, and connection-pooler setup (PgBouncer) all become the owner''s unassisted responsibility", "The single biggest risk given this project''s own stated posture (''one environment... no staging, no second copy to restore from'') — a self-managed VPS with no managed backup service makes that risk strictly worse, not better", "No compliance certification of any kind — a non-starter the moment any regulated (DFARS/CUI) question becomes live for this specific host", "Every landmine currently absorbed by Supabase''s managed layer (pooler mode, IPv4/IPv6 behavior, backup scheduling) has to be independently rediscovered and then permanently maintained by one person"]'::jsonb, 'Never recommended as the primary store for this system while it remains a one-person shop — only plausible as a local dev/test throwaway instance.', 'The worst fit in this set for a system whose own documented rule is that operational burden is a first-class cost — this option maximizes exactly that burden to save single-digit dollars a month.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://www.hetzner.com/cloud/', '{"kind": "byo", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 4.7, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "No separate database fee beyond the VPS itself — e.g. Hetzner CX22 at ~$4.70/mo already covers both a small Express API and a small Postgres instance if colocated on one box.", "caveat": "This is a shared cost with the control-plane-hosting Hetzner option if colocated on the same box — don''t double-count if both are selected together; operator time not priced."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 80),
  ('job-scheduling:azure-container-apps-jobs', 'job-scheduling', 'Azure Container Apps Jobs', 'Microsoft', 'Scheduled and event-driven job execution inside Azure Container Apps, run in the same environment as the app''s regular container revisions.', 'As of Aug 2026, per Microsoft''s own billing docs and the Azure Retail Prices API: Jobs are NOT a separate billable product — they consume the same vCPU-second and GiB-second consumption meters as Container Apps itself (confirmed active rates ~$0.000024/vCPU-sec and ~$0.000003-0.000007/GiB-sec, region-dependent), billed only for the wall-clock duration each execution actually runs, with no idle charge once it completes. The same subscription-wide free grant applies: first 180,000 vCPU-seconds, 360,000 GiB-seconds, and 2,000,000 requests per month, shared across the whole Container Apps environment (jobs plus apps).', '["No separate line item or environment to provision — reuses the app''s existing Container Apps environment and free grant", "Billed only while an execution runs; a cron job that fires for 10 seconds a day costs about 10 seconds a day", "Event-driven trigger mode (e.g. on a queue) fits webhook-style async work, not just cron"]'::jsonb, '["Cold start on each execution is a real container start (image pull/cache + init), typically single-digit seconds to tens of seconds depending on image size — not sub-second like a warm function", "Sharing the free grant with the main app means a chatty job schedule quietly eats into the app''s own free 180k vCPU-seconds", "Needs a container image and registry, more moving parts than a managed cron trigger on a PaaS scheduler"]'::jsonb, 'A team that already runs its API on Container Apps and wants cron/queue-triggered jobs without standing up a second compute product.', 'Once the Integrator API itself is likely to move to Container Apps, this is the natural home for its cron-style background work (e.g. weekly-ops-brief) — but it should never appear as its own line in a cost model, since it bills as consumption on the environment already being paid for.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/billing', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "tiers": [], "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Microsoft Learn ''Billing in Azure Container Apps'' confirms Jobs consume the same active-rate vCPU-second/GiB-second meters as the Container Apps environment, with no distinct Jobs charge and no idle billing between executions.", "caveat": "Already counted under the Azure Container Apps environment''s own consumption line — do not add a second line for Jobs, or the calculator double-counts the same vCPU/GiB-second meters."}'::jsonb, 'chosen', 'Josh 2026-08-05: migrate / sync / expire / infer, all the same Go binary with a different command, so a job and the API can never disagree about a rule.', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('job-scheduling:pg-cron', 'job-scheduling', 'pg_cron (Postgres extension / Supabase Cron)', 'PostgreSQL / Supabase (hosted as ''Supabase Cron'')', 'pg_cron is a Postgres extension that runs SQL, database functions, or (via pg_net) HTTP calls on a cron schedule, from inside the database itself — already the mechanism the sibling Master-Plan project uses for its weekly brief and standing syncs.', 'Free — it''s a Postgres extension, included on Supabase''s hosted plans at no extra charge (documented as ''Supabase Cron'', built on the same pg_cron extension).', '["Zero new infrastructure or vendor — runs inside the Postgres already being used, with no separate service to keep alive", "Every run is logged to cron.job_run_details, giving a queryable audit table for free", "Already proven at Master-Plan scale (that project''s real weekly-brief and sync jobs run on it today)", "Matches the ''everything variable is a row'' philosophy — a scheduled job is a row in cron.job, not a deployed service"]'::jsonb, '["CONFIRMED (from pg_net''s own GitHub documentation) the exact failure mode this project''s memory warns about: net.http_post is asynchronous and fire-and-forget — it only confirms the request was queued into net.http_request_queue, not that it received a successful HTTP response. cron.job_run_details records the queue-insert as ''succeeded'' regardless of the real outcome, which is precisely how Master-Plan''s 08-16 weekly brief silently failed to run", "The real HTTP outcome lives in a separate table, net._http_response, that nothing queries automatically — detecting a silent failure requires a second job that specifically checks that table and alerts, which is not built by default", "Individual jobs are recommended to stay under 10 minutes with a soft cap of roughly 8 concurrent jobs — not built for heavy background-processing workloads", "No native retry logic — a failed job just logs its (misleading) status and waits for the next scheduled tick"]'::jsonb, 'For simple, low-stakes scheduled SQL or HTTP-trigger jobs where the owner is willing to build the second ''did it actually work'' check against net._http_response — exactly the fix Master-Plan needs after its 08-16 miss.', 'The cheapest and most architecturally consistent option, but only if the fire-and-forget gap is closed with an explicit response-checking job — adopting it without that fix repeats the exact failure this project''s own memory already documents.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://github.com/supabase/pg_net', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free — a Postgres extension, included on Supabase''s hosted plans at no extra charge (documented as ''Supabase Cron'').", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('job-scheduling:supabase-scheduled-functions', 'job-scheduling', 'Supabase Scheduled Edge Functions', 'Supabase, Inc.', 'Supabase Edge Functions (Deno-based serverless functions) triggered on a schedule via the same pg_cron + pg_net combination, for logic too complex for plain SQL.', 'No separate scheduling fee found on Supabase''s docs in this pass — it rides on the same pg_cron/pg_net mechanism plus Edge Functions'' own usage-based pricing (included invocations on Pro, then metered).', '["Lets scheduled logic be real TypeScript/Deno code instead of SQL, useful for anything more complex than a query or a single HTTP call", "Uses the exact same underlying pg_cron mechanism already proven at Master-Plan — no new scheduling engine to learn", "Runs inside the same Supabase project already hosting this system''s database, so no new vendor relationship", "Reasonable middle ground between ''raw SQL cron job'' and ''a whole external scheduling service''"]'::jsonb, '["Inherits pg_cron''s exact same fire-and-forget characteristic at the trigger layer — the function is invoked via the same net.http_post pattern, so the same ''succeeded'' row problem applies unless the function itself writes its own success/failure record somewhere queryable", "Supabase''s own documentation, fetched directly in this pass, does not address failure-detection or observability for scheduled functions at all — a confirmed documentation gap, not just an omission here", "Six of Master-Plan''s seven AI functions already live as hardcoded Deno edge functions with prompts baked in — the Integrator''s own agent registry was built specifically to replace that pattern, so leaning further into edge functions cuts against the project''s own stated direction", "Same soft operational limits (10-minute job ceiling, concurrency guidance) as plain pg_cron"]'::jsonb, 'For scheduled logic that''s genuinely too complex for SQL but doesn''t yet justify a whole external job-orchestration platform — a narrow middle case.', 'Weak fit specifically for this project: it would mean building more of the exact pattern (logic hardcoded in Deno functions) the Integrator''s AI Agents registry was explicitly built to retire.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://supabase.com/docs/guides/functions/schedule-functions', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "No separate scheduling fee found on Supabase''s docs — it rides on the same pg_cron/pg_net mechanism.", "caveat": "Edge Functions'' own usage-based invocation pricing beyond Pro''s included allotment is not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('job-scheduling:render-cron', 'job-scheduling', 'Render Cron Jobs', 'Render Services, Inc.', 'Render''s own Cron Jobs product — a scheduled one-off task defined in the same render.yaml/dashboard already running this project''s API, using a Docker image or git repo.', 'Prorated by the second based on actual run time, with a $1/month minimum charge per cron job service.', '["Lives in the exact same platform, account, and render.yaml already running this project''s API — zero new vendor relationship", "Guarantees at most one concurrent run of a given job, preventing the classic double-fire race condition", "$1/mo minimum makes it essentially free for a light weekly/daily job", "Runs as a real container with the full Node/TypeScript runtime already used elsewhere in this project — no Deno/SQL translation needed"]'::jsonb, '["Coarsest minimum granularity in this set: every-10-minutes at best (Render''s own docs express this as */10 in cron syntax) — fine for a weekly brief, wrong for anything needing tighter cadence", "Render''s own docs, fetched directly, do not specify any failure-notification mechanism for cron jobs specifically in this pass", "No persistent disk access and a 12-hour max runtime — fine for this project''s likely job sizes, but a real ceiling to know about", "Introduces exactly the same ''did it actually succeed'' question as pg_cron unless the job itself writes a completion record somewhere queryable — a cron job that starts is not proof it finished correctly"]'::jsonb, 'For simple, coarse-grained (hourly or slower) scheduled tasks that benefit from running as a real Node process rather than SQL or Deno, with zero new vendor to add.', 'A strong, low-friction fit precisely because it needs no new account or vendor evaluation — the main gap (failure notification) needs the same kind of explicit self-check this project would need to add to pg_cron anyway.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://render.com/docs/cronjobs', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 1, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 1, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Prorated by the second based on actual run time, with a $1/month minimum charge per cron job service (Render pricing).", "caveat": "Actual cost above the $1 minimum scales with run time and is not itemized here."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('job-scheduling:github-actions', 'job-scheduling', 'GitHub Actions scheduled workflows', 'GitHub, Inc. (Microsoft)', 'GitHub Actions'' schedule: trigger runs a workflow (any script, any language) on a cron expression, hosted on GitHub''s own runners at no extra infrastructure cost.', 'Free for public repos; private repos get free minutes on the free/Pro plan (2,000-3,000 min/mo) before metered billing — since this repo is public, scheduled workflow minutes are effectively free.', '["Genuinely free given this repo is already public", "No new vendor, database, or hosting account — it''s a YAML file already living next to render.yaml and netlify.toml in the same repo", "Can run any script (curl, node, psql) against the production API or database with secrets stored in GitHub''s own encrypted secrets store", "Well-understood, well-documented product most developers already know"]'::jsonb, '["Minimum interval is 5 minutes and GitHub is explicit this is best-effort, not exact — delays of 5-30 minutes are common at peak times per third-party monitoring guides, and sub-5-minute schedules can silently skip runs entirely", "A workflow on a repo with 60 days of no other activity gets its scheduled runs auto-disabled by GitHub, with only an email as warning — a genuinely dangerous silent-failure mode for a business-critical weekly job on a repo that might go quiet between feature pushes", "Whether GitHub notifies on a scheduled workflow''s outright failure by default (versus just not running at all) was not confirmed in this pass and needs a direct check", "Using CI infrastructure as a production job scheduler is an unconventional pattern that could hit platform limits GitHub didn''t design for this use case"]'::jsonb, 'For low-stakes, infrequent (daily/weekly) jobs where the owner already watches the repo''s Actions tab and free cost matters more than tight scheduling precision.', 'Free and requires zero new infrastructure, but the 60-day-inactivity auto-disable is a real, confirmed landmine for a repo that could plausibly go quiet for two months between coding sessions — exactly the kind of silent failure this project''s own memory already got burned by once.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://cronbuilder.dev/blog/github-actions-cron-schedule.html', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free for public repos — since bilbils/integratorApp is public, scheduled workflow minutes are effectively free.", "caveat": "Would incur metered billing beyond free minutes (2,000-3,000/mo) if the repo were private."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('job-scheduling:inngest', 'job-scheduling', 'Inngest', 'Inngest Inc.', 'Inngest is an event-driven durable-workflow platform purpose-built for background jobs and scheduled functions, with first-class observability for exactly the ''did this actually run'' question.', 'Free (Hobby): $0/mo, 50k executions/month, basic tracing/metrics/alerts, 24-hour trace history. Pro: from $99/mo, 1M executions included, granular tracing plus 7-day trace retention and 15-minute metrics granularity.', '["Built specifically to answer this project''s own stated hardest question — ''how do you find out a scheduled job silently did not run'' — with tracing, metrics and alerts as first-class product features, not an afterthought bolted onto a database extension", "Free tier (50k executions/mo) is almost certainly enough for this system''s current job volume (a weekly brief, occasional syncs) at zero cost", "Durable execution model means a job can retry, resume, and report its own failure state explicitly, rather than relying on a queue-insert being mistaken for success", "Purpose-built for ''background tasks that outlive a request'' and ''agent invokes,'' both named directly in this research prompt"]'::jsonb, '["A new vendor and a new SDK to integrate into the Express API, versus reusing the database or hosting platform already in place", "Pro tier''s $99/mo floor is a real jump if usage ever exceeds the free tier''s 50k executions — a step-function cost, not a gradual one", "Free tier''s basic alerting and only 24-hour trace history is a real limit on the failure-forensics story specifically for a job that failed weeks ago", "No FedRAMP/compliance information was surfaced in this research pass and would need a direct check for the regulated angle"]'::jsonb, 'The best-argued option for solving the exact ''silent failure'' problem this research prompt calls out by name — worth adopting specifically for that reason once job complexity grows past what a single cron-triggered SQL call handles well.', 'Strong fit for the stated pain point (Master-Plan''s fire-and-forget failure) precisely because observability is the product''s whole reason to exist, not a side feature — the free tier makes it low-risk to prove out.', 'n/a', 'growing', 'https://www.inngest.com/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "api_calls", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Hobby (free): 50,000 executions/month. Pro: from $99/month, 1M executions included (Inngest pricing).", "caveat": "Enterprise/custom tiers beyond Pro and the per-execution overage rate beyond 1M are not published in the reviewed material.", "tiers": [{"upto": 50000, "monthly": 0}, {"upto": 1000000, "monthly": 99}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('job-scheduling:trigger-dev', 'job-scheduling', 'Trigger.dev', 'Trigger.dev Inc.', 'Trigger.dev is an open-source, TypeScript-native background-jobs platform (competitor to Inngest) with a hosted cloud and a real self-host path.', 'Free: $0/mo, $5/mo of usage credits included, 20 concurrent runs. Hobby: $10/mo, $10/mo credits included, 50 concurrent runs.', '["Genuinely open source (Apache 2.0), confirmed on their own pricing page — a real self-host escape hatch if the hosted product''s terms or price ever stop making sense, unlike most vendors in this set", "Lower entry price than Inngest''s Pro tier ($10/mo vs $99/mo) for the first paid step up from free", "TypeScript-native, matching this project''s own stack exactly (the API is already Node/Express + TypeScript)", "Configurable billing alerts at multiple usage thresholds (75%-500%) are a real, product-level safeguard against surprise bills"]'::jsonb, '["This research pass''s own fetch of Trigger.dev''s pricing page could not confirm any specific feature for detecting a silently missed scheduled run (as opposed to a cost/usage-spike alert) — a real, confirmed gap in the evidence, not a settled advantage over Inngest", "Smaller and less established than Inngest in this exact market segment", "Self-hosting Trigger.dev is itself a service to operate — the same ''daemon'' cost this project explicitly wants to avoid, unless the hosted tiers are used instead", "A new vendor/SDK integration cost, same as Inngest"]'::jsonb, 'When self-hosting flexibility matters as an insurance policy, or when the lower Hobby-tier price point ($10 vs $99/mo) matters more than Inngest''s more clearly evidenced failure-alerting story.', 'A reasonable Inngest alternative on cost and stack-fit (TypeScript-native), but this pass could not confirm it solves the specific ''silent miss'' problem as clearly as Inngest''s own pricing page does.', 'n/a', 'growing', 'https://trigger.dev/pricing', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 10, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free: $0/mo, $5/mo usage credits included, 20 concurrent runs. Hobby: $10/mo, $10/mo credits included, 50 concurrent runs (Trigger.dev pricing).", "caveat": "Usage beyond included credits is billed additionally at rates not itemized in the reviewed material; higher tiers not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('job-scheduling:temporal-cloud', 'job-scheduling', 'Temporal / Temporal Cloud', 'Temporal Technologies, Inc.', 'Temporal is a durable-execution workflow engine (the industrial-strength option in this set), available as a managed Temporal Cloud or fully self-hosted open source.', 'Temporal Cloud Essentials: $100/mo, 1M actions, 1GB active storage, 40GB retained storage, though new accounts get $1,000 in free credits (roughly 10 months at that floor). Self-hosted Temporal (Service, SDKs, CLI, UI, community support) is free but you operate it yourself.', '["The most robust durable-workflow guarantees in this set — a workflow''s state (including whether each step succeeded) is always queryable, a direct architectural answer to ''how do you know a job silently didn''t run''", "Free self-hosted option exists with zero licensing cost if the operational burden were acceptable", "$1,000 in free credits effectively removes cost risk for the first ~10 months of evaluation on Temporal Cloud", "Extremely mature, widely adopted at scale by companies with far more complex workflow needs than this project has today"]'::jsonb, '["$100/mo floor after the free-credit period is the single most expensive managed option in this set — a hard number to justify against a weekly brief and a handful of syncs", "Self-hosting Temporal (the free path) requires running the Temporal Service itself — a genuine multi-component system (matching service, history service, worker, UI, its own datastore), a far bigger operational commitment than any other ''free'' option here, squarely the ''operator required even at $0 license'' cost this project''s own rules flag as expensive", "Architecturally significant overkill for the actual job list described (a weekly brief, standing syncs, agent invokes) — Temporal is built for orchestrating complex, long-running, multi-step business processes with compensating transactions, not ''run this HTTP call every Sunday''", "No FedRAMP/compliance information was found in this pass for Temporal Cloud specifically"]'::jsonb, 'If the job list ever grows into genuinely complex multi-step, multi-day workflows with compensation/rollback logic — not the current job list of a weekly brief and periodic syncs.', 'Overpowered for this project''s stated needs today — matches the general finding in this project''s own memory that ''a cluster or anything needing an operator is expensive here even at a $0 licence,'' which describes self-hosted Temporal almost exactly.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://temporal.io/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "api_calls", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Temporal Cloud Essentials: $100/month, 1M actions, 1GB active storage, 40GB retained storage (new accounts get $1,000 in free credits, roughly 10 months at that floor).", "caveat": "Self-hosted Temporal (free OSS, operate yourself) not modeled as an alternative; overage rates beyond 1M actions are not published in the reviewed material.", "tiers": [{"upto": 1000000, "monthly": 100}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('job-scheduling:bullmq-redis', 'job-scheduling', 'BullMQ + Redis', 'n/a — open-source library (Taskforce.sh) + a Redis host of your choice', 'BullMQ is a free, open-source Node.js job-queue library backed by Redis — you write the worker code and run it inside your own process or a separate one, using Redis purely as the queue''s storage.', 'BullMQ itself is free and open source (MIT-licensed npm package). The real cost is hosting Redis: a small managed Redis instance typically runs $0-$15/mo for a workload this small; self-hosted Redis on an existing VPS is free.', '["Zero framework lock-in cost beyond a Redis instance — it''s a library, not a hosted platform, so there''s no vendor SLA or ToS to depend on for the scheduling logic itself", "TypeScript-native and designed for exactly this project''s stack (Node/Express), with delayed jobs, repeatable (cron-like) jobs, and retries built in", "Genuinely mature and widely used in production Node applications well beyond this project''s scale", "A small managed Redis tier can be cheaper than any hosted job-scheduling SaaS in this set"]'::jsonb, '["This is now an ops burden the project didn''t have before: a new stateful service (Redis) to provision, secure, and keep running, plus a worker process that must itself stay alive — closer to ''build your own Inngest'' than a turnkey answer to the silent-failure problem", "Observability (knowing a job silently failed) is entirely DIY — BullMQ gives you the primitives (job events, failed-job listeners) but building actual alerting is custom code, not a product feature the way it is with Inngest or Temporal", "Redis itself becomes a new single point of failure and a new thing that can silently misbehave, ironically adding a new instance of the exact ''is it actually working'' question this research set is trying to solve", "No compliance certifications apply since this is a library plus whatever Redis host is chosen — compliance now depends entirely on that separate choice"]'::jsonb, 'When there''s already a reason to run Redis (caching, rate-limiting) and the team wants full code-level control over job behavior without paying a SaaS platform fee.', 'A capable, honest DIY option, but it adds a new stateful dependency (Redis) and pushes the entire ''did it actually run'' observability problem back onto the owner to build by hand — the opposite direction from what this research set is trying to solve.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://docs.bullmq.io/', '{"kind": "byo", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 7.5, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "BullMQ itself is free and open source (MIT-licensed npm package); the real cost is hosting Redis, typically $0-15/mo for a small managed instance, or free if self-hosted on an existing VPS; midpoint used.", "caveat": "Assumes a small managed Redis instance; self-hosting on an already-running VPS could bring this to $0."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 80),
  ('job-scheduling:hatchet', 'job-scheduling', 'Hatchet', 'Hatchet Technologies, Inc.', 'Hatchet is a newer, open-source task-orchestration platform (Postgres-backed under the hood) positioned between BullMQ''s DIY model and Temporal''s heavyweight one.', 'Developer (free): first 100,000 task runs included, then $10 per additional 1M runs. Team: $500/mo + usage. Enterprise: bring-your-own-cloud / self-host support available.', '["Free tier (100k runs included) is generous enough to prove the pattern at zero cost for this project''s current job volume", "Postgres-backed engine is philosophically consistent with this project''s own ''plain SQL and standard Postgres'' preference, more so than a Redis-backed queue", "Self-host support exists at the Enterprise tier, giving a durability escape hatch similar to Trigger.dev''s open-source path", "Positioned specifically as durable task orchestration, implying built-in run-status tracking rather than fire-and-forget"]'::jsonb, '["The jump from free to the next real tier is a steep $500/mo — no visible middle ground for ''a bit more than the free tier'' the way Inngest''s $99/mo Pro or Trigger.dev''s $10/mo Hobby provide", "This research pass could not confirm specific failure/silent-miss detection features from Hatchet''s own pricing page — the claim of built-in run-status tracking is inferred from its positioning, not confirmed from documentation read in this session", "Newer and less proven at scale than Inngest, Trigger.dev, or Temporal — real vendor-durability risk", "Self-host support is gated to Enterprise, so the free/Developer tier offers no self-host insurance policy the way Trigger.dev''s fully open-source model does"]'::jsonb, 'Worth a free-tier trial specifically because of its Postgres-backed model matching this project''s own stack preference, but budget past the free tier only after confirming the $500/mo Team-tier features are actually needed.', 'An interesting philosophical fit (Postgres-backed, matches the 