  ('job-scheduling:setinterval-in-process', 'job-scheduling', 'setInterval inside the API process', 'n/a — no vendor, Node.js built-in', 'Skip a scheduler entirely: keep a setInterval (or a lightweight node-cron package) running inside the same long-lived Express process already deployed on Render, firing the weekly brief or sync logic directly.', 'Free — no new service, vendor, or library beyond what''s already running (or, at most, the free node-cron npm package).', '["Absolute zero new infrastructure, vendor, or monthly cost — the process is already running 24/7 on Render''s Starter tier for the API itself", "No new integration surface, SDK, or account to manage — it''s a few lines of code in the same TypeScript codebase already there", "For a single-instance deployment (which this project is, and Render''s Starter tier is exactly one instance), there''s no double-fire risk from multiple instances all trying to run the same job", "Full control over exactly when and how the job runs, with the same logging/error-handling conventions already used throughout the API"]'::jsonb, '["Honestly argued: this is the single most fragile option in the set for exactly the failure mode this research prompt names — if the API process restarts (a deploy, a crash, Render recycling the container), the in-memory interval is gone and silently never fires again until the next deploy or restart, with zero record that it was ever supposed to run", "No persistent job-run log exists unless explicitly built — there is no cron.job_run_details-equivalent table anywhere in this pattern by default", "If Render ever needs to scale this service to more than one instance for any reason, every instance would independently fire the same interval, running the job multiple times with no built-in mutual exclusion", "This pattern has no observability story at all beyond whatever the owner manually adds — it inherits every weakness of pg_cron''s fire-and-forget problem while adding a new one (surviving restarts) that pg_cron doesn''t have, since pg_cron''s schedule lives in the database, not the process''s memory"]'::jsonb, 'Only for genuinely disposable, non-critical timers where a missed run has zero business consequence — never for the weekly brief or a standing sync that the owner actually depends on.', 'The worst-argued option in this set for anything that matters: it fails silently on every restart, which is functionally the same lesson Master-Plan''s pg_net failure already taught this project, except worse because there is no queryable trace of the job ever having been scheduled at all. (This argument rests on Render''s own confirmed single-instance Starter tier model.)', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://render.com/pricing', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free — no new service, vendor, or library beyond what''s already running (or, at most, the free node-cron npm package).", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 100),
  ('admin-auth:bcrypt-jwt', 'admin-auth', 'Keep bcrypt + JWT (home-grown)', 'n/a — in-house', 'The current admin_users table (email + bcrypt hash) exchanged for a signed JWT on login, checked by requireAdmin on every protected route.', '$0 — no vendor, runs entirely in api/src on the already-provisioned Postgres and Render service.', '["Already built, already the thing every other route (requireAdmin, requireReader) checks against — a swap touches every protected endpoint at once.", "No new vendor holds admin credentials or session data — matters for the same reason a second AI vendor matters: fewer places a secret can leak from.", "Deliberately designed to port to Entra later (stated in CLAUDE.md), so today''s simplicity is not accidental technical debt, it''s a sequencing choice.", "Zero recurring cost at any user count, which matters at exactly 2 admins today and probably fewer than 10 for a long while."]'::jsonb, '["OWASP no longer recommends bcrypt as a first choice for new systems — current guidance prefers Argon2id (or scrypt), with bcrypt only ''for legacy systems where Argon2id and scrypt are not available'' (verified from OWASP''s Password Storage Cheat Sheet, Aug 2026) — worth a note, not a fire drill, since bcrypt with a proper work factor is still considered acceptable, just not best-practice-first-choice.", "No SSO, no MFA, no session revocation UI, no audit log of who signed in when — anything beyond ''does this password match'' is unbuilt.", "JWTs as currently likely implemented (no evidence of a revocation list) can''t be forcibly invalidated mid-lifetime without extra machinery.", "Every new admin (Josh, eventually Staffility contacts) is a manually created row and password, not an invite-and-SSO flow."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick as long as the admin list stays small, trusted, and manually managed, and the near-term plan is genuinely ''move to Entra later,'' not ''never revisit this.''', 'Correct for today''s 2-user reality; the open question is timing, not whether this was the wrong call originally.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "No vendor — runs entirely in api/src on the already-provisioned Postgres and Render service.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('admin-auth:entra-id', 'admin-auth', 'Microsoft Entra ID', 'Microsoft', 'Microsoft''s workforce identity platform — the stated future target in CLAUDE.md — providing SSO, MFA, and conditional access for internal admin users.', 'Entra ID Free is included with any Azure/Microsoft 365/Dynamics 365 subscription. P1 (SSO, conditional access, self-service groups): $7/user/month paid yearly, also bundled into Microsoft 365 Business Premium. P2 (adds Identity Protection, PIM): $10/user/month paid yearly, bundled into Microsoft 365 E5. (Verified microsoft.com/en-us/security/business/microsoft-entra-pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["This is the destination CLAUDE.md already names — adopting it now instead of later avoids a second migration.", "If LFODIE already has any Microsoft 365 subscription, Entra ID Free may already be in hand at $0 incremental cost for basic SSO.", "P1 at $7/user/month for 2-3 admins is a trivial recurring cost ($14-21/mo) for real MFA, conditional access, and centralized offboarding.", "Matches how a regulated client (IPTA) is likely already managing its own workforce identity, easing any future story about who can access what."]'::jsonb, '["JWT-based auth in api/src has to be replaced with an OIDC flow against Entra — a real code change across every protected route, not a config toggle.", "Annual-commitment pricing (paid yearly) is a small but real lock-in for a 2-person shop used to month-to-month tools.", "Entra is workforce-identity-first; if Staffility or other external consumer-app admins ever need logins, that likely means Entra External ID (a separate product/pricing line) layered on top.", "Overkill in raw capability for 2 users today — the payoff is in the migration timing, not in solving a problem the current system actually has yet."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick at the moment a second or third admin (Josh, then others) needs real offboarding/MFA discipline, or when IPTA''s compliance story needs centralized identity governance.', 'The correct long-term target per CLAUDE.md''s own stated direction — the question is only whether now or later is the right time to pay the migration cost.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/business/microsoft-entra-pricing', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "admin_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 7, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": true, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Entra ID P1 (SSO, conditional access, self-service groups): $7/user/month paid yearly (microsoft.com/en-us/security/business/microsoft-entra-pricing, Aug 2026); Free tier is included with any existing Azure/M365/Dynamics 365 subscription.", "caveat": "P2 ($10/user/mo, adds Identity Protection/PIM) not modeled; assumes yearly-commitment billing."}'::jsonb, 'chosen', 'Josh 2026-08-05: staff sign in with Entra; candidates get email+password in Staffility''s own DB. Entra is asked once, at login, then it is a session row.', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('admin-auth:workos', 'admin-auth', 'WorkOS', 'WorkOS, Inc.', 'A developer-first auth platform (AuthKit for user management, plus SSO/Directory Sync as separate add-ons) aimed at B2B apps that need to support each customer''s own SSO later.', 'AuthKit (user management): free up to 1 million monthly active users. SSO connections: tiered per-connection pricing, $125/connection/month for the first 1-15, discounting to $65/connection at 51-100. No per-seat admin cost. (Verified workos.com/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["Effectively free for the admin-login use case itself (1M MAU free) — the cost only appears if/when an external consumer app wants its own SSO connection.", "No per-seat pricing for admins — a 2-10 person admin team costs nothing beyond whatever SSO connections are actually turned on.", "Purpose-built for exactly the shape this system may grow into: LFODIE''s own admins plus, eventually, each consumer company (Staffility, IPTA) bringing its own SSO.", "Migration off bcrypt+JWT is a well-trodden path — WorkOS''s whole product is designed to bolt onto an existing app''s user table."]'::jsonb, '["Each individual SSO connection (e.g., one per client company) is $125/month before volume discounts — real money the moment a client wants Entra/Okta-based SSO into a shared surface.", "A new vendor holding session/login data, another entry on the ''what would have to be rotated if this repo leaked'' checklist.", "Not the destination CLAUDE.md already committed to (that''s Entra), so choosing WorkOS now risks a second future migration instead of one.", "Best-fit for external customer-facing SSO, a problem this system doesn''t have yet — its own admins are all internal LFODIE staff."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick if and when a consumer app (Staffility, or a client of IPTA) needs to bring its own corporate SSO into a shared portal, not for the LFODIE-internal admin login itself.', 'A strong option for a future ''consumer apps bring their own identity'' problem, but not the one this system has today, and it competes with the already-decided Entra direction.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://workos.com/pricing', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "admin_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 1000000, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "AuthKit user management is free up to 1,000,000 monthly active users (workos.com/pricing) — an internal admin panel is unlikely to exceed that, so cost is effectively $0.", "caveat": "SSO connections are priced separately ($125/connection/mo, discounting to $65 at 51-100) if per-customer SSO is later needed; not modeled here."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('admin-auth:auth0', 'admin-auth', 'Auth0', 'Okta, Inc.', 'A long-established hosted identity platform (now owned by Okta) covering both consumer and workforce auth, with a broad, well-worn free tier.', 'Free: up to 25,000 MAU, includes passwordless auth, unlimited social connections, and now 1 free enterprise connection plus self-service SSO. Paid Essentials: $35/mo (B2C, 500 MAU) or $150/mo (B2B, 500 MAU). Additional enterprise connections beyond the free one: $100/month each on paid plans. (Verified auth0.com/pricing.md, Aug 2026.)', '["25,000 MAU free tier is far beyond this system''s current 2 admins, so genuinely $0 for the immediate use case.", "Recently added a free enterprise connection and self-service SSO to the free tier, which used to be paid-only — a real, recent improvement worth knowing about.", "Very mature product with extensive documentation, SDKs, and a long track record — low platform risk.", "Owned by Okta since 2021, so enterprise-grade security operations sit behind it even on cheaper tiers."]'::jsonb, '["Now an Okta product, not an independent company — pricing and roadmap priorities are set by Okta''s broader strategy, worth tracking for drift.", "Same migration cost as any hosted IdP: bcrypt+JWT has to be replaced with Auth0''s SDK and session model across every protected route.", "Not the destination CLAUDE.md already committed to (Entra); adopting it risks a second future migration.", "Beyond the single free enterprise connection, each additional one is $100/month — costs scale the same way WorkOS''s SSO pricing does, once past that first connection."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick for a team that wants an extremely mature, well-documented IdP now and either doesn''t care about the eventual Entra target or plans to stay generic longer.', 'Technically capable and cheap at this scale, but competes directly with the already-decided Entra direction rather than extending it.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://auth0.com/pricing.md', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "admin_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 25000, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free tier covers up to 25,000 MAU including passwordless auth, unlimited social connections, and one free enterprise connection (auth0.com/pricing.md) — well above admin-user scale.", "caveat": "Paid Essentials ($35/mo B2C or $150/mo B2B) and additional enterprise connections ($100/mo each) apply only if enterprise SSO or the MAU cap is exceeded; not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('admin-auth:clerk', 'admin-auth', 'Clerk', 'Clerk, Inc.', 'A developer-focused auth platform built for fast integration into modern web apps (React/Next.js-friendly), with prebuilt UI components for sign-in/sign-up.', 'Free: 50,000 monthly retained users, up to 3 dashboard seats. Pro: $20-25/mo, 1 enterprise SSO connection included, additional connections $75/mo each. Business: $250-300/mo, 10 dashboard seats. Enterprise: custom, adds HIPAA BAA and 99.99% SLA. (Verified clerk.com/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["50,000-user free tier comfortably covers 2 admins at $0.", "Prebuilt sign-in UI components would save real frontend work versus hand-rolling the Angular login screen further.", "Enterprise SSO connections are cheaper here ($75/mo) than WorkOS''s ($125/mo) at the low end.", "Clear, simple tier ladder with no annual-commitment requirement at the entry tiers, unlike Entra or Okta."]'::jsonb, '["Strongest fit is React/Next.js apps; this admin is Angular, so the prebuilt-UI advantage mostly doesn''t apply here.", "Same bcrypt+JWT replacement cost as every other hosted IdP option.", "Not the CLAUDE.md-stated Entra direction — another candidate for a second migration later.", "Add-ons (B2B auth, admin features) are priced separately at $85-100/mo each, so a fuller feature set costs more than the headline Pro price suggests."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick for a team building a new React/Next.js-based admin from scratch that wants fast, polished auth UI out of the box.', 'Weak fit specifically because the mismatch with Angular erases its main advantage, and it still competes with the Entra direction.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://clerk.com/pricing', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "admin_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 50000, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free tier covers up to 50,000 monthly retained users and 3 dashboard seats (clerk.com/pricing) — sufficient for internal admin auth.", "caveat": "Pro ($20-25/mo, 1 SSO connection) and Business ($250-300/mo, 10 seats) tiers, plus extra SSO connections ($75/mo each), not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('admin-auth:supabase-auth', 'admin-auth', 'Supabase Auth', 'Supabase, Inc.', 'Supabase''s own built-in auth product, already sitting on the same database this system already uses — but explicitly not adopted here on purpose.', 'Included in the existing Supabase project at no extra platform fee beyond the current Postgres usage (free tier: 500 MB DB; Pro: $25/mo base, 8 GB included). (Verified supabase.com/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["Zero new vendor — it''s the database already in use, so there''s no additional account, contract, or data-processing relationship to add.", "Would be the least-code path technically, since the Postgres connection already exists.", "Free at the current usage scale, same as the incumbent.", "Confirmed during the 2026-08-22 workbench session to have zero users today, so there''s no existing Supabase Auth investment being wasted by not choosing it."]'::jsonb, '["CLAUDE.md is explicit and deliberate that auth is NOT Supabase Auth, specifically so it can port to Entra later — adopting it now directly contradicts a recorded architectural decision.", "Ties admin identity to the same database that RLS-with-no-policies is protecting from the anon key; conflating the identity store with the data store cuts against the ''control plane, not a database'' framing.", "No path to Entra from Supabase Auth was researched or found to be materially easier than from bcrypt+JWT — likely trades one bespoke migration for a different bespoke migration.", "Not verified to include enterprise SSO on affordable tiers the way WorkOS or Auth0 do, if that''s ever needed for a client."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick only if the Entra-later plan is abandoned and the goal becomes ''consolidate everything onto Supabase'' instead.', 'Explicitly rejected already, in writing, for a specific and still-valid reason — including it here is to show the reasoning survives scrutiny, not to reopen it.', 'pooled', 'mature', 'https://supabase.com/pricing', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Included in the existing Supabase project''s Auth service at no incremental platform fee beyond current Postgres usage (supabase.com/pricing).", "caveat": "Assumes the underlying Supabase project already exists — its own cost is tracked under postgres-host, not duplicated here."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('admin-auth:keycloak-authentik', 'admin-auth', 'Keycloak or Authentik (self-hosted)', 'Red Hat community (Keycloak) / Authentik Security', 'Open-source, self-hosted identity providers (Keycloak is the older, Java-based, feature-complete option; Authentik is the newer, Python-based, often cited as easier to operate) covering SSO, MFA, and user federation.', 'Both free and open-source software with no license fee at any scale; cost is entirely the compute to run them (a small VM or container) plus the time to operate them.', '["Zero license cost at any user count, and full control over where identity data physically lives — relevant to the same residency logic as the DFARS/CUI angle.", "Mature protocol support (SAML, OIDC, LDAP) suitable for eventually federating with a client''s own identity system.", "No vendor to go out of business or reprice out from under the project.", "Authentik specifically is commonly described as lower operational overhead than Keycloak for a small team, per current comparison write-ups."]'::jsonb, '["Both are a real running service to patch, back up, and monitor — precisely the daemon/operator burden this shop is structured to avoid, worse than any hosted option on this list.", "No convenient free tier of someone else''s SLA; an outage here is entirely LFODIE''s problem to fix, unlike a hosted IdP''s status page and support line.", "No commercial support contract by default (Keycloak has a Red Hat SSO commercial variant, but not for a project this size), so there''s no one to call when it breaks.", "Nothing about this path leads toward Entra; it is a third distinct direction, not a stepping stone to the stated target."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick for a team with genuine data-residency requirements (e.g., a CUI-adjacent deployment) and either existing ops capacity or a strong reason to avoid every hosted IdP.', 'Wrong fit here specifically because of the ''operational burden is a first-class cost'' rule — this is the most operator-heavy option on the list for a shop of one.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://www.cerbos.dev/blog/authentik-vs-keycloak-selfhosted-idp-comparison', '{"kind": "byo", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 7, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Both Keycloak and Authentik are free and open-source with no license fee at any scale.", "caveat": "base_monthly is a placeholder small-VM hosting cost; operator time to run and secure the IdP is not priced."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('admin-auth:stytch', 'admin-auth', 'Stytch', 'Stytch, Inc.', 'A developer-first auth platform emphasizing passwordless (magic link, passkeys, OTP) and B2B org/SSO management, with consumption-based pricing.', 'Free: 10,000 monthly active users/agents, unlimited organizations, 5 SSO or SCIM connections included. Beyond that: consumption-based per-MAU and per-connection charges; additional SSO/SCIM connections $125 each. Enterprise: custom, discounted rates and SLA. (Verified stytch.com/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["5 free SSO/SCIM connections included before any per-connection charge — more generous than WorkOS''s zero-free-connections model.", "10,000 free MAU comfortably covers 2 admins and considerable headroom beyond.", "Strong passwordless/passkey support, which sidesteps the OWASP bcrypt-vs-Argon2id question entirely by not storing passwords the same way.", "Consumption-based pricing with no seat minimum matches the small-shop cost profile well."]'::jsonb, '["Smaller, less-established name than Auth0/Okta/Microsoft in the identity space — more platform risk for a decision meant to last years.", "Same bcrypt+JWT-replacement migration cost as any other hosted IdP.", "Not the CLAUDE.md-stated Entra direction — a third candidate that would still need a second migration later.", "Its stated focus increasingly includes ''AI agents'' as a MAU category, suggesting a product still actively repositioning rather than a settled roadmap."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick for a team that wants modern passwordless auth (magic links, passkeys) and is comfortable with a less-established vendor.', 'Technically fine at this scale but adds a third possible direction alongside Entra and WorkOS with no clear advantage over either for this system''s stated future.', 'n/a', 'growing', 'https://stytch.com/pricing', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "admin_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 10000, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free tier covers up to 10,000 monthly active users/agents, unlimited organizations, and 5 SSO or SCIM connections included (stytch.com/pricing) — comfortably above admin-user scale.", "caveat": "Consumption-based per-MAU/per-connection charges beyond the free tier, and additional SSO/SCIM connections at $125 each, not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('tenant-credentials:app-side-envelope', 'tenant-credentials', 'App-side envelope encryption (libsodium / node crypto)', 'n/a - in-house, one master key in Render''s env store', 'Every tenant credential is encrypted in the app with a per-tenant data key before the INSERT, and only the one master key that wraps those data keys lives outside Postgres, in Render''s env store.', 'not published - no vendor, no per-call fee; the only cost is the engineering time to write and test the wrap/unwrap functions, already zero-cost tools (Node''s built-in crypto or libsodium via the sodium-native/libsodium-wrappers packages).', '["No new host, no new bill, no new uptime dependency beyond Postgres and Render, which the system already depends on.", "Per-tenant data-key isolation is trivial: one row, one random key, wrapped by the master key - exactly the shape connections/connection_state already wants.", "Rotation of the master key is a background job (re-wrap every data key), not a redeploy, and rotating one tenant''s key touches only that tenant''s row.", "Matches the project''s own secret tiers: repo=publishable, DB-with-authz=operational, env/vault=secret - the master key is the only thing promoted to ''secret''."]'::jsonb, '["The master key sits in Render''s env store, which the project has already flagged as write-only - there is no independent audit of what value is actually loaded at runtime.", "All the correctness burden (nonce handling, AEAD, key-wrapping) is on this app''s code and its tests, not a vendor''s.", "A leaked master key decrypts every tenant''s credentials at once - there is no per-tenant blast-radius limit unless data keys are also rotated independently.", "Does nothing for DFARS Security Protection Data if a regulated tenant''s credentials ever land here - the encryption is real but the host (Render) is still not FedRAMP-anything."]'::jsonb, 'The right default for every non-regulated tenant (Master-Plan, Staffility, Blue Orbit) for as long as this stays a one-person shop - it is the only option with zero new operational surface.', 'This is the current sketch in the card and it is the correct starting point for migration 005; the open design work is the data-key-per-tenant table and the re-wrap-on-rotation job, not a vendor integration.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://nodejs.org/api/crypto.html', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "No vendor, no per-call fee; the only cost is engineering time to write/test wrap-unwrap functions using zero-cost tools (Node''s built-in crypto or libsodium).", "caveat": "Engineering time is not priced; the one master key still needs secure storage in Render''s env store at no additional cost."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('tenant-credentials:aws-kms', 'tenant-credentials', 'AWS KMS (envelope encryption, key held remotely)', 'Amazon Web Services', 'AWS holds the master key in a managed HSM-backed service; the app calls KMS to encrypt/decrypt the small per-tenant data keys instead of holding the master key itself.', '$1/month per customer master key (CMK), prorated hourly; $0.03 per 10,000 standard API requests, $0.15 per 10,000 for asymmetric operations; 20,000 free requests/month account-wide.', '["Master key never leaves AWS''s HSM boundary - not even the app process holds it, unlike the in-house sketch.", "Built-in automatic annual rotation for symmetric CMKs, or manual rotation on demand, with old key material retained for existing ciphertext.", "CloudTrail logs every Decrypt call, giving an audit trail the in-house sketch does not have for free.", "Per-tenant isolation is still just ''one data key per tenant'', same shape as the in-house design - KMS only replaces where the wrapping key lives."]'::jsonb, '["Introduces a second cloud account and a second bill for a one-person shop that has deliberately kept everything to Render+Supabase+Netlify so far.", "If AWS KMS has an outage or the app''s AWS credentials expire, every decrypt call fails - the app cannot serve a single tenant''s connector until KMS answers again.", "Requires provisioning an IAM role/user, its own credential, and its own rotation - a new secret to manage in order to protect other secrets.", "Standard AWS KMS is not FedRAMP High and is not GovCloud by default - CUI-adjacent use needs AWS GovCloud KMS specifically, a separate account and higher cost."]'::jsonb, 'Worth adopting only once there are enough non-regulated tenants that independent key-rotation audit trail and HSM-backed custody are worth a second cloud bill and account.', 'Drop-in replacement for the master-key half of the app-side design - the data-key-per-connection table doesn''t change, only the ''who unwraps the master key'' call becomes a network hop to AWS.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://aws.amazon.com/kms/pricing/', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "api_calls", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 3e-06, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "$1/month per customer master key (CMK), prorated hourly; 20,000 free API requests/month account-wide, then $0.03 per 10,000 standard requests ($0.15/10,000 for asymmetric operations).", "caveat": "Assumes one CMK, matching the app-side-envelope architecture''s single master key; the higher asymmetric-operation rate is not used as the default.", "tiers": [{"upto": 20000, "monthly": 1}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('tenant-credentials:azure-key-vault', 'tenant-credentials', 'Azure Key Vault (Standard/Premium) + Managed HSM', 'Microsoft Azure', 'Azure''s secret/key vault service, billed per 10,000 transactions for Standard, with HSM-backed keys and a dedicated Managed HSM pool available on Premium.', 'Standard tier: per-10,000-transactions pricing (exact current rate needs the Azure calculator - the public pricing page ships blanked ''$-'' placeholders); Managed HSM adds an hourly per-pool fee on top. Not fully published without a calculator run or sales quote.', '["Natural fit if the project ever moves toward Azure per the original ''production target = Azure'' note still in this file''s stack section.", "Managed HSM gives FIPS 140-2 Level 3 dedicated HSM pools when a regulated tenant eventually needs that assurance.", "Premium tier''s HSM-backed keys give per-key isolation with hardware-backed custody, not just a wrapped blob in Postgres.", "Azure AD-integrated access policies give fine-grained per-identity access control beyond a single service-role secret."]'::jsonb, '["The public pricing page itself would not resolve to concrete dollar figures - actual cost requires the calculator or a sales conversation, an odd trait for a ''simple, predictable'' billing claim.", "Managed HSM''s per-pool hourly fee is a fixed cost regardless of tenant count - expensive overkill at today''s single-digit tenant count.", "Adds a third cloud vendor relationship (after Render/Netlify and Supabase) for a shop that has none today.", "Azure Key Vault''s outage or an expired app registration blocks every decrypt the same way KMS does - a new single point of failure."]'::jsonb, 'Makes sense only if/when the stack actually migrates to Azure, matching the ''production target = Azure'' aspiration already on record - not worth adopting in isolation.', 'Same shape as AWS KMS for migration 005 - replaces the master-key custody layer only; not worth the account-setup cost unless Azure becomes the deploy target.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/pricing/details/key-vault/', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Standard tier is priced per 10,000 transactions, but the public pricing page ships blanked ''$-'' placeholders requiring the Azure calculator; Managed HSM adds a separate hourly per-pool fee, also unpublished here.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'chosen', 'Josh 2026-08-05: two Key Vaults per environment, deliberately split so the migration runner and the API cannot read each other''s secrets.', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('tenant-credentials:gcp-kms', 'tenant-credentials', 'Google Cloud KMS', 'Google Cloud', 'Google''s key management service; each active key version is billed monthly and each cryptographic operation is billed per-10,000-calls, with optional HSM- or externally-backed protection levels.', '$0.06/month per active SOFTWARE key version, $1.00/month per HSM key version, $3.00/month per EXTERNAL key version; $0.03 per 10,000 operations (+$0.15/10,000 extra for HSM); 100 free Autokey versions and 10,000 free operations/month.', '["Cheapest of the three hyperscaler KMS options at this system''s tiny scale - software-protection-level keys cost pennies per month.", "Clean per-tenant isolation: one key version per tenant costs $0.06/month regardless of how many tenants exist.", "Key rotation is always free and can be automated on a schedule.", "External/EKM protection level exists for organizations that need the key material to never leave their own HSM, a future option if a client demands it."]'::jsonb, '["Adds a fourth cloud vendor to a stack that today is only Render + Netlify + Supabase - one more account, one more IAM surface, one more bill to reconcile.", "Same remote-dependency risk as AWS/Azure: a GCP outage or expired service-account key stops every decrypt call app-wide.", "HSM protection level jumps to $1/month per key and $0.15/10k ops - a step-function cost increase the moment a tenant needs hardware-backed keys.", "Google Cloud KMS by itself is not FedRAMP High/DoD IL4-5 - that requires Assured Workloads wrapped around it, a separate, non-trivial commitment."]'::jsonb, 'Best pick among the three hyperscaler KMS services purely on unit cost, if the team ever decides remote HSM custody is worth a new cloud account.', 'Functionally identical fit to AWS KMS for migration 005 - only the master-key custody changes; adopting it means opening a GCP billing account for one purpose.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://cloud.google.com/kms/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "api_calls", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 3e-06, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "$0.06/month per active SOFTWARE key version; $0.03 per 10,000 operations; 10,000 free operations/month (Google Cloud KMS pricing).", "caveat": "HSM ($1.00/mo/version, +$0.15/10,000 ops) and EXTERNAL ($3.00/mo/version) protection levels not modeled; 100 free Autokey versions not modeled.", "tiers": [{"upto": 10000, "monthly": 0.06}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('tenant-credentials:hashicorp-vault', 'tenant-credentials', 'HashiCorp Vault (self-hosted OSS or HCP Vault Dedicated/Secrets)', 'HashiCorp (IBM)', 'A dedicated secrets-management server with dynamic secrets, leasing, and its own access-policy language; can be run self-hosted for free or bought as HashiCorp''s managed HCP Vault.', 'Community/OSS self-hosted: free (MPL 2.0 -> Business Source License for newer releases), but requires running and operating a server. HCP Vault: pay-as-you-go usage billing with a $500 starting credit, or Flex/Enterprise annual commitments - exact per-month figures are gated behind IBM''s PAYG pricing table, not published as a flat number.', '["Purpose-built secrets engine with dynamic/short-lived credentials, not just static encrypt/decrypt - a real upgrade path if connections ever need short-lived tokens instead of long-lived API keys.", "Fine-grained policy language (paths, capabilities) beyond a single service-role check.", "HCP Vault Dedicated removes the self-host operational burden in exchange for a subscription.", "Wide ecosystem/plugin support (dynamic DB creds, PKI, transit encryption-as-a-service) if the connection layer grows beyond simple credential storage."]'::jsonb, '["Self-hosted OSS means running, patching, and backing up a stateful server - exactly the ''operator burden'' this system is trying to avoid as a one-person shop.", "HashiCorp relicensed core Vault off pure open-source (BSL) in 2023, which is why OpenBao exists - a self-hosted OSS choice today inherits that licensing uncertainty.", "HCP Vault''s actual price is not a public flat number; it requires the calculator or a sales quote, making budget forecasting harder than the hyperscaler KMS options.", "A Vault outage (self-hosted, and it is one more service to keep alive) blocks every decrypt the same way a hyperscaler KMS outage would, but now the operator on call for that outage is the one-person team."]'::jsonb, 'Only worth it if migration 005 grows into needing dynamic/short-lived per-tenant credentials (not just encrypted-at-rest static tokens) - a real capability jump, not just a storage swap.', 'Overkill for what migration 005 needs today (encrypt a static OAuth token/API key at rest); revisit only if per-tenant credentials need automatic expiry/rotation as a first-class feature.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://www.hashicorp.com/en/products/vault/pricing', '{"kind": "byo", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 10, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Community/OSS self-hosted edition is free (MPL 2.0, moving to Business Source License for newer releases), but requires running and operating a server.", "caveat": "HCP Vault''s actual PAYG/Flex/Enterprise pricing is gated behind IBM''s pricing table and not used ($500 starting credit only); base_monthly is a placeholder self-host VM estimate; operator time not priced."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('tenant-credentials:supabase-vault', 'tenant-credentials', 'Supabase Vault', 'Supabase (already the dev-hosted Postgres)', 'A Postgres extension, already available in the same Supabase project the Integrator uses today, that stores secrets AEAD-encrypted at rest via libsodium, decryptable through a `vault.decrypted_secrets` view.', 'Included at no extra charge on any Supabase plan that already hosts the project''s Postgres - no new billing line.', '["Zero new infrastructure - it is a feature of the database this system already runs on.", "Encryption key is stored by Supabase separately from the database, so a raw pg_dump of the database does not expose secrets on its own.", "AEAD (libsodium) is the same cryptographic primitive the in-house sketch would use, just managed by Supabase instead of by the app.", "Free, so there is no cost argument against trying it for non-regulated tenants."]'::jsonb, '["Confirmed directly from Supabase''s own docs: it does NOT protect against a service-role read - ''anyone that has access to the view has access to decrypted secrets,'' and the API''s pooled Postgres role is exactly that kind of access.", "This is precisely the failure mode Bill''s own secret tiers already rule out: ''a table of secrets is not a secret store - anything with the service role reads it.'' Vault is still a table (with a friendlier encryption wrapper), reachable by the same service role.", "No independent audit log of who called `vault.decrypted_secrets` and when, beyond whatever Postgres/Supabase logging already exists.", "Ties credential custody to Supabase specifically - if the project ever needs to move off Supabase (the card notes ''the host is a connection string, not an architecture''), Vault''s secret material has to migrate too."]'::jsonb, 'Not a fit for migration 005 as the actual secret store; at most useful for encrypting something less sensitive than tenant OAuth tokens, where at-rest encryption against a stolen disk/backup (not against the app''s own role) is the goal.', 'Explicitly ruled out for the connection layer''s real secret tier - it is operational-tier protection (encrypted at rest, readable by an authorized role) not secret-tier, and migration 005''s per-tenant credentials need secret-tier.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/vault', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Included at no extra charge on any Supabase plan that already hosts the project''s Postgres (a Postgres extension, AEAD-encrypted at rest via libsodium).", "caveat": "Assumes the Supabase project already exists; no incremental cost beyond what''s already paid for hosting."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('tenant-credentials:infisical', 'tenant-credentials', 'Infisical (self-host or cloud)', 'Infisical, Inc.', 'An open-source secrets manager (env-var style secrets, dynamic secrets, PKI) with a free self-hosted tier and a paid cloud SaaS tier.', 'Free tier: 5 identities, unlimited projects, 3 environments. Pro: $20/identity/month (billed annually) or $23/month. Advanced: $40/identity/month (annually) or $46/month, adds dynamic secrets, SOC 2 Type II. Enterprise: custom, adds external KMS/HSM support.', '["Free self-hosted tier removes the per-identity cost entirely if the one-person team is willing to run the container.", "Cloud tier removes operator burden the way HCP Vault does, at a lower headline per-identity price.", "Dynamic secrets and secret rotation are built-in on the Pro/Advanced tiers - a capability jump over static envelope encryption.", "Open-source core gives an exit path if the vendor relationship ends, unlike a pure SaaS-only option."]'::jsonb, '["Self-hosting reintroduces the same operator burden (patching, backup, uptime) this shop is trying to avoid; cloud tier reintroduces per-identity billing that scales with tenant/service-account count, not with data volume.", "''Identity'' pricing is ambiguous for a system where the caller is usually a service account (the API server), not a human - could mean paying per connector or per environment rather than per person.", "Newer/smaller vendor than the hyperscalers or HashiCorp - less operating history if the master relationship needs to be trusted for years.", "Adds a fifth external account relationship for a stack that already has Render, Netlify, Supabase, and OpenRouter."]'::jsonb, 'A reasonable middle ground if the team wants dynamic secrets and rotation without hyperscaler account sprawl, and is comfortable self-hosting one more small container.', 'Would replace the whole master-key/data-key design in migration 005 with an external secrets store the API calls at request time - a bigger architectural change than swapping just the master-key custody.', 'n/a', 'growing', 'https://infisical.com/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "tenants", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 20, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": true, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free tier: 5 identities. Pro: $20/identity/month billed annually ($23/month month-to-month) (Infisical pricing).", "caveat": "Advanced ($40/identity/mo annual, adds dynamic secrets/SOC2 Type II) and Enterprise (custom) tiers not modeled.", "tiers": [{"upto": 5, "monthly": 0}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('tenant-credentials:doppler', 'tenant-credentials', 'Doppler', 'Doppler (Docker, Inc. spinoff)', 'A SaaS secrets/config manager built primarily for application env-vars and CI/CD secret injection, with a free tier and per-user team pricing.', 'Developer plan: free for up to 3 users, then $8/month per additional user (max 25 total). Team plan: $21/month per user (up to 500 users). Enterprise: custom.', '["Very fast to adopt - built for exactly the env-var-injection use case, with CLI/SDK integrations for Node out of the box.", "Free tier covers a one-person shop''s own team seats at zero cost.", "Enterprise tier offers on-prem/cloud deployment choice and dynamic secrets if the system ever needs that.", "''AI agents and non-human identities ride free'' - Doppler''s own claim - which matters here since most callers would be service accounts, not humans, unlike Infisical''s per-identity model."]'::jsonb, '["Pricing and product shape is oriented at platform/CI secrets (one secret per env var, shared across a deploy), not naturally at per-tenant dynamic rows the way migration 005 needs - it would likely be forced into an awkward shape (one Doppler project per tenant) rather than a native fit.", "No indication in its pricing page of per-tenant isolation as a first-class primitive - it isn''t built for multi-tenant customer credential storage.", "A SaaS-down scenario still blocks every decrypt/read the app needs, same as any remote KMS.", "Team-plan per-user pricing ($21/mo) is aimed at DevOps teams, not proportional to the number of tenant connections this system actually needs to store."]'::jsonb, 'Better fit for the Integrator''s own deployment secrets (JWT_SECRET, INGEST_TOKEN, OPENROUTER_API_KEY) than for per-tenant connection credentials - i.e., a Render-env-store replacement, not a migration-005 tenant-credential store.', 'Poor fit for migration 005 specifically - it solves the wrong problem (app config secrets) rather than the problem migration 005 has (many small, per-tenant, per-connection secrets keyed by tenant_id).', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://www.doppler.com/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "admin_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 8, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Developer plan: free for up to 3 users, then $8/month per additional user (max 25 total) (Doppler pricing).", "caveat": "Team plan ($21/user/mo, up to 500 users) and Enterprise (custom) not modeled.", "tiers": [{"upto": 3, "monthly": 0}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('tenant-credentials:1password-secrets-automation', 'tenant-credentials', '1Password Secrets Automation', '1Password', '1Password''s developer/infrastructure product for injecting secrets from a 1Password vault into applications and CI/CD via a Connect server or Service Accounts, layered on top of their password-manager platform.', 'not published at a standalone rate for this research pass - the dedicated product page returned a 404 on 2026-08-22, and general 1Password Business pricing (per-user, from roughly $8/user/month) does not clearly itemize the Secrets Automation add-on; would need a direct sales quote to confirm.', '["Leverages a vault product with a long operating history and strong brand trust in password/secret management generally.", "Service-account/Connect-server model separates human vault access from machine access to secrets.", "Likely bundles well if the team ever adopts 1Password Business for its own internal credential hygiene anyway.", "SOC 2 and broad enterprise compliance posture already established for the core 1Password product."]'::jsonb, '["Pricing could not be confirmed from a primary source this pass - the dedicated pricing page 404''d, which is itself a signal this is a secondary/legacy product line worth double-checking before relying on it.", "Architecturally aimed at ''secrets for CI/CD and infra'', the same category mismatch as Doppler - not designed around per-tenant customer credential rows.", "Requires running a Connect server (self-hosted proxy) for on-prem-style access, adding an operational component back in.", "Sixth-plus vendor relationship for a system that is trying to minimize exactly that."]'::jsonb, 'Not recommended to pursue further without a live sales conversation to confirm the product still exists in its documented form and get real pricing - treat as low priority pending that confirmation.', 'Same category mismatch as Doppler for migration 005''s actual need (per-tenant dynamic credential storage), and the pricing page''s 404 is itself a caution flag.', 'n/a', 'declining', 'https://1password.com/secrets-automation', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Dedicated product page returned a 404 on 2026-08-22, and general 1Password Business pricing (~$8/user/month) does not clearly itemize the Secrets Automation add-on.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 80),
  ('regulated-plane:scope-out-contractually', 'regulated-plane', 'Do not touch controlled data - scope out contractually', 'n/a - a contracting/architecture decision, not a product', 'Structure IPTA''s engagement so CDI/CUI never flows to or through this system - no technical system change, a contract-and-workflow change confirmed with IPTA''s contracting officer and written down.', 'not published - the cost is legal/consulting time to confirm scope with IPTA and, if needed, amend the SOW/subcontract language; effectively $0 in cloud spend.', '["Directly matches the acquisition.gov text of 252.204-7012: the clause is triggered by a system that ''processes, stores, or transmits'' covered defense information for contract performance - if the Integrator''s role in IPTA''s work is structurally kept out of that path, the clause''s technical obligations do not attach to it.", "Zero new infrastructure, zero new vendor, zero new annual bill - the single cheapest option by a wide margin for a one-person shop.", "Consistent with the project''s own existing rule (§ NEVER #7): route IPTA client-adjacent content through an agent only after residency is decided - the safest interim answer to ''decide residency'' is often ''decide it never lands here.''", "Buys time - CMMC Phase 2''s suspension and the FedRAMP Moderate dissolution both mean the compliant-cloud landscape is still shifting through 2027; not building a second environment against a moving target is defensible."]'::jsonb, '["Requires real diligence, not just an assumption - the open question already on record (''does IPTA actually hold a 7012 contract, and does it receive CUI'') has to be answered first, in writing, from IPTA''s contracting officer, not inferred.", "The acquisition.gov text is explicit that scope follows the system ''designated to support contract performance where such information may flow'' - an informal ''we just won''t put CUI in there'' policy without a documented technical boundary and contract language is not a defensible position if audited.", "If IPTA''s needs from the Integrator ever expand to genuinely require CUI (not just client-adjacent metadata), this option evaporates and one of the compliant-cloud options becomes mandatory on short notice.", "Provides no readiness runway - if the answer to the open question turns out to be ''yes, CUI flows,'' none of the enclave/GovCloud groundwork will have been done."]'::jsonb, 'The correct default position right now, today, until IPTA''s contracting officer confirms in writing whether 7012 and CUI actually apply - exactly the recommendation a small shop should reach for first.', 'This is not a migration-005 feature at all - it is the reason migration 005''s tenant_id/residency design can stay simple (pass-through/land-in-consumer/pooled) without a fourth ''regulated'' residency value, for as long as this holds.', 'pass-through', 'n/a', 'https://www.acquisition.gov/dfars/252.204-7012-safeguarding-covered-defense-information-and-cyber-incident-reporting.', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Not a technical/product cost — a contracting/architecture decision; effectively $0 in cloud spend, the cost is legal/consulting time to confirm scope with IPTA and amend SOW/subcontract language.", "caveat": "Legal/consulting time itself is not priced here."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('regulated-plane:m365-gcc-high', 'regulated-plane', 'Microsoft 365 GCC High (+ Azure Government for compute)', 'Microsoft', 'Microsoft''s segregated US-sovereign cloud for defense contractors, covering Office/Exchange/SharePoint/Teams under GCC High and compute/storage under Azure Government, both requiring DoD-affiliation screening to purchase.', 'Per-user licensing as of July 1, 2026: Business Premium $35.80/mo ($429.60/yr), Enterprise G3 $65.20/mo ($782.40/yr), Enterprise G5 $97.50/mo ($1,170/yr); for 10 users, roughly $4,300-$11,700/year in licensing alone, before any Azure Government compute/storage spend.', '["Business Premium is now positioned as an affordable GCC High entry point and was the one tier Microsoft did not raise on the July 2026 price change, making small-contractor entry cheaper than it used to be.", "Covers the collaboration surface (email, files, Teams) most small-contractor CUI work actually needs, not just compute.", "Widely deployed in the defense-contractor ecosystem, so MSPs (Agile IT, Summit 7) and documentation exist specifically for it.", "Sits alongside Azure Government for compute, giving a matched pair rather than mixing vendors for the collaboration and compute halves."]'::jsonb, '["Tenant provisioning alone can take ''up to 30 days per Microsoft SLA'' before any migration work even starts - a real onboarding delay, not a same-week decision.", "Requires proving DoD affiliation to purchase at all, which for a company whose primary business (LFODIE/Staffility/IPTA) is not exclusively defense work adds a qualification step before pricing even matters.", "Total cost is genuinely a second full IT environment - separate tenant, separate identities, separate admin overhead - not a flag on the existing Netlify/Render stack, exactly as the system prompt already establishes.", "The FedRAMP Moderate dissolution (mandatory transition by 2027-01-01, Classes A-D not mapped from the old baseline) creates near-term uncertainty about how GCC High''s own FedRAMP High authorization gets re-validated under the new regime - worth re-checking before committing multi-year spend."]'::jsonb, 'The standard, well-trodden choice once IPTA confirms CUI genuinely must be handled, if the actual workload is collaboration/file-sharing-shaped rather than needing custom compute.', 'Would host the regulated plane entirely outside today''s Render/Netlify/Supabase stack, as a wholly separate deployment per the card''s own framing (''a real CMMC path is a separate deployment, not a migration'').', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://secureframe.com/blog/gcc-high-pricing', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "admin_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 35.8, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Business Premium: $35.80/user/month ($429.60/yr) as of July 1, 2026 — the cheapest published GCC High tier.", "caveat": "Enterprise G3 ($65.20/user/mo) and G5 ($97.50/user/mo) tiers, and all Azure Government compute/storage spend on top of licensing, not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('regulated-plane:aws-govcloud', 'regulated-plane', 'AWS GovCloud (US)', 'Amazon Web Services', 'A physically and logically isolated AWS region restricted to vetted US persons/entities, FedRAMP-authorized at what AWS itself now calls ''Certified Class D (formerly High baseline)''.', 'No flat number published; industry estimates put required compliance tooling (CloudTrail, Config, Security Hub, GuardDuty) alone at roughly $1,500-$5,000/month ($18k-$60k/year), on top of a 10-15% premium over commercial AWS pricing for core EC2/S3/EBS compute and storage.', '["Highest DoD impact-level authorization among the hyperscalers - AWS states GovCloud supports ''high impact level'' workloads.", "If the Integrator''s API/Postgres stack were rebuilt there, the code is ''plain Express + pg'' per the card, so the application layer itself would port without a rewrite - only the infrastructure and secrets change.", "Mature ecosystem of DoD-focused MSPs and reference architectures.", "Reserved Instance pricing can claw back 40-60% of the on-demand premium for steady-state workloads, softening the long-run cost gap."]'::jsonb, '["Realistic all-in annual cost for even a minimal environment (compute + the mandatory security tooling stack) runs well into five figures - a large jump from today''s $7/mo Render Starter plus free-tier Supabase.", "Account approval/vetting and onboarding timeline could not be confirmed from a primary AWS source in this pass - treat as an open unknown, not a quick signup.", "AWS''s own naming (''Certified Class D, formerly High baseline'') reflects the FedRAMP 20x transition already underway - the authorization label the shop would rely on today is mid-change, with the 2027-01-01 mandatory-adoption date for the new Class structure.", "A second AWS account plus GovCloud''s own IAM/networking model is real new operational surface for a one-person team, on top of whatever MSP is hired to run it."]'::jsonb, 'Makes sense only for compute-heavy or infrastructure-custom CUI workloads where GCC High''s collaboration-first shape doesn''t fit - overkill for a system whose regulated need is ''store some connection credentials and metadata.''', 'Same ''separate deployment, not a migration'' framing as GCC High - a genuine second environment, not a mode migration 005 can toggle into on the existing hosts.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://docs.aws.amazon.com/govcloud-us/latest/UserGuide/govcloud-compliance.html', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 3250, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "No flat number published; industry estimates put required compliance tooling (CloudTrail, Config, Security Hub, GuardDuty) alone at roughly $1,500-5,000/month; midpoint used.", "caveat": "Excludes the underlying 10-15% premium over commercial AWS pricing for core EC2/S3/EBS compute/storage, and excludes all actual compute/storage spend itself — this is tooling overhead only."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('regulated-plane:google-assured-workloads', 'regulated-plane', 'Google Cloud Assured Workloads', 'Google Cloud', 'A control layer over standard Google Cloud that constrains a ''folder'' of resources to a chosen compliance regime (FedRAMP Moderate free; FedRAMP High, IL4/5, ITAR, CJIS at a 20% usage surcharge), rather than a wholly separate physical cloud.', 'FedRAMP Moderate: no additional charge beyond normal GCP usage. FedRAMP High / IL4 / IL5 / ITAR / CJIS: +20% surcharge on all billable GCP usage inside that folder; Enhanced or Premium Support subscription required. 60-day free trial available for the premium tiers.', '["Uniquely transparent, checkable pricing model (a flat 20% surcharge) compared to GCC High/GovCloud''s opaque quote-driven pricing - easiest of the three hyperscalers to actually budget from a public page.", "FedRAMP Moderate control package is free, which is notable, though per the established finding here FedRAMP Moderate itself is the baseline being dissolved by 2027-01-01, undercutting how much that free tier will mean going forward.", "Runs on standard Google Cloud services (not a separately air-gapped cloud like GCC High/GovCloud), so it may be less operationally distinct if the team already uses GCP for anything else.", "IL4/5 and ITAR are available as named, purchasable control packages rather than requiring a bespoke enclave vendor."]'::jsonb, '["Assured Workloads is a compliance-boundary wrapper, not a compliance guarantee by itself - it constrains where data can be processed but the contractor is still responsible for the full NIST 800-171/CMMC control implementation on top.", "The free FedRAMP Moderate tier is exactly the baseline the DoD CIO FAQ says cannot store CUI and that FedRAMP itself is dissolving by 2027-01-01 - not a real answer for 7012 without moving up to the 20%-surcharge IL4/5 or ITAR tier.", "Least-used of the three hyperscalers in the defense-contractor small-business ecosystem, per the search results here - fewer MSPs and less community documentation than GCC High or AWS GovCloud.", "20% is a surcharge on top of already-unknown base GCP usage - still not a flat annual number without first sizing the actual workload."]'::jsonb, 'Worth a serious look only if the team is already leaning toward GCP for other reasons - its transparent pricing model is a genuine advantage, but going in cold to a smaller ecosystem is a real switching cost.', 'Same separate-deployment framing as the other two hyperscalers for migration 005''s regulated plane - not a flag on the existing stack.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://cloud.google.com/assured-workloads/pricing', '{"kind": "markup", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 20, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "FedRAMP Moderate: no additional charge. FedRAMP High/IL4/IL5/ITAR/CJIS: +20% surcharge on all billable GCP usage inside the constrained folder, plus a required Enhanced/Premium Support subscription (Google Cloud pricing).", "caveat": "This is a percentage surcharge on whatever underlying GCP compute/storage spend already exists, not a token-spend markup; the Enhanced/Premium Support subscription cost itself is not itemized."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('regulated-plane:preveil', 'regulated-plane', 'PreVeil (Gov Community / PreVeil Pass)', 'PreVeil, LLC', 'An encrypted-email-and-drive overlay purpose-built for small defense contractors, hosted in AWS GovCloud, sold as a bundled compliance package rather than raw cloud infrastructure.', 'Gov Community tier: custom-quoted, hosted in AWS GovCloud. ''PreVeil Pass'' bundle: $450/month for 3 Gov Community licenses plus a compliance documentation package - the clearest small-business entry price found.', '["Purpose-built for exactly this shop''s profile: a small contractor needing CMMC Level 2/ITAR-covered email and file sharing without standing up its own cloud tenant.", "States FedRAMP Moderate Equivalent certification and FIPS 140-3 validated modules, and includes a pre-filled NIST 800-171/CMMC documentation package - real help with the paperwork burden, not just the technology.", "$450/month for a 3-seat bundle is concrete, public, and dramatically cheaper than GCC High or GovCloud entry costs for a handful of users.", "Overlay model (encrypted email/drive) is architecturally narrow - it doesn''t require re-platforming the whole business, just routing CUI-touching communication through it."]'::jsonb, '["Narrow scope - email and file/drive encryption only, not a general application-hosting environment; the Integrator''s Postgres/API/connection-layer work would still need a home elsewhere if it ever touches CUI directly.", "''FedRAMP Moderate Equivalent'' (not full FedRAMP Moderate authorization) is exactly the kind of self-attested equivalency the FedRAMP Moderate dissolution/Class A-D transition puts under new scrutiny - worth re-verifying what ''equivalent'' means once the 2027-01-01 mandatory rules land.", "Minimum commitment terms (3-9 users for 24 months, 10+ users for 12 months) lock in a term contract, atypical for a one-person shop''s usual month-to-month vendor posture.", "As an overlay, not a full enclave, a security assessor will still need to see how PreVeil-protected data is kept from leaking back into the non-compliant main stack - it reduces but doesn''t eliminate the boundary-drawing work."]'::jsonb, 'The strongest ''smallest real technical option'' if the answer to the open IPTA question is ''yes, but only email/file-sharing-shaped CUI'' rather than needing custom application hosting.', 'Solves the narrow slice of CUI handling (documents/messages) that might arise around IPTA work without requiring migration 005 to model a whole second application stack - but does not host the Integrator''s own database or API.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://www.preveil.com/new-pricing-page/', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "admin_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "''PreVeil Pass'' bundle: $450/month for 3 Gov Community licenses plus a compliance documentation package — the clearest small-business entry price found.", "caveat": "Beyond 3 licenses requires a custom Gov Community quote; per-additional-license rate not published.", "tiers": [{"upto": 3, "monthly": 450}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('regulated-plane:summit7-managed-enclave', 'regulated-plane', 'Summit 7 managed CMMC enclave', 'Summit 7', 'A fully managed GCC High-based CUI enclave sold specifically to small defense-industrial-base businesses, bundling the Microsoft licensing, configuration, and ongoing operations into one vendor relationship.', 'Not itemized on a public page at the per-user level; industry surveys of managed CMMC enclaves broadly (not Summit 7-specific pricing) put a fully managed enclave at roughly $300-$400/user/month, i.e. $3,000-$4,000+/month for a small team - confirm directly with Summit 7 before budgeting.', '["Removes essentially all of the operational burden GCC High would otherwise dump on a one-person shop - Summit 7 runs the tenant, not the internal team.", "Purpose-marketed at exactly this shop''s size (their own announcement targets small businesses supporting DoD).", "Bundles the Microsoft licensing purchase-eligibility screening and setup into the vendor relationship rather than making the shop navigate it alone.", "Established managed-services track record specifically in the CMMC/GCC High space, reducing the risk of hiring an inexperienced integrator."]'::jsonb, '["At an estimated $300-$400/user/month, this is by far the most expensive option in this set on a per-seat basis - a handful of users could run $36,000-$50,000+/year.", "No public per-seat number was found for Summit 7 specifically - the figure above is an industry-wide managed-enclave estimate, not a vendor quote, and must be verified before any commitment.", "Onboarding for managed enclaves generally runs ''days to weeks'' for the platform itself, but full CMMC readiness (documentation, SSP, assessment prep) takes materially longer - the platform date is not the compliance-ready date.", "Same underlying GCC High FedRAMP-transition uncertainty (2027-01-01 mandatory Class structure) applies here as it does to buying GCC High directly - a managed wrapper doesn''t insulate from that."]'::jsonb, 'The right call if the team decides it needs a full regulated enclave but has zero appetite to run any part of it itself - pay the premium specifically to make it someone else''s operational problem.', 'Same as raw GCC High: a wholesale separate deployment, and at a materially higher price than doing it directly - worth it only for the operator-burden reduction, which matters a lot for a one-person shop.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://www.summit7.us/blog/summit-7-releases-managed-cmmc-solution-for-small-businesses-supporting-the-us-department-of-defense', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "admin_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 350, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Not itemized publicly for Summit 7 specifically; industry surveys of managed CMMC enclaves broadly put this at roughly $300-400/user/month; midpoint used.", "caveat": "This is a broad industry estimate, not Summit 7''s own confirmed rate — confirm directly with Summit 7 before budgeting."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('regulated-plane:kiteworks-private-cloud', 'regulated-plane', 'Kiteworks Private Cloud', 'Kiteworks', 'A private, single-tenant deployment of Kiteworks'' secure file-sharing/content-communication platform, marketed specifically at CMMC Level 2 and FedRAMP-authorized deployment options.', 'Not published at a small-scale per-seat rate in this research pass - pricing is quote-driven; Kiteworks does appear on the FedRAMP Marketplace as an authorized offering, which is a meaningful signal but not a price.', '["Listed on FedRAMP''s own marketplace, a stronger authorization signal than a vendor''s self-description alone.", "Like PreVeil, scoped narrowly to secure content exchange (file sharing, messaging) rather than requiring a full application re-platform.", "Private-cloud (single-tenant) deployment option addresses the ''don''t want to share infrastructure with unknown other tenants'' concern some assessors raise.", "Positioned specifically for CMMC 2.0 Level 2, i.e. the same level 7012/NIST 800-171 implies here."]'::jsonb, '["No concrete price found from a primary source - every figure in the search results was a comparison-blog estimate, not a Kiteworks number; a sales conversation is required before this can be budgeted.", "Same content-exchange-only scope limitation as PreVeil - does not host the Integrator''s database/API/connection layer, only secures document/file movement.", "Smaller ecosystem presence in the small-defense-contractor space specifically compared to PreVeil or the GCC High managed-enclave vendors, based on available search coverage.", "''Private Cloud'' single-tenant deployments are typically priced at an enterprise level even for small seat counts - likely a poor cost fit for a one or two-person regulated-data need."]'::jsonb, 'Worth a quote only if the actual regulated need turns out to be large-file/content-collaboration-heavy in a way PreVeil''s email/drive model doesn''t cover well.', 'Same narrow content-exchange fit as PreVeil for migration 005''s likely actual need - not a home for the API/database, and pricing must be confirmed before it can be compared fairly.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://www.fedramp.gov/marketplace/products/F1511167634/', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Not published at a small-scale per-seat rate; pricing is quote-driven, though Kiteworks appears on the FedRAMP Marketplace as an authorized offering (a signal, not a price).", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('regulated-plane:agile-it-managed', 'regulated-plane', 'Agile IT managed GCC High / CMMC services', 'Agile IT', 'A managed-services provider offering both GCC High tenant implementation (''AgileAscend'') and ongoing managed operations (''AgileDefend'') for small-to-mid defense contractors.', 'Not published at a flat rate in this research pass - Agile IT sells implementation and ongoing management as services layered on top of Microsoft''s own GCC High licensing costs; requires a direct quote.', '["Splits the problem cleanly into a one-time implementation engagement plus an ongoing managed-services subscription, which may be easier for a one-person shop to reason about than an all-in-one enclave price.", "Available directly through the Microsoft commercial marketplace, simplifying procurement paperwork.", "Specializes specifically in the GCC High/CMMC MSP niche, similar to Summit 7, giving a second vendor option to compare against.", "Ongoing managed services (AgileDefend) exist as a distinct offering from implementation, so the team could in principle do a lighter-touch implementation and evaluate before committing to full ongoing management."]'::jsonb, '["No public pricing found for either AgileAscend or AgileDefend - a sales conversation is required for even a rough number.", "Still requires buying the underlying GCC High licenses separately (roughly $4,300-$11,700/year for 10 users per the earlier figures), so Agile IT''s fee is additive on top of Microsoft''s own cost, not a replacement for it.", "Smaller company than Microsoft or the hyperscalers - due diligence on Agile IT''s own security posture and business continuity matters more here.", "Same underlying GCC High FedRAMP-transition uncertainty applies, since Agile IT is reselling/managing Microsoft''s platform, not an independent authorization."]'::jsonb, 'A reasonable second quote to get alongside Summit 7 once the team has decided it wants a managed GCC High relationship rather than self-managing the tenant.', 'Same separate-deployment framing as GCC High directly, with the same ''someone else runs it'' value proposition as Summit 7 - compare quotes between the two rather than assuming either is cheaper.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://agileit.com/gcc-high-guide/ongoing-managed-services/', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Not published at a flat rate — sold as implementation/ongoing management services layered on top of Microsoft''s own GCC High licensing costs; requires a direct quote.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('regulated-plane:on-prem-separate-machine', 'regulated-plane', 'On-premises / a physically separate machine', 'n/a - owner-operated hardware', 'A dedicated, air-gapped or network-isolated machine physically controlled by the business, holding only the regulated-plane workload, with no path back to the Render/Netlify/Supabase stack.', 'not published - cost is one-time hardware (a few hundred to low thousands of dollars for a capable small server/workstation) plus the owner''s own time; no recurring cloud bill, though a compliant setup still needs endpoint security tooling, backup, and possibly a NIST 800-171-aligned configuration review.', '["No cloud vendor relationship at all for the regulated workload - the DFARS Security Protection Data concern (a provider holding credentials/logs that grant access) is minimized when there is no third-party provider in that path.", "One-time hardware cost rather than an ongoing per-seat or per-usage bill - potentially the cheapest technical (not scope-out) option over a multi-year horizon.", "Full control over physical security, patching cadence, and data location - answers the ''where does it run'' question about as concretely as possible.", "The DFARS 7012 fixture used elsewhere in this project (the DFARS contract scanner, per this file''s own changelog) already established the precedent of doing sensitive-document work entirely on Bill''s own machine with local tools and no network egress, precisely to avoid exactly this kind of exposure - direct proof this pattern already works for this business."]'::jsonb, '["The business still has to independently implement and evidence all 110 NIST SP 800-171 Rev 2 controls itself - ''on-prem'' removes the cloud vendor but does not remove the compliance work; a small business assessing itself against 110 controls without a platform''s help is a real burden.", "No path to a FedRAMP-anything authorization inherits from this option - it stands entirely on the business''s own SSP, POA&M, and eventual C3PAO assessment.", "A single physical machine is a single point of failure for backup/disaster-recovery purposes, and 7012''s 90-day forensic-preservation requirement needs real process discipline to satisfy without cloud tooling''s built-in logging.", "Scaling beyond a handful of users or beyond simple document/credential storage (e.g. needing real compute, redundancy, or multi-location access) quickly outgrows what a single machine can responsibly provide."]'::jsonb, 'The right technical (not scope-out) fallback specifically because this business has already proven the pattern works for it once, with the DFARS scanner - reach for this before any cloud-enclave vendor if the actual regulated workload stays small and local by nature (documents, credential storage, not a live multi-user service).', 'A genuine second, physically separate deployment as the card''s own rule requires (''a real CMMC path is a separate deployment, not a migration'') - and the cheapest one, at the cost of doing the compliance paperwork with no vendor''s help.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://www.acquisition.gov/dfars/252.204-7012-safeguarding-covered-defense-information-and-cyber-incident-reporting.', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 1500, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "One-time hardware cost only (a few hundred to low thousands of dollars for a capable small server/workstation); no recurring cloud bill; midpoint ($1,500) used for setup_usd.", "caveat": "Owner''s own time, endpoint security tooling, backup, and a possible NIST 800-171-aligned configuration review are not priced; base_monthly is $0 since there is no recurring vendor bill."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 80),
  ('ai-gateway:azure-ai-foundry', 'ai-gateway', 'Azure AI Foundry', 'Microsoft', 'Microsoft''s unified model-serving and agent platform (formerly Azure AI Studio) for running LLM inference against 11,000+ hosted and partner models, including Azure OpenAI.', 'As of Aug 2026: pay-as-you-go is per-token, priced per model (e.g. gpt-4o-mini: $0.15/1M input tokens, $0.60/1M output tokens, global standard deployment). Provisioned Throughput Units (PTU) are a separate committed-capacity model, billed hourly or as a monthly reservation independent of token volume — confirmed global Provisioned Managed Unit at $1.00/hour, and a $312/month reservation meter for Provisioned Throughput Units; regional/data-zone PTU SKUs run $1.10-$2.20/hour. PAYG is the right fit at this scale; PTU only pays off at sustained high volume.', '["Pay-as-you-go token pricing needs zero capacity planning at small scale", "Structured outputs (JSON schema strict mode) natively supported on OpenAI models", "Azure Government has a real model subset (GPT-4.1, GPT-4.1-mini, GPT-4o, o3-mini, GPT-5.1, embeddings) with PTU support there too", "One platform covers both the highest-compliance tenant and the ordinary ones"]'::jsonb, '["Azure Gov model list is a narrower subset than commercial, and lags new model releases", "PTU pricing is committed-capacity and hourly, a poor fit for spiky small-shop traffic", "Structured-output support is per-model/per-endpoint, not universal across all 11,000+ catalog models", "Still a provider-specific integration, unlike OpenRouter''s one-API-many-providers abstraction"]'::jsonb, 'A shop that must run the same model behind a genuine FedRAMP-High-adjacent Azure Government boundary for regulated data, not just a residency label.', 'For the two ordinary tenants this is a straight swap for OpenRouter with worse model breadth; for the DFARS-carrying sibling company it is the one path (paired with the rest of an Azure Government deployment) that can legitimately hold CUI, which OpenRouter/Render never can.', 'pooled', 'growing', 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/foundry/foundry-models/concepts/models-sold-directly-by-azure-gov', '{"kind": "usage_passthrough", "driver": "tokens_m", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.375, "tiers": [], "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Azure Retail Prices API, gpt-4o-mini global standard PAYG: $0.15/1M input tokens + $0.60/1M output tokens; per_unit is the simple blended average of the two ($0.375/1M tokens), not usage-weighted.", "caveat": "Excludes PTU (committed-hourly/monthly, not usage-based), fine-tuning, batch, and Azure Government''s own rate card, which was not separately priced in this pass."}'::jsonb, 'chosen', 'Josh 2026-08-05: Azure AI Foundry is the default; which service is used is configuration, not code.', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('ai-gateway:openrouter', 'ai-gateway', 'OpenRouter', 'OpenRouter, Inc.', 'A hosted single-API router that lets one key call hundreds of models across providers, billed at each provider''s own list price.', 'No markup on inference — you pay the provider''s rate. OpenRouter''s own revenue is a funding fee: 5.5% (min $0.80) on card top-ups, 5% on crypto. (Verified from OpenRouter''s own FAQ, Aug 2026.)', '["Already wired in end to end — services/gateway.ts, ai_agent_runs, and OpenRouter''s own usage.cost field are live and proven.", "No inference markup; the only extra cost is the one-time credit-funding fee, not a per-call tax.", "One key, hundreds of models, including cheap open-weight options that fit the cheapest-capable-by-default rule.", "Per-API-key filtering on OpenRouter''s own Activity dashboard, a coarser mirror of what ai_agent_runs already tracks per agent."]'::jsonb, '["Structured outputs (json_schema) only work on ''select'' endpoints, and support depends on which provider is serving that model that day — require_parameters:true is mandatory or a route silently degrades.", "Reported cost is OpenRouter''s own account credits, not literal USD, until the credit-to-USD rate is confirmed (already flagged as § NEVER #9).", "Single hosted vendor sits between every agent and every model — one outage there stops everything.", "No team console or seat model at all; it''s a developer API, fine here since only gateway.ts touches it, but nothing to hand a non-developer to browse."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick for a solo-owner shop that wants zero markup, zero new ops, and has already paid the integration cost.', 'This is what''s running today; nothing else researched beats its price (zero markup) without adding a new vendor, a new failure mode, or both.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://openrouter.ai/docs/faq', '{"kind": "markup", "driver": "tokens_m", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 5.5, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "derived", "basis": "No inference markup — you pay the provider''s own rate; OpenRouter''s revenue is a 5.5% fee (min $0.80) on card top-ups (5% on crypto), which in steady state is the practical cost of funding usage.", "caveat": "Not a literal per-call markup — the fee applies to funding transactions, so effective overhead depends on top-up frequency and payment method."}'::jsonb, 'shortlist', 'Named by Josh as an optional alternative alongside Ollama and LM Studio, and it is what the Integrator runs today — so the two plans already agree that the provider is a config value.', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('ai-gateway:litellm', 'ai-gateway', 'LiteLLM', 'BerriAI', 'An open-source LLM proxy (self-host, MIT license) that normalizes 100+ providers behind one OpenAI-shaped API, with a paid Enterprise edition for SSO, audit logs and support.', 'Self-hosted core: free forever, no card. Enterprise: annual, priced to gateway request volume and deployment architecture, described as ''never per token'' — no published number, sales-quote only. (Verified litellm.ai/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["Self-host means zero per-call markup ever and no third party sees the traffic — closest fit to ''control plane, not a database.''", "Widest provider coverage researched, with documented per-request fallback lists similar to the existing escalation model.", "Enterprise tier adds SSO/SCIM and audit logs if a second admin or a compliance ask ever needs them.", "Its own community has documented the exact structured-output + fallback-model failure mode this codebase already guards against."]'::jsonb, '["Self-hosting means owning a second running service — a daemon needing patches and uptime, exactly the operational burden this shop is built to avoid.", "Migrating off OpenRouter means rewriting the only provider-aware file and re-validating the escalation rule against a different error taxonomy.", "The free edition has no built-in per-caller billing UI — ai_agent_runs still does that job, so switching buys no observability upgrade.", "Enterprise pricing is opaque until a sales call, which is friction for a two-person shop with no procurement team."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick only if self-hosted data control or provider breadth becomes a hard requirement OpenRouter genuinely can''t meet.', 'Poor fit today — trading zero ops burden for zero markup savings that OpenRouter already provides.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://www.litellm.ai/pricing', '{"kind": "byo", "driver": "tokens_m", "base_monthly": 7, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Self-hosted core is free forever (MIT, no card); base_monthly is a placeholder for the smallest VM to run the proxy.", "caveat": "Enterprise edition is annual, sales-quoted to gateway request volume and deployment architecture — no published number, not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('ai-gateway:portkey', 'ai-gateway', 'Portkey', 'Portkey AI', 'A hosted AI gateway with a real admin console for routing, caching, guardrails and cost dashboards, built on an open-source gateway core.', 'Free forever up to 10k logs/month. Production: $49/mo for 100k logs, then $9 per additional 100k. Enterprise: custom. Self-hosted OSS core exists but is the stripped-down tier (community support only). (Verified portkey.ai/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["A real multi-seat admin UI, unlike OpenRouter''s developer-only dashboard.", "Open-source gateway core can be self-hosted at no license cost if the paid dashboard is ever dropped.", "Built-in guardrails and semantic caching that the current registry has none of.", "Structured-output handling passes through to the underlying provider the same way OpenRouter''s does — no regression."]'::jsonb, '["$49/mo recurring for a log-viewing job ai_agent_runs already does for free, with no clearly new capability for this system.", "Per-caller cost tracking is mentioned in customer testimonials but the pricing page doesn''t spell out the mechanics — would need hands-on testing before trusting it as an audit source.", "Migrating the invoke path off OpenRouter carries the same rewrite cost as LiteLLM.", "Self-hosted OSS edition explicitly lacks semantic caching and the fuller observability the paid tiers sell."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick if the owner specifically wants a clickable multi-seat gateway console and $49-199/mo is acceptable for it.', 'Redundant with the AI Agents registry''s own screen — pays for a UI to duplicate a job already done.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://portkey.ai/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "ai_calls", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 9e-05, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Portkey published tiers: Free up to 10,000 logs/month; Production $49/mo for 100,000 logs, then $9 per additional 100,000 (portkey.ai/pricing).", "caveat": "Enterprise tier is custom; the self-hosted OSS core is a stripped-down community-support-only tier, not modeled separately.", "tiers": [{"upto": 10000, "monthly": 0}, {"upto": 100000, "monthly": 49}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('ai-gateway:direct-provider-apis', 'ai-gateway', 'Direct provider APIs', 'OpenAI / Anthropic / Google (per provider)', 'Skip the gateway layer entirely and call each model provider''s own API directly from services/gateway.ts.', 'Each provider''s own list price, no markup at all — the same net cost as OpenRouter today since OpenRouter already charges zero inference markup.', '["Removes OpenRouter as a single point of failure between LFODIE and every model provider.", "Some providers'' native structured-output modes are stricter than OpenRouter''s pass-through of the same feature.", "No new vendor relationship or ToS to evaluate for the CUI/data-residency question.", "Costs are exactly the provider''s own billing — no ''account credits vs. real dollars'' ambiguity."]'::jsonb, '["Every new provider becomes new code (a new key, a new client, new error handling) — breaks the ''everything variable is a row'' philosophy the whole registry is built on.", "No cheapest-first catalogue like GET /agents/models; someone has to hand-maintain a rate card, the exact chore the current design avoided.", "Loses the one-key-many-models flexibility that lets an agent''s model field change without an ops task.", "Per-caller cost computation has to be reimplemented per provider''s own usage response shape."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick only if OpenRouter''s aggregate risk (single vendor, credit-based billing) becomes unacceptable and just one or two providers are actually in use.', 'A step backward from the row-based registry design; worth it only if OpenRouter itself is the problem, not a cost play.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/features/structured-outputs', '{"kind": "usage_passthrough", "driver": "tokens_m", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Each provider''s own list price, no markup at all — the same net cost as OpenRouter today since OpenRouter already charges zero inference markup.", "caveat": "Actual per-token rate depends on which model/provider is chosen; not itemized here."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('ai-gateway:vercel-ai-gateway', 'ai-gateway', 'Vercel AI Gateway', 'Vercel Inc.', 'Vercel''s hosted multi-provider gateway, positioned as neutral infrastructure usable even by apps not hosted on Vercel.', 'No markup and no platform fee on tokens — pay-as-you-go via prepaid Gateway Credits at the provider''s own list price; BYOK also carries no markup. Paid add-ons: custom-reporting tags $0.075/1,000 writes + $5/1,000 queries, team-wide provider allowlist $0.10/1,000 requests, team-wide zero-data-retention $0.10/1,000 requests. (Verified vercel.com/docs/ai-gateway/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["Genuinely zero markup like OpenRouter, backed by a much larger infrastructure company.", "Custom Reporting can tag every request with a user ID or quota-entity ID — built-in per-caller attribution OpenRouter''s dashboard doesn''t offer.", "A free tier exists (subset of models) before any credit purchase is required.", "No requirement to host anything on Vercel itself to use the gateway."]'::jsonb, '["The per-caller cost-tagging feature is a metered add-on, not free — the ''better attribution'' selling point has its own bill.", "Structured-output support is provider-dependent exactly like OpenRouter — no verified improvement.", "Smaller model catalogue than OpenRouter''s; better known inside the Vercel ecosystem than as a standalone gateway.", "Migrating gateway.ts to a different response shape for no clear cost win over the current zero-markup setup."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick if the org were already building on Vercel, or wanted built-in per-request cost tagging enough to pay the metering fee for it.', 'No real advantage over OpenRouter for this Render/Netlify stack; ai_agent_runs already gives per-agent attribution for free.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://vercel.com/docs/ai-gateway/pricing', '{"kind": "usage_passthrough", "driver": "tokens_m", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "No markup and no platform fee on tokens — pay-as-you-go via prepaid Gateway Credits at the provider''s own list price; BYOK also carries no markup (vercel.com/docs/ai-gateway/pricing).", "caveat": "Optional add-ons (custom-reporting tags $0.075/1,000 writes + $5/1,000 queries, provider allowlist $0.10/1,000 requests, zero-data-retention $0.10/1,000 requests) not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('ai-gateway:cloudflare-ai-gateway', 'ai-gateway', 'Cloudflare AI Gateway', 'Cloudflare, Inc.', 'A free proxy/cache/analytics layer that sits in front of any model provider''s API as part of Cloudflare''s platform, rather than a gateway that replaces the provider relationship.', 'Core features (analytics, caching, rate limiting) are free on every plan when you bring your own provider keys. ''Unified Billing'' (letting Cloudflare hold the model spend) carries a 5% fee on credit purchases; Logpush beyond 10M free logs/month on Workers Paid is $0.05/million. (Verified developers.cloudflare.com/ai-gateway/reference/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["Genuinely free as a proxy/cache/log layer if you keep your own provider keys (BYOK) instead of Cloudflare''s unified billing.", "Custom metadata tagging is the documented mechanism for per-caller cost attribution.", "Dollar-denominated spend limits are a real safety feature the current registry has no equivalent of.", "Cloudflare is already a credible edge/proxy operator — thematically close to what Netlify''s own proxy does for this app."]'::jsonb, '["Adding Cloudflare in front of OpenRouter is a second network hop and a second vendor for a system whose whole design goal is one controlled doorway.", "The 5% unified-billing fee only applies if Cloudflare holds the spend; otherwise you''re back to managing provider keys directly, which OpenRouter currently avoids.", "No agent-registry concept at all — the existing ai_agents / ai_agent_runs tables would still be needed on top.", "Free-tier log retention is capped (100k logs on Workers Free), so long-run history still lives in Postgres — no real observability upgrade over what''s built."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick as a caching/rate-limit/spend-cap layer in front of a gateway, not as a gateway replacement.', 'Solves a problem (edge caching, spend caps) the system hasn''t hit yet, at the cost of an extra hop, without removing OpenRouter.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/ai-gateway/reference/pricing/', '{"kind": "markup", "driver": "tokens_m", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 5, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Core proxy/analytics/caching are free on every plan when bringing your own provider keys; optional ''Unified Billing'' (Cloudflare holds the model spend) carries a 5% fee on credit purchases (developers.cloudflare.com/ai-gateway/reference/pricing).", "caveat": "0% overhead if using your own provider keys directly instead of Unified Billing; Logpush beyond 10M free logs/month ($0.05/million) not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('ai-gateway:requesty', 'ai-gateway', 'Requesty', 'Requesty, Inc.', 'A smaller hosted AI gateway (600+ models) that competes directly with OpenRouter on simplicity, at a real markup.', 'Free tier: free models only, 200 requests/day, no card. Paid: pay-as-you-go with a 5% markup on provider rates, no per-seat fee, no minimum spend. (Verified requesty.ai/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["No per-seat or minimum-spend friction, matching the small-shop constraint.", "Enterprise tier offers SSO/RBAC if a second admin or compliance need ever arises.", "A simple, transparent 5% markup is easy to explain to a non-developer owner.", "Listed on AWS Marketplace, useful if procurement ever needs to route through existing AWS billing."]'::jsonb, '["A 5% markup on every token is strictly worse than OpenRouter''s zero markup for identical functionality.", "Much smaller company and community than OpenRouter, with less evidence of long-run stability.", "No independent confirmation found of per-caller cost attribution beyond the vendor''s own marketing copy.", "Nothing in its feature set justifies paying a markup to leave a free, already-integrated incumbent."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick only if OpenRouter itself becomes unavailable or unacceptable and a like-for-like hosted alternative is needed fast.', 'Directly comparable to OpenRouter but strictly more expensive for this usage pattern — no reason to switch today.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://www.requesty.ai/pricing', '{"kind": "markup", "driver": "tokens_m", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 5, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Paid tier: pay-as-you-go with a flat 5% markup on provider rates, no per-seat fee, no minimum spend (requesty.ai/pricing).", "caveat": "Free tier (free models only, 200 requests/day, no card) not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('ai-gateway:aws-bedrock', 'ai-gateway', 'AWS Bedrock', 'Amazon Web Services', 'AWS''s managed multi-model API (Anthropic, Meta, Amazon and others), billed per token like a direct provider but inside AWS''s own account/IAM boundary.', 'Pure per-token pricing, no separate platform fee — from under $0.10 to $75+ per million tokens depending on the model — though real deployments report added costs from provisioned throughput and cross-service data transfer. (Verified aws.amazon.com/bedrock/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["The only option researched with a FedRAMP High / DoD IL-4-5 authorized path: Bedrock models in AWS GovCloud (US) received that approval per AWS''s 2026 announcements — directly relevant if IPTA''s DFARS obligations ever require a genuinely separate compliant AI plane.", "Per-token pricing with AWS''s own cost-allocation tags gives per-caller attribution through standard AWS billing tooling.", "No per-request markup on top of the model price.", "Backed by AWS SLAs and account-level IAM, a stronger security boundary than a bare third-party API key."]'::jsonb, '["Requires standing up an AWS account and IAM from a shop with no AWS footprint today; GovCloud specifically is a separate, harder-to-obtain environment, not a checkbox.", "Narrower model catalogue than OpenRouter''s — a new model means AWS has to have onboarded it, not a config change.", "None of gateway.ts is AWS-aware today; adopting Bedrock means a new cloud SDK dependency and new code, not a row.", "The FedRAMP High path is GovCloud-specific and is a genuinely separate deployment, matching CLAUDE.md''s own framing that a real CMMC path is not a Render migration."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick specifically and only for a DFARS/CUI-adjacent workload, if IPTA''s contracts turn out to require a FedRAMP-authorized model path — never for the general-purpose registry.', 'Wrong fit for day-to-day agent traffic; exactly right if and only if the open CUI question resolves to ''yes, IPTA needs a compliant AI path.''', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://aws.amazon.com/bedrock/pricing/', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "tokens_m", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.1, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Pure per-token pricing, no separate platform fee; AWS''s own published range spans $0.10 to $75+ per million tokens depending on model (aws.amazon.com/bedrock/pricing) — low end used as the base rate.", "caveat": "Actual rate is highly model-dependent (up to ~750x higher than the low end used); provisioned throughput and cross-service data transfer add further cost not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 80),
  ('ai-observability:ai-agent-runs', 'ai-observability', 'Keep ai_agent_runs (home-grown)', 'n/a — in-house', 'The existing Postgres table: every OpenRouter call already writes model, escalation flag, tokens, cost, latency and outcome, foreign-keyed to the agent and consumer app that triggered it.', '$0 incremental — runs on the already-provisioned Supabase Postgres; the STATE PROBE reported 0 rows as of 2026-08-23, so there is no scale cost yet either.', '["Already joined by real foreign keys to ai_agents and consumer_apps — no separate tool to reconcile a run against which prompt and which caller produced it.", "No new vendor, no new login, no new place run content could leak to — directly relevant to the DFARS/CUI angle if any agent ever touches IPTA-adjacent content.", "GET /agents/spend already turns it into a report; extending it further is a SQL query, not a vendor integration.", "Retention is whatever Bill decides in Postgres — no vendor-imposed 14/30/90-day cutoff to negotiate for a multi-year hiring-decision audit trail."]'::jsonb, '["No prompt-version diffing, side-by-side trace comparison, or automated eval scoring — anything past ''what happened and what did it cost'' is hand-built.", "No dedicated UI yet — reading it means a SQL client or a screen nobody has built; a non-developer owner can''t casually browse runs today.", "No OpenTelemetry export, so it can''t feed a future centralized observability tool without a bridge being written.", "A prompt-version hash column (needed for defensible governance under evolving hiring-AI rules) doesn''t exist yet and would need to be added and backfilled."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick as long as volume and audience stay small and the governance need is ''query the database,'' not ''hand a dashboard to a non-technical reviewer.''', 'Best fit today by a wide margin — it is already load-bearing, already wired into the escalation rule, and a vendor here would duplicate exactly what this schema was built to do.', 'pooled', 'mature', 'https://supabase.com/pricing', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Existing Postgres table, already provisioned on the shared Supabase project — $0 incremental cost; the STATE PROBE reported 0 rows as of 2026-08-23 so there is no scale cost yet either.", "caveat": "Storage cost would eventually accrue to the shared Supabase Postgres plan as rows grow, not broken out separately."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('ai-observability:langfuse', 'ai-observability', 'Langfuse', 'Langfuse GmbH', 'An open-source LLM engineering platform (tracing, prompt management, evals, datasets) with both a free self-hosted edition and a hosted cloud product.', 'Hobby (free): 50k units/mo, 30-day retention, 2 users. Core: $29/mo, 100k units, $8/100k over, 90-day retention. Pro: $199/mo, 3-year retention. Enterprise: $2,499/mo, audit logs + SCIM. Fully open-source and self-hostable (Docker/K8s/AWS/GCP/Azure) at every tier. (Verified langfuse.com/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["Prompt versioning is included on every plan, including the free one — directly useful for the hiring-decision audit-trail question.", "3-year data retention is available on the Pro self-hosted-or-cloud tier, which is the kind of multi-year window a hiring-AI risk assessment would want.", "Genuinely open-source and self-hostable end to end, not a stripped-down community edition like some competitors.", "SOC2 Type II and ISO27001 reports exist from Pro up, useful if a client ever asks about the AI vendor chain."]'::jsonb, '["A second tool with its own login and its own copy of every prompt/response, expanding the surface a DFARS/CUI concern would need to cover.", "Self-hosting it is a second running service to operate — the same daemon-ownership cost this shop is built to minimize.", "The free Hobby tier''s 30-day retention is shorter than what a defensible multi-year hiring audit trail needs, so real use means a $29-199/mo bill.", "Duplicates the join between agent, caller and cost that ai_agent_runs already has for free, just with a nicer UI."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick once the owner wants prompt-version diffing, side-by-side trace review, or LLM-graded evals that ai_agent_runs cannot do on its own.', 'The strongest vendor option if a dedicated tool is ever justified — self-hostable, has real governance features — but not yet a proven need over the free incumbent.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://langfuse.com/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "traces", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 8e-05, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Langfuse published tiers: Hobby (free) 50,000 units/mo, 30-day retention; Core $29/mo for 100,000 units, $8 per additional 100,000 (langfuse.com/pricing).", "caveat": "Pro ($199/mo, 3-year retention) and Enterprise ($2,499/mo, audit logs/SCIM) tiers not modeled; fully open-source self-hosting at every tier not modeled as a byo alternative.", "tiers": [{"upto": 50000, "monthly": 0}, {"upto": 100000, "monthly": 29}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('ai-observability:arize-phoenix', 'ai-observability', 'Arize Phoenix', 'Arize AI', 'A fully open-source (self-hosted) LLM tracing and evaluation tool built on OpenTelemetry, with prompt management and a playground.', 'Free, open-source, self-hosted (Docker, Kubernetes/Helm, or deploy to Railway/Render/GCP/Azure/AWS). No hosted-cloud tier confirmed in this research. (Verified github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix, Aug 2026.)', '["No license cost at any scale — matches the free-incumbent baseline instead of undercutting it with a paid tier later.", "Built on OpenTelemetry from the ground up, so it is the option best positioned to receive traces from other tools later without a bespoke bridge.", "Includes prompt versioning and dataset/experiment tracking, both relevant to a defensible hiring-AI audit trail.", "Can be deployed directly on Render (already the API''s host), which is a smaller operational jump than a brand-new platform."]'::jsonb, '["Self-hosted only in the researched material — another daemon to run, patch and keep up, on top of the API and the admin.", "No confirmed hosted/managed offering means the small-shop ''no daemon'' preference is violated by definition.", "No confirmed SOC2/compliance attestations, which would matter if a client ever asked about the observability vendor chain.", "The specific OSS license text was not independently confirmed in this research — flagged rather than assumed Apache-2.0."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick for a team that wants OpenTelemetry-native tracing and evals with zero license cost and is willing to run one more service to get it.', 'A credible free self-hosted alternative to Langfuse, but it adds an operator burden the current in-house table avoids entirely.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix', '{"kind": "byo", "driver": "traces", "base_monthly": 5, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Fully open-source, self-hosted (Docker/K8s/cloud) with no license fee; no hosted-cloud tier confirmed (GitHub, Aug 2026).", "caveat": "base_monthly is a placeholder small-VM hosting estimate; operator time not priced."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('ai-observability:helicone', 'ai-observability', 'Helicone', 'Helicone, Inc.', 'A hosted LLM observability and logging proxy with alerting, reporting, and a query language over request logs.', 'Hobby (free): 10,000 requests, 1 GB storage, 7-day retention, 1 seat. Pro: $79/mo, unlimited seats, 1-month retention. Team: $799/mo, SOC2/HIPAA, 3-month retention. Enterprise: custom, includes on-prem deployment. (Verified helicone.ai/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["Free tier has no seat limit issue for a 2-person shop and requires no card to try.", "HQL (a query language over logs) and alerting are more built-out than anything ai_agent_runs offers today.", "On-prem deployment exists at Enterprise, a path back to self-hosting if the hosted product is ever a concern.", "Structured, tiered retention windows make the cost of longer audit history explicit and plannable."]'::jsonb, '["Free-tier retention (7 days) is far too short for any governance/audit use; real retention needs the $79-799/mo tiers.", "The jump from Pro ($79) to Team ($799) for SOC2/HIPAA and 3-month retention is steep for a 2-person shop.", "On-prem/self-host is Enterprise-only — no accessible free self-hosted path, unlike Langfuse or Phoenix.", "A second vendor holding a copy of every prompt/response, which is exactly the kind of new exposure the DFARS angle warns about."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick for a team that wants a hosted, easy-onboarding logging layer and is comfortable paying for meaningful retention.', 'Weaker fit than Langfuse for this system specifically — no accessible self-host tier and a steep price step to reach compliance-relevant retention.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://www.helicone.ai/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "ai_calls", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Helicone published tiers: Hobby (free) 10,000 requests, 1GB storage, 7-day retention; Pro $79/mo, unlimited seats, 1-month retention (helicone.ai/pricing).", "caveat": "Team ($799/mo, SOC2/HIPAA, 3-month retention) and Enterprise (custom, on-prem) tiers not modeled; Pro has no stated request-volume ceiling in the reviewed material.", "tiers": [{"upto": 10000, "monthly": 0}, {"upto": null, "monthly": 79}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('ai-observability:braintrust', 'ai-observability', 'Braintrust', 'Braintrust Data, Inc.', 'A hosted eval-and-observability platform aimed at continuously scoring and improving prompts, with logging as one part of a larger eval workflow.', 'Starter (free): $10 in model credits/mo, 1 GB data/mo, 10,000 scores/mo, 14-day retention. Pro: $249/mo, $249 credits, 5 GB, 50,000 scores, 30-day retention. Enterprise: custom, custom retention. Usage-based overages on data/scores/tokens. (Verified braintrust.dev/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["Unlimited users, projects, datasets and experiments even on the free Starter plan.", "Strongest eval-scoring workflow of the hosted options researched, useful if agent quality (not just cost) becomes something to actively test.", "Pay-as-you-scale usage pricing rather than a hard per-seat wall.", "Enterprise tier explicitly offers on-premises or hosted deployment and custom retention."]'::jsonb, '["14-30 day retention on the accessible tiers is short for a multi-year hiring-decision audit trail; that needs the custom Enterprise tier.", "No accessible free self-hosted option — Enterprise-only, unlike Langfuse or Phoenix.", "$249/mo Pro tier is a real recurring cost increase for a feature set (logging + scoring) partly already covered by the free ai_agent_runs table.", "Built around continuous prompt evaluation, which is more machinery than this system currently needs — it''s optimizing for a problem (systematic prompt quality regression) not yet observed here."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick if and when the owner wants to systematically A/B-test and score prompt changes, not just log what happened.', 'Overbuilt for the current need; its eval strength doesn''t offset the lack of an accessible self-host tier or long retention.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://www.braintrust.dev/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "ai_calls", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Braintrust published tiers: Starter (free) $10 model credits/mo, 10,000 scores/mo, 14-day retention; Pro $249/mo, $249 credits, 50,000 scores, 30-day retention (braintrust.dev/pricing).", "caveat": "Enterprise (custom, custom retention) tier and usage-based overages on data/scores/tokens beyond tier limits not modeled.", "tiers": [{"upto": 10000, "monthly": 0}, {"upto": 50000, "monthly": 249}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('ai-observability:langsmith', 'ai-observability', 'LangSmith', 'LangChain, Inc.', 'LangChain''s hosted tracing and evaluation platform for LLM applications, most commonly paired with the LangChain/LangGraph frameworks.', 'Developer (free): up to 5k base traces/mo, 1 seat, pay-as-you-go beyond that. Plus: $39/seat/mo, unlimited seats. Enterprise: custom, includes self-hosted/hybrid deployment. Extra usage billed via LangChain Compute Units ($1.50 each) and Storage Units ($1.00 each). (Verified langchain.com/pricing, Aug 2026.)', '["Self-hosted/hybrid deployment exists at Enterprise for teams that eventually need data to stay in their own VPC.", "Free tier requires no card and covers a real number of traces before billing starts.", "Cost-tracking is a first-class LangSmith feature (their own docs page is dedicated to it), so per-caller attribution is a supported use case, not an afterthought.", "Widely used, well-documented, low risk of the product disappearing."]'::jsonb, '["Self-hosting is Enterprise-only and custom-priced — no accessible free or mid-tier self-host path.", "Its value proposition is strongest for apps built with LangChain/LangGraph; this codebase uses neither, so a chunk of the integration convenience doesn''t apply.", "LCU/LSU usage-based billing beyond the trace allotment adds a second cost dimension (compute units, storage units) to reason about.", "A second vendor holding request/response content, the same DFARS-adjacent exposure concern as Helicone and Braintrust."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick for a team already standardized on LangChain/LangGraph that wants first-party tracing with minimal setup.', 'Weak fit here — this codebase talks to OpenRouter directly with no LangChain dependency, so LangSmith''s main integration advantage doesn''t apply.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://www.langchain.com/pricing', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "admin_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 39, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Plus plan billed per seat at $39/mo, unlimited seats (langchain.com/pricing); Developer tier is free up to 5,000 base traces/mo, pay-as-you-go beyond.", "caveat": "Extra usage beyond included allotments is billed via LangChain Compute Units ($1.50 each) and Storage Units ($1.00 each), not modeled; the free tier''s trace cap is not reflected in this per-seat model."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('ai-observability:traceloop-openllmetry', 'ai-observability', 'Traceloop / OpenLLMetry', 'Traceloop', 'An Apache-2.0 open-source OpenTelemetry instrumentation library (OpenLLMetry) for LLM calls, paired with Traceloop''s own hosted platform that consumes those traces.', 'OpenLLMetry itself: free, Apache-2.0, self-host and point at any of 25+ compatible observability backends. Traceloop hosted: Free Forever up to 50,000 spans/mo, 5 seats, 24-hour retention. Enterprise: unlimited seats, custom retention, on-prem, SOC2. (Verified traceloop.com/pricing and the OpenLLMetry GitHub repo, Aug 2026.)', '["The instrumentation layer (OpenLLMetry) is free and vendor-neutral — it can feed Traceloop, or any other OpenTelemetry-compatible backend, without lock-in.", "Genuinely portable: if the destination tool changes later, the instrumentation code doesn''t have to.", "Free hosted tier needs no card and covers a real span volume before any cost.", "Marketplace listings on AWS/GCP/Azure simplify procurement if that ever matters."]'::jsonb, '["Traceloop''s free hosted tier''s 24-hour retention is unusable for any audit/governance purpose — real retention needs the custom Enterprise tier.", "Adopting OpenLLMetry means instrumenting the codebase with OpenTelemetry spans, real engineering work with no immediate payoff over the existing table.", "Enterprise pricing is opaque, sales-quote only.", "Yet another vendor with a copy of trace content, unless it''s used purely as a from-scratch self-hosted OTel collector, which adds its own operational burden."]'::jsonb, 'Right pick if the org is standardizing on OpenTelemetry for observability generally, not just for LLM calls, and wants a vendor-neutral instrumentation layer.', 'Interesting for optionality (no lock-in) but adds real engineering effort for a benefit — OTel portability — nobody has asked for yet.', 'land-in-consumer', 'growing', 'https://www.traceloop.com/pricing', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "traces", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 50000, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "OpenLLMetry library itself is free and self-hosted (Apache-2.0, 25+ compatible backends); hosted Traceloop Free Forever tier covers up to 50,000 spans/mo, 5 seats, 24-hour retention (traceloop.com/pricing).", "caveat": "Beyond 50,000 spans/mo requires Enterprise (unlimited seats, custom retention, on-prem, SOC2) — custom-quoted, not priced numerically."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('doc-scanning:poppler-tesseract-current', 'doc-scanning', 'Current pipeline: poppler + tesseract + libreoffice (keep)', 'Open source (poppler, Tesseract/Google, LibreOffice)', 'The existing local-only DFARS scanner: poppler renders/extracts PDF text, Tesseract OCRs image-only pages, LibreOffice normalizes office-doc formats — no network call anywhere in the pipeline.', 'Free. All three components are open source with no license fee.', '["Already built, already self-tested against six fixtures including an image-only scanned modification (per this repo''s own changelog) — it has a track record on THIS problem specifically", "Zero network dependency by construction — the only option in this set with no vendor, no API key, and nothing to disclose in a contract review about where the document went", "Tesseract itself is Apache 2.0, actively maintained (originally HP, now Google-sponsored, community-run) — the underlying engine is not a risk even though it''s old"]'::jsonb, '["It''s bespoke glue code the shop wrote and now has to keep working across poppler/tesseract/libreoffice version upgrades on Bill''s machine — real, if small, ongoing maintenance", "Design already had two real bugs found by testing (a length floor discarding short OCR''d files, a density rule flagging short clean contracts) — a reminder that a homegrown pipeline needs its own test discipline going forward", "No layout/table structure extraction beyond what was purpose-built — a general document-AI tool would give richer structured output for other doc-scanning needs beyond DFARS clause detection"]'::jsonb, 'Specifically for DFARS-clause scanning, where "never leaves the machine" is worth more than a marginal accuracy or convenience gain — this is the one job where switching away is the wrong call.', 'Best possible fit for the regulated case: it already satisfies the one property that matters (fully offline) and it already works, tested, on real fixtures.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://tesseractocr.org/', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free — poppler, Tesseract/Google, and LibreOffice are all open source with no license fee.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('doc-scanning:ocrmypdf', 'doc-scanning', 'OCRmyPDF', 'Open source (ocrmypdf project, MPL-2.0)', 'A maintained, widely-used open-source tool that adds a searchable OCR text layer to scanned PDFs, built on the same Tesseract + Ghostscript stack as the current pipeline.', 'Free, MPL-2.0 licensed.', '["Would let the shop retire some of the custom glue code in the current pipeline in favor of a maintained project with its own test suite and release history", "Same core dependency (Tesseract) as the pipeline already in use, so the residency story doesn''t change — still fully local", "Cross-platform packaging (apt, brew, dnf, Docker) means it''s easy to pin a known-good version rather than hand-maintaining version compatibility"]'::jsonb, '["It solves the OCR-layer problem, not the DFARS-clause-detection problem — it would need to sit alongside, not replace, the shop''s own clause-scanning logic", "MPL-2.0 requires publishing source-level modifications made TO OCRmyPDF itself if it''s forked/modified — a mild constraint, though using it unmodified as a CLI avoids the question entirely", "Still depends on external Ghostscript and Tesseract installs, so it doesn''t remove any of the current pipeline''s install-and-maintain surface, just reorganizes it"]'::jsonb, 'As a drop-in replacement for the "add OCR layer to a scanned PDF" step specifically, if the hand-rolled version of that step is the part causing the most maintenance pain.', 'Good fit as a component swap — same offline guarantee as today, less custom code to maintain — but it''s an incremental improvement to the existing approach, not a different one.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://github.com/ocrmypdf/OCRmyPDF', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free, MPL-2.0 licensed.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('doc-scanning:docling', 'doc-scanning', 'Docling', 'IBM Research (open source, MIT)', 'A modern, open-source document-conversion library that does layout analysis, reading-order recovery, and table-structure extraction, with pluggable OCR engines (including Tesseract) — runs entirely locally.', 'Free, MIT licensed.', '["MIT license and \"runs 100% locally without forced cloud API dependencies\" per the project''s own site — same offline guarantee as the current pipeline, from IBM Research rather than a hobby project", "Table-structure extraction (TableFormer) and reading-order recovery go well beyond what raw Tesseract output gives — genuinely richer structured output (Markdown/JSON) for anything downstream that needs it", "OCR engine is pluggable — can use Tesseract (matching what''s already installed) or swap to EasyOCR/RapidOCR/NVIDIA Nemotron OCR later without re-architecting"]'::jsonb, '["It''s a Python library to integrate, not a drop-in CLI replacement — more integration work than OCRmyPDF for the same offline guarantee", "Newer and more actively evolving than Tesseract itself — less multi-year field-proven than the engine already in production use here", "Does not itself do DFARS-clause pattern matching — same caveat as OCRmyPDF, it''s a better front-end for the scanning step, not a replacement for the shop''s own logic"]'::jsonb, 'If the shop ever wants structured, table-aware extraction from contracts (e.g., pulling a rate table, not just clause text) while keeping everything local — this is the most capable local option researched here.', 'Strong fit for a future, richer version of the local scanner: same DFARS-safe residency, meaningfully better structure extraction than the current raw-OCR approach.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://docling.org/', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free, MIT licensed.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('doc-scanning:local-vision-llm', 'doc-scanning', 'Local vision-LLM OCR (Qwen2.5-VL / olmOCR via Ollama or LM Studio)', 'Open source (Allen AI olmOCR, Alibaba Qwen; various local-inference tools)', 'The newest approach: run an open-weight vision-language model on Bill''s own machine to read scanned pages directly, instead of a traditional OCR engine.', 'Free (open-weight models); the only cost is local compute (a GPU helps; CPU-only is slow).', '["Confirmed offline end-to-end by a working reference project: \"100% private, offline, and free,\" no cloud API, no subscription", "Vision-LLMs generally handle messy/low-quality scans and unusual layouts better than classic Tesseract OCR — the exact failure mode the DFARS scanner''s fixtures were built to catch", "Can run on \"modest consumer hardware\" per the reference project (single-GPU targets, ~2-4s/page) — not out of reach for a one-person shop''s existing machine"]'::jsonb, '["This is the least field-proven option in the entire set — a hobby-scale GitHub project, not a maintained product with a multi-year track record like Tesseract", "Needs a local LLM server (Ollama/LM Studio/vLLM) running as an additional standing dependency — more moving parts than a CLI tool, in keeping with the general \"local = more ops\" tradeoff seen across all three sets", "Larger/better vision models want a real GPU; without one, throughput and even feasibility degrade significantly — a hardware dependency the current CPU-based pipeline doesn''t have"]'::jsonb, 'As a research spike to see whether a local vision-LLM catches contract-scan failure modes the current Tesseract-based pipeline still misses — not yet a replacement to commit to in production.', 'Interesting future direction for the same offline guarantee with potentially better accuracy on hard scans, but too immature today to replace a pipeline that''s already tested and working.', 'pass-through', 'new', 'https://github.com/ahnafnafee/local-llm-pdf-ocr', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free (open-weight models); the only cost is local compute.", "caveat": "Local compute (a GPU helps; CPU-only is slow) is not priced; assumes existing hardware."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('doc-scanning:azure-doc-intelligence', 'doc-scanning', 'Azure AI Document Intelligence', 'Microsoft', 'Microsoft''s cloud document-OCR/layout/prebuilt-model service, with an optional "disconnected container" deployment aimed at air-gapped environments.', 'Per-1,000-page tiers exist but exact current dollar figures were not visible in the fetched pricing page (shown as placeholders); disconnected containers are billed as annual commitment plans, not pay-as-you-go.', '["The only cloud OCR vendor researched here that publishes an explicit \"disconnected container\" story aimed at exactly this kind of regulated-document problem", "If LFODIE is already an Azure customer, billing and IAM plumbing is one less new vendor relationship", "Prebuilt models exist for structured document types beyond raw OCR (invoices, receipts, ID docs) if the hub ever needs those for non-contract documents"]'::jsonb, '["Even the \"disconnected\" container still runs on a commitment-based annual license and periodically writes usage records that get collected/synced — Microsoft''s own FAQ does not confirm it can run fully air-gapped forever with zero reconnection, which undercuts the one property this whole category is being evaluated on", "Standard (non-disconnected) usage sends full document images to Azure''s cloud — categorically wrong for anything DFARS-scoped, exactly the risk the local pipeline exists to avoid", "Disconnected container licensing requires an actual Azure sales conversation and commitment purchase — not something a solo shop spins up in an afternoon the way it can install Tesseract"]'::jsonb, 'For a much larger, already-Azure-native organization that needs a semi-air-gapped OCR deployment and has the budget/relationship to negotiate a disconnected-container license — not this shop''s shape.', 'Disqualified for the regulated (DFARS) use case on the residency test itself — "disconnected" here means occasionally-connected-for-licensing, not the same guarantee as software that never talks to a network at all. Could be fine for OCR on documents that are NOT DFARS-scoped.', 'pooled', 'mature', 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/containers/disconnected-container-faq', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Per-1,000-page tiers exist but exact dollar figures were not visible on the fetched pricing page (shown as placeholders); disconnected containers use annual commitment plans, not pay-as-you-go.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'chosen', 'Josh 2026-08-05: turns an uploaded resume into plain text. It reads, it does not interpret.', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('doc-scanning:aws-textract', 'doc-scanning', 'AWS Textract', 'Amazon Web Services', 'Amazon''s cloud OCR/document-analysis API — cheap, capable, cloud-only.', '$0.0015/page (Detect Document Text) for the first 1M pages/mo, dropping to $0.0006/page beyond that, per AWS''s own pricing page (US West/Oregon).', '["Cheapest per-page cloud OCR price researched in this set by a wide margin", "No infrastructure to run or maintain — a pure API call, simplest possible integration for non-regulated documents", "Mature, widely used, well-documented, with broad language/format support"]'::jsonb, '["No offline or on-premises deployment option exists at all — confirmed absent from AWS''s own pricing documentation, unlike Azure''s (imperfect) disconnected container story", "Every document sent through it leaves the local machine and is processed on AWS infrastructure — an automatic disqualifier for anything DFARS-scoped, full stop", "Pay-per-page billing needs monitoring/alerting so an unexpected batch (e.g., re-scanning an entire contract archive) doesn''t produce a surprise bill"]'::jsonb, 'For OCR needs that are explicitly NOT contract/CUI-adjacent — e.g., scanning general business paperwork where cloud processing carries no regulatory weight — where its price and simplicity win outright.', 'Disqualified for the DFARS scanner specifically (no offline mode at all); could be a fine, cheap choice for any other non-regulated OCR need the hub picks up later.', 'pooled', 'mature', 'https://aws.amazon.com/textract/pricing/', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "docs", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.0015, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "$0.0015/page (Detect Document Text) for the first 1M pages/month, dropping to $0.0006/page beyond (AWS pricing, US West/Oregon).", "caveat": "Lower per-page rate beyond 1M pages/month not modeled as a tier break; other Textract APIs (Forms/Tables/Queries) cost more per page and are not covered."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('doc-scanning:google-document-ai', 'doc-scanning', 'Google Document AI', 'Google Cloud', 'Google''s cloud document-processing/OCR service, similar in shape and price to Textract.', 'Enterprise Document OCR: $1.50/1,000 pages (0-5M pages/mo), $0.60/1,000 beyond; no OCR-specific free tier, though a general $300 GCP trial credit exists for new accounts.', '["Comparable per-page pricing to Textract, similarly cheap at low-to-moderate volume", "Broad prebuilt-processor catalogue for specific document types beyond generic OCR", "Mature Google Cloud ecosystem if the hub or LFODIE already has GCP infrastructure elsewhere"]'::jsonb, '["No offline or on-premises deployment option found in Google''s own pricing documentation — cloud-only, same disqualifier as Textract for the regulated case", "No OCR-specific free tier — only a generic, time-limited account credit, unlike some of the transactional-email vendors researched in the other set", "Failed-request billing exemptions aside, this is one more cloud vendor relationship/API key to manage for a one-person shop, with no residency upside over Textract to justify picking it specifically"]'::jsonb, 'Only if the hub is already standardized on GCP for other reasons and needs non-regulated OCR at a similar price point to Textract.', 'Same disqualification as Textract for DFARS-scoped work; no differentiator that would make it preferable to Textract for the hub''s non-regulated OCR needs either.', 'pooled', 'mature', 'https://cloud.google.com/document-ai/pricing', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "docs", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.0015, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Enterprise Document OCR: $1.50 per 1,000 pages (0-5M pages/mo), dropping to $0.60/1,000 beyond (Google Cloud pricing).", "caveat": "Lower per-1,000 rate beyond 5M pages/mo not modeled as a tier break; general $300 GCP trial credit not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('doc-scanning:mistral-ocr', 'doc-scanning', 'Mistral OCR', 'Mistral AI', 'A cloud OCR API positioned on state-of-the-art accuracy at commodity pricing, from the French AI lab.', 'OCR 4: $4/1,000 pages via API, $2/1,000 with the batch-API 50% discount; Document AI variant $5/1,000 pages, per Mistral''s own announcement.', '["Aggressively cheap for the accuracy claimed — batch pricing at $2/1,000 pages undercuts Textract/Google at low-to-mid volume once accuracy is weighted in", "Model is \"compact enough to deploy on a single container\" per Mistral, meaning a genuinely local deployment is technically plausible in principle", "Available through multiple cloud marketplaces (SageMaker, Microsoft Foundry, Snowflake) if the hub is already using one of those for other things"]'::jsonb, '["Self-hosted/local deployment is explicitly \"available to enterprise customers\" only, requiring a sales conversation — not attainable for a one-person shop the way Tesseract/Docling are", "The model is not open-weight — there is no path to running it yourself without Mistral''s enterprise program, unlike the local vision-LLM option above", "Standard API usage is cloud-only, so the same DFARS disqualifier applies as Textract/Google/standard Azure usage"]'::jsonb, 'For cheap, high-accuracy OCR on documents that are explicitly not regulated, where the API''s simplicity beats self-hosting anything.', 'Disqualified for the DFARS use case (self-hosting is enterprise-sales-gated, not available at this shop''s scale); could be worth a look for cheap, non-regulated OCR elsewhere given its low batch price.', 'pooled', 'growing', 'https://mistral.ai/news/ocr-4/', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "docs", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.004, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "OCR 4: $4 per 1,000 pages via API (Mistral AI announcement).", "caveat": "$2/1,000 batch-API 50% discount and the $5/1,000-page Document AI variant not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('doc-scanning:llamaparse', 'doc-scanning', 'LlamaParse', 'LlamaIndex, Inc.', 'A cloud document-parsing API tuned for feeding LLM/RAG pipelines, with a credit-based pricing model and an advanced Bring-Your-Own-Cloud self-host option.', 'Credit-based: 1-45 credits/page for parsing depending on mode, $1.25/1,000 credits; 48-hour re-parse caching is free.', '["Purpose-built output format (structured Markdown/JSON) if the hub''s downstream use is feeding an LLM rather than just archiving text", "48-hour parse caching means iterating on the same document during development doesn''t re-cost credits every time", "BYOC self-hosting guides exist for Azure, which is at least a documented (if involved) path toward more control over where documents go"]'::jsonb, '["Primary offering is cloud-first and credit-metered — the default, easiest path is exactly the kind of document-leaves-the-building flow the DFARS scanner exists to avoid", "Self-hosting/BYOC is real infrastructure work (their own docs frame it as enterprise-leaning), not a same-afternoon option the way installing Tesseract is", "Credit pricing (1-60 credits/page across modes) is harder to reason about at a glance than a flat per-page dollar price — needs a calculator, not mental math, before committing"]'::jsonb, 'For LLM/RAG-pipeline document ingestion on non-regulated documents where structured, model-ready output matters more than raw OCR text.', 'Weakest fit of the cloud options for the regulated case specifically — even its self-hosting path is enterprise-oriented rather than something a solo shop can stand up quickly; fine for a different, non-regulated ingestion need if one arises.', 'pooled', 'growing', 'https://developers.llamaindex.ai/llamaparse/general/pricing/', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "docs", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.02875, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Credit-based: 1-45 credits/page depending on parsing mode, at $1.25/1,000 credits; midpoint (~23 credits/page, ~$0.029/page) used as a representative rate.", "caveat": "Actual rate depends heavily on parsing mode chosen (basic vs. premium/agentic); 48-hour re-parse caching (free) not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 80),
  ('ats-integration:bullhorn-rest-direct', 'ats-integration', 'Bullhorn REST API (direct)', 'Bullhorn, Inc.', 'Bullhorn''s own REST API, the ATS with the largest share of the staffing market and the one Bill is most likely to hit first.', 'Free to obtain OAuth keys as a customer for internal use, but any integration offered to more than one customer requires the Bullhorn Partner Program: $5,000/year platform fee, $1,000-$4,000 one-time technical validation fee, plus a 15% revenue share on mutual customers (waived for the first 14). The integrator/partner pays this, not the staffing client, per Bullhorn''s own partner FAQ.', '["Largest installed base among staffing ATSes, so it is the single vendor most worth building well", "Rate limits are explicitly described as anti-abuse, not a revenue lever, and partners get an order-of-magnitude limit increase once revenue-sharing starts", "OAuth keys for single-customer use are obtainable via a normal support ticket, no partner program needed for a one-off"]'::jsonb, '["Reselling the integration to a second client crosses into the $5k/yr + revenue-share Partner Program — a real, recurring cost for a one-owner shop", "Validation is Bullhorn''s own review, not self-serve; timeline and bar are Bullhorn''s to set", "15% revenue share means Bullhorn takes a cut of whatever the registry charges for a Bullhorn-connected job", "Exact production rate-limit numbers are not published anywhere public; you find out at partner validation"]'::jsonb, 'When Bullhorn is confirmed as a specific client''s ATS and the volume justifies owning the integration outright rather than paying a unified-API markup on every call.', 'Good fit for land-in-consumer or pass-through reads if scoped per client — the Integrator never needs to store candidate PII, only broker the call — but the partner program''s revenue share is a business-model decision, not just a technical one, and belongs in front of Bill before the first paying client.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://www.bullhorn.com/bullhorn-partner-program-faqs/', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "tenants", "base_monthly": 416.67, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": true, "setup_usd": 2500, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Bullhorn Partner Program: $5,000/year platform fee (base_monthly = annual/12) plus a $1,000-$4,000 one-time technical validation fee (Bullhorn''s own partner FAQ).", "caveat": "15% revenue share on mutual customers (waived for the first 14) not modeled, as it applies to revenue, not cost; free for single-customer internal use — this pricing applies only when offered to more than one staffing client."}'::jsonb, 'shortlist', 'Josh 2026-08-05 says job listings are pulled from partner ATS APIs on a schedule but names no vendor. Flagged, not decided.', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('ats-integration:unified-to-ats', 'ats-integration', 'Unified.to (unified ATS API)', 'Unified.to', 'A unified API that maps 130+ ATS/HR systems (including Bullhorn, JobDiva-adjacent recruiting tools, Ashby, Workday) to one schema, with real-time pass-through calls rather than a cached copy.', 'Usage-based pricing described by the vendor as scaling with API call volume rather than number of end customers; exact dollar tiers require a quote. The integrator (Bill) is the one who pays Unified.to; whether that cost is passed to the staffing client is a business decision, not something the vendor dictates.', '["Real-time pass-through model — no second copy of candidate data cached in Unified.to''s infrastructure, which matches a pass-through/never-warehouse posture", "131+ ATS integrations claimed including Bullhorn, so one integration can plausibly cover several of Staffility''s clients", "Customers are said to \"own your credentials and migrate anytime,\" reducing lock-in versus a black-box connector", "Usage-based (not per-customer-seat) pricing scales down for a small shop with few clients"]'::jsonb, '["Whether Unified.to itself has passed each underlying vendor''s partner/marketplace approval gate (e.g. Bullhorn''s) is not documented on their public pages — an unresolved dependency, not a guarantee", "Pass-through-only design means no local caching to smooth over a slow or rate-limited underlying ATS — every registry call is only as fast as the slowest link", "A newer, smaller company than Merge; less independently verifiable track record at enterprise scale", "Real dollar pricing not published — must be requested"]'::jsonb, 'When the plan is to onboard several different staffing clients on different ATSes quickly and the priority is speed-to-integration over owning the vendor relationship.', 'Strong fit for the control-plane model specifically because it does not persist a copy of the data — it is closer to a smart proxy than a data store, which matches ''the Integrator is a control plane, not a database.''', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://unified.to/ats', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Usage-based, scaling with API call volume per the vendor''s own description; exact dollar tiers require a direct quote.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('ats-integration:kombo-ats', 'ats-integration', 'Kombo (unified ATS/HRIS API)', 'Kombo', 'A European unified-API vendor covering ATS, HRIS, assessment and LMS integrations under one schema, priced as a flat platform fee plus a per-connected-customer fee rather than per API call.', 'Fixed annual platform fee (tier-dependent: Start/Scale/Enterprise) plus a variable per-end-customer fee, billed monthly and only while that customer''s connection is active — deactivating a client''s connection stops the charge for that connection. The integrator pays Kombo directly.', '["Unlimited API calls per connection — no volume-based surprise bill, which matters for a nightly sync pattern", "Per-customer fee stops the moment a client''s connection is deactivated, so a churned Staffility client doesn''t keep costing money", "Explicitly covers ATS as a named integration category, not just HRIS", "Predictable cost curve for a small number of clients versus a usage-metered vendor"]'::jsonb, '["Per-connected-customer pricing means cost scales directly with how many staffing clients get connected — a very different curve than usage-based Unified.to", "Kombo is EU-headquartered and its ATS catalogue skews toward European ATS/HR platforms; US staffing-specific coverage (Bullhorn, JobDiva, Ceipal, Avionté) needs to be confirmed per vendor before committing", "No published flat-fee dollar figures — both the platform fee and per-customer fee require a quote", "Smaller and less US-market-proven than Merge for staffing specifically"]'::jsonb, 'When the client roster is expected to be small and stable (a handful of named staffing clients) and predictable per-client cost matters more than raw API-call economics.', 'Reasonable fit for land-in-consumer or pass-through depending on Kombo''s own architecture per connector; needs a direct question to Kombo about whether US staffing ATSes (vs. European ATSes) are actually in their catalogue before relying on it.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://www.kombo.dev/pricing', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Fixed annual platform fee (tier-dependent: Start/Scale/Enterprise) plus a variable per-connected-customer fee; neither is a published number in the reviewed material.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('ats-integration:merge-ats', 'ats-integration', 'Merge ATS & Recruiting API', 'Merge, Inc.', 'The most established unified-API vendor for ATS/recruiting data, mapping many ATSes (including Bullhorn, JobDiva, Ceipal, Greenhouse) to one schema with a published entry-level price.', 'Launch plan: first 3 production "Linked Accounts" free, then $650/month covers up to 10 total production Linked Accounts, $65/linked account after that. Professional and Enterprise tiers are custom-quoted. The integrator pays Merge; whether that flows through to the staffing client is Bill''s call.', '["Published starting price ($650/mo for up to 10 linked accounts) — the only ATS-integration option in this set with a transparent public number, useful for budgeting before a sales call", "Widely used and referenced across the integration-tooling ecosystem, meaning more example code and prior art than smaller competitors", "Covers Bullhorn, JobDiva, Ceipal and other staffing-specific ATSes by name in its integration directory", "\"Linked Account\" pricing model maps cleanly onto \"one staffing client = one linked account,\" which is easy for a non-developer owner to reason about"]'::jsonb, '["$650/month floor for up to 10 accounts is real money against a $7/mo Render bill and a one-owner shop — this is the most expensive unified option in dollar terms at low client counts", "A \"Linked Account\" is Merge''s data-syncing model, which by default pulls and stores a normalized copy on Merge''s side — the opposite of pass-through, and needs an explicit architecture check against ''never warehouse client candidate data''", "Professional/Enterprise pricing (likely needed once past 10 accounts) is not published — could jump sharply", "Does not remove the need for the underlying ATS vendor''s own partner approval in some cases — Merge is validated with vendors, but coverage depth per ATS varies"]'::jsonb, 'When budget supports $650+/month and the priority is proven reliability and broad documentation over minimizing per-client cost — e.g., once Staffility has multiple ATS-diverse clients confirmed.', 'Needs a direct architecture check: Merge''s normal model syncs and caches a copy of ATS data on Merge''s infrastructure, which conflicts with a pass-through/no-custody posture unless configured for real-time proxy calls only — confirm before committing candidate data to it.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://www.merge.dev/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "tenants", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 65, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Merge Launch plan: free for the first 3 production Linked Accounts, then $650/month for up to 10 total, $65/account after.", "caveat": "Professional/Enterprise tiers are custom-quoted and not modeled.", "tiers": [{"upto": 3, "monthly": 0}, {"upto": 10, "monthly": 650}, {"upto": null, "monthly": 0}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('ats-integration:crelate-api-direct', 'ats-integration', 'Crelate public API (direct)', 'Crelate, Inc.', 'Crelate''s own REST/public API, documented via Postman, aimed at a mid-market/small-agency ATS user base rather than enterprise staffing.', 'Not published in Crelate''s help center — access requirements (whether it''s included in the base subscription or a paid tier) require contacting Crelate support directly.', '["Crelate is explicitly positioned as a Bullhorn-migration target for smaller agencies, so if a Staffility client is on Crelate it is likely a smaller, simpler integration than Bullhorn''s partner program", "Public API documentation is openly hosted on Postman rather than gated behind a developer portal login", "Growing user base among small-to-midsize staffing agencies, a plausible profile for some Staffility clients"]'::jsonb, '["No published information on rate limits, approval gates, or whether API access is a paid add-on — this is an open question, not a known-good", "Smaller ecosystem than Bullhorn/JobDiva means fewer third-party examples and less community troubleshooting", "Whether Crelate''s own terms allow reselling an integration to multiple end customers (a partner-program question) is undocumented publicly"]'::jsonb, 'When a specific Staffility client is confirmed on Crelate and the volume is low enough that a direct, unmediated integration is worth the setup cost.', 'Unknown until Crelate support is contacted directly — do not commit a client-facing timeline against this option before that call.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://help.crelate.com/en/collections/2434134-api-documentation', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Not published in Crelate''s help center — access requirements (base subscription vs. paid tier) require contacting Crelate support directly.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('ats-integration:loxo-api-direct', 'ats-integration', 'Loxo Open API (direct)', 'Loxo, Inc.', 'Loxo''s own REST API for its all-in-one recruiting CRM/ATS platform, explicitly sold as a paid add-on feature.', 'Confirmed paid feature: ''Access to our Open API is a paid feature. If you do not have an active subscription with us, please schedule a demo.'' The staffing client (Loxo''s own subscriber) is the one who must have this add-on active — the cost sits with the client''s Loxo contract, not the Integrator.', '["Explicit, unambiguous statement that API access is paid — no guessing about who bears the cost, unlike several competitors in this set", "Only account admins can generate keys, which is a clean, auditable access-control model", "A separate Developer Program exists for third-party/custom integrations beyond the in-app list, a real (if support-ticket-gated) path to production"]'::jsonb, '["The staffing client must already be paying for the Loxo Open API add-on before the Integrator can connect — a dependency outside Bill''s control per client", "No published rate limits found in the public help article", "Being a paid tier gate (versus baked into every plan) means some Staffility-referred clients on Loxo simply won''t have API access without an upsell conversation with Loxo first"]'::jsonb, 'When a specific client is already on Loxo''s Open API add-on — otherwise this becomes a sales blocker before it''s an engineering one.', 'Fine for pass-through once the add-on is active; the residency question is moot if the client hasn''t purchased API access at all.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://help.loxo.co/en/articles/446640-loxo-s-open-api', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Confirmed to be a paid add-on requiring an active subscription, but no dollar figure is published; the cost sits with the staffing client''s own Loxo contract, not the Integrator.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('ats-integration:ceipal-api-direct', 'ats-integration', 'Ceipal ATS API v2 (direct)', 'Ceipal Corp', 'Ceipal''s REST API v2, documented with a formal reference and an explicit token-based rate-limiting scheme.', 'Not published — Ceipal requires contacting an account representative to obtain API credentials, and pricing/inclusion in the base subscription is not stated in the public developer docs.', '["Rate limiting is documented precisely: per-user-token, 429 response on limit exceeded — a known, engineerable behavior rather than an unknown", "Formal versioned API reference (v2) suggests an intentionally maintained developer surface, not a bolted-on afterthought", "Growing presence among mid-market staffing agencies as a lower-cost Bullhorn alternative"]'::jsonb, '["Access requires going through an account representative per client — an added coordination step with no public self-serve path", "No published rate-limit numbers (requests per minute/hour), only that a 429 is returned — nightly-sync feasibility can''t be assessed without asking Ceipal directly", "Pricing for API access itself (included vs. add-on) is undocumented publicly"]'::jsonb, 'When a Staffility client is confirmed on Ceipal and the relationship with a Ceipal account rep can be established per client.', 'Workable for pass-through once credentials are issued; the per-token rate limit design is reasonable for scoped, per-client API keys.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://developer.ceipal.com/ceipal-ats-v2/ceipal-ats-v2-api-reference', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Not published — Ceipal requires contacting an account representative to obtain API credentials; pricing/inclusion in the base subscription is not stated in public docs.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('ats-integration:avionte-api-direct', 'ats-integration', 'Avionté BOLD API (direct)', 'Avionté Staffing Software', 'Avionté''s API surface for its BOLD platform, gated through a formal ''Certified Integration Program'' for third parties.', 'Not published for third-party/partner access. Avionté clients themselves must request ''subscription information'' from their Avionté rep to enable API access — suggesting it is a paid or negotiated add-on, but the public docs don''t state a figure.', '["A named, documented onboarding path exists (''Step 1: Get access to Avionté APIs'') rather than an ad hoc support-ticket process", "Once approved, a clear credential set (Tenant ID, Client ID, Client Secret, API Key) is issued, a standard OAuth-style flow", "Certified Integration Program implies Avionté has an established review process, reducing the risk of building against an API that changes without notice"]'::jsonb, '["Two-track gate: the staffing client must separately request ''subscription options'' AND a third-party integrator must separately apply to the Certified Integration Program — twice the coordination of a single-vendor flow", "No public rate limits documented — feasibility of a nightly sync is unverified", "No pricing transparency at all for either the client-side subscription or the partner-side certification"]'::jsonb, 'When a specific client is confirmed on Avionté BOLD and there is time to run both the client subscription conversation and the Avionté certification process in parallel.', 'Cannot be assessed for residency fit until both approval tracks are cleared and real rate limits are known — treat as an unresolved item, not a ready-to-build option.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://developer.avionte.com/docs/step-1-get-access-to-avionte-apis', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Not published for third-party/partner access — clients must request ''subscription information'' from their Avionté rep, suggesting a paid or negotiated add-on with no stated figure.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('ats-integration:jobdiva-api-direct', 'ats-integration', 'JobDiva API (direct)', 'JobDiva, Inc.', 'JobDiva''s own SOAP/REST hybrid API for its ATS/VMS platform, one of the older and more established systems in US staffing.', 'Not published anywhere in JobDiva''s public-facing pages found during this research; access process, cost, and any partner requirement could not be confirmed from a primary source.', '["JobDiva is a long-established, widely-used ATS/VMS in US staffing, so client demand for this connector is plausible", "A dedicated API landing page and sandbox environment exist, suggesting there is a real developer path even if not documented in depth publicly", "Multiple third-party unified-API vendors (Merge, Unified.to, Knit) already list JobDiva as a supported integration, indirect evidence that production API access is obtainable"]'::jsonb, '["No primary-source documentation on pricing, rate limits, or approval process was found — this entire option rests on secondhand evidence from unified-API vendor pages, not JobDiva''s own docs", "JobDiva''s API is older and reportedly mixes SOAP and REST patterns in places, which can mean more integration effort than a modern REST-only API", "Cannot verify who pays for API access (JobDiva customer vs. integrator) without a direct sales conversation"]'::jsonb, 'Only after a direct conversation with JobDiva confirms cost, approval process and rate limits — do not scope client work against this option on the current public evidence.', 'Unknown — flagged for verification rather than assumed.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://api.jobdiva.com/jobdiva-api.html', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Not published anywhere in JobDiva''s public-facing pages found during this research; access process, cost, and any partner requirement could not be confirmed.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 80),
  ('m365-ingest:graph-direct', 'm365-ingest', 'Microsoft Graph API (direct)', 'Microsoft', 'Calling Microsoft Graph directly with application permissions, delta queries for incremental sync, and change-notification webhooks for near-real-time updates — the same pattern the Integrator already uses for its own API design.', 'No separate product cost — Graph API calls are included with the M365/Entra ID tenant subscription already being paid for. All cost is the Integrator''s own engineering time to build and maintain the sync logic.', '["Zero additional vendor cost beyond the M365 licenses already in place", "graph.microsoft.us exists as a dedicated GCC High endpoint — this is the only realistic path for the IPTA tenant if it''s on GCC High, since third-party SaaS ETL tools generally aren''t", "Delta query is Microsoft''s own documented mechanism for incremental sync (avoids re-pulling everything every run)", "Full control over exactly what is stored where, matching the two-tier residency model (pass-through vs. land-in-consumer vs. pooled) per data type"]'::jsonb, '["Three separate tenants means three separate app registrations and three separate admin-consent ceremonies — a recurring one-owner burden, not a one-time cost", "Global throttling limit is 130,000 requests/10 seconds per app across ALL tenants combined, and Teams-specific limits are much tighter (e.g., 10,000 presence requests/30s per app per tenant) — a naive standing sync across three tenants plus growth needs real backoff logic", "Microsoft''s own docs warn that ''certain services and features... might not be available in all national clouds'' — GCC High is not guaranteed to have 1:1 feature parity with commercial Graph, and gaps have historically lagged by months to years (e.g., the To Do API only recently reached GCC High/DoD)", "All the engineering burden (auth, webhooks, retry/backoff, delta cursor storage) is the Integrator''s to build and maintain — there is no vendor SLA to lean on"]'::jsonb, 'The default choice for any tenant where GCC High is in play (IPTA) or where the Integrator wants to keep full control of what data ever leaves M365, matching the existing control-plane design.', 'Best fit of all options: it''s the only one that can serve the IPTA/GCC High tenant at all, and it lets each data type be routed pass-through/land-in-consumer/pooled exactly as designed rather than inheriting a vendor''s default (usually land-and-cache) behavior.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/deployments', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "No separate product cost — Graph API calls are included with the M365/Entra ID tenant subscription already being paid for.", "caveat": "All cost is the Integrator''s own engineering time to build/maintain the sync logic; assumes the M365 subscription is already paid for separately."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('m365-ingest:graph-data-connect', 'm365-ingest', 'Microsoft Graph Data Connect (MGDC)', 'Microsoft', 'Microsoft''s own bulk-extraction pipeline product for pulling large-scale, tenant-wide datasets (mail metadata, Teams, Entra ID, SharePoint/O365, Viva Insights) into an Azure data sink, rather than calling Graph endpoints one record at a time.', 'Billed per pipeline run, rounded up to whole units regardless of row count (e.g., a 500-row-per-run pipeline run 20 times bills as 20 units even if total rows were far less) — a metered Azure cost on top of the M365 subscription, separate from Graph API calls which are free.', '["Purpose-built for exactly ''bulk access to at-scale Microsoft Graph datasets,'' which is the stated use case here (mail, calendar, files, Teams, org data across whole tenants)", "Offers ''Cleaned'' (deduplicated/normalized) dataset tiers for SharePoint/O365, reducing some of the ETL work the Integrator would otherwise write by hand", "Native Microsoft product, so no third-party vendor relationship or additional data processor to vet"]'::jsonb, '["Confirmed explicitly in Microsoft''s own docs: MGDC ''does not support any national cloud deployments'' — meaning it is unusable for a GCC High IPTA tenant, the exact tenant with the regulatory stakes", "Billing model penalizes small, frequent syncs (each run rounds up to a full unit) — awkward for a lean, low-volume three-tenant setup versus a big-batch enterprise use case it''s really designed for", "Requires standing up an Azure Data Factory-style pipeline and sink, real infrastructure to build and operate beyond what the Integrator''s Express/Postgres stack already runs", "Regional availability is limited to specific geos (North America, Europe, Asia Pacific, UK, Australia) — fine for these tenants today, but another constraint to track"]'::jsonb, 'Only worth it for the two commercial tenants (LFODIE, Staffility) if and when the sync volume becomes large enough that per-record Graph calls become impractical — not a near-term pick for a three-tenant pilot.', 'Poor fit today: it adds infrastructure and cost for a scale problem the Integrator doesn''t have yet, and it is categorically unavailable for the one tenant (IPTA/GCC High) where a bulk-extraction shortcut would matter most.', 'pooled', 'mature', 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/data-connect-faq?WT.mc_id=M365-MVP-5001530', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Billed per pipeline run (rounded up to whole units regardless of row count) as a metered Azure cost on top of the M365 subscription; no per-unit dollar figure was found in the reviewed material.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('m365-ingest:graph-in-fabric', 'm365-ingest', 'Graph (database) in Microsoft Fabric', 'Microsoft', 'A graph-database/analytics feature inside Microsoft Fabric for modeling relationships in data already sitting in OneLake, using GQL — this is NOT a connector to Microsoft 365 mail/calendar/Teams data despite the name.', 'Part of Microsoft Fabric capacity pricing (consumption-based compute units); not evaluated in depth here because it doesn''t do what this decision needs.', '["Confirmed directly from Microsoft''s own docs to be a real, generally-available Fabric capability, not vaporware", "Could be useful much later for relationship analytics (e.g., who-emails-whom patterns) once M365 data is already landed in OneLake by some other means"]'::jsonb, '["This is the single most important finding in this set: ''Graph in Fabric'' is a graph *database* for data relationships, unrelated to Microsoft *Graph API* which is the actual M365 data-access layer — confirmed directly against Microsoft''s own Fabric docs", "It does not natively ingest M365 data at all; M365 data would first need to be extracted via Graph API (or MGDC) and loaded into OneLake before Graph-in-Fabric could touch it", "Including this as an ''ingest'' option in planning would be a naming-driven mistake, not a real architecture choice"]'::jsonb, 'Not applicable to this decision. Only relevant far downstream, and only after M365 data has already been ingested by one of the other options in this set.', 'No fit for M365 ingest. Flagged here specifically so the name doesn''t get mistaken for a Graph-API ingestion product in a later planning conversation.', 'n/a', 'new', 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/graph/overview', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Part of Microsoft Fabric capacity pricing (consumption-based compute units); not evaluated in depth and no dollar figure obtained, since this feature is not actually an M365-connector — it operates on data already in OneLake.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('m365-ingest:fivetran-m365', 'm365-ingest', 'Fivetran (Microsoft Teams / SharePoint / OneDrive connectors)', 'Fivetran, Inc.', 'A managed, no-code ETL platform with dedicated connectors for Microsoft Teams, SharePoint and OneDrive, syncing into a destination warehouse on a consumption-based (Monthly Active Rows) pricing model.', 'Usage-based on Monthly Active Rows (MAR): a free tier covers 500,000 MAR; paid usage has a $5 minimum per connection between 1 and 1M MAR, with the per-row rate declining as volume grows. A comparable low-volume connector (Google Analytics 4) was quoted around $11/month in Fivetran''s own example — Teams/SharePoint volumes would need Fivetran''s estimator to size precisely.', '["Free tier (500,000 MAR) may be enough to pilot one tenant''s Teams or SharePoint sync at effectively no cost", "Fully managed — no pipeline infrastructure to run, unlike Graph Data Connect or a self-hosted Airbyte instance", "Purpose-built connectors exist today for Teams, SharePoint and OneDrive specifically, not just a generic Graph passthrough", "Mature, widely-used platform with strong documentation and support"]'::jsonb, '["No GCC High or government-cloud support found anywhere in Fivetran''s public docs or press materials — its enterprise deployment options (AWS GovCloud, hybrid) are about *where Fivetran''s own infrastructure runs*, not about connecting to a GCC High Entra ID tenant, and this needs a direct confirmation before ruling it in or out for IPTA", "Connector-level documentation does not disclose whether Graph delta queries are used internally, or what admin-consent application permissions are required — opaque compared to building on Graph directly", "By design, this lands a full copy of tenant data into a destination warehouse — a ''pooled'' architecture that runs counter to a pass-through-by-default residency posture unless deliberately scoped", "MAR-based pricing can become unpredictable as tenant activity grows, unlike a flat platform fee"]'::jsonb, 'For the two commercial tenants (LFODIE, Staffility) if the plan shifts to warehousing M365 data for analytics rather than live-fetching it — not for IPTA unless GCC High support is separately confirmed.', 'Weak fit for the control-plane''s default pass-through posture (it lands data by design) and unconfirmed/likely absent for the GCC High tenant where fit matters most.', 'pooled', 'mature', 'https://www.fivetran.com/pricing', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "connections", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 1, "per_unit": 5, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Free tier covers up to 500,000 Monthly Active Rows; paid usage carries a $5/mo minimum per connection (1-1M MAR), with a comparable low-volume connector (Google Analytics 4) quoted around $11/mo in Fivetran''s own example.", "caveat": "Exact MAR-based rate for Teams/SharePoint specifically requires Fivetran''s estimator; the $5 minimum is used as a floor, not the expected bill."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('m365-ingest:airbyte-m365', 'm365-ingest', 'Airbyte (Microsoft SharePoint / Teams sources)', 'Airbyte, Inc.', 'An open-source ETL platform with self-hostable Microsoft SharePoint and Teams source connectors, authenticating via standard Graph application permissions (e.g. Files.Read.All).', 'Free and self-hostable as open source; Airbyte Cloud (managed) pricing separately starts around $10/month per third-party reporting, scaling with usage — self-hosting avoids that fee entirely at the cost of running the infrastructure yourself.', '["Open source and self-hostable — for a one-owner shop already running its own Render/Postgres stack, this is the only ETL option in this set with a real $0 licensing path", "Confirmed to use standard Microsoft Graph application permissions (Files.Read.All) and standard Entra ID app registration (client ID, tenant ID, client secret) — the same auth model the Integrator would use calling Graph directly", "Transparent, inspectable connector source code (it''s open source), useful for verifying exactly what data it touches before trusting it with any tenant''s data"]'::jsonb, '["No mention of GCC High or sovereign/government cloud support in the connector docs — like Fivetran, this needs a direct test or vendor confirmation before considering it for IPTA", "Self-hosting means the Integrator owns uptime, upgrades and scaling for an additional service, on top of the Express/Postgres/Render stack already being operated by one person", "By design, an ETL sync lands (copies) SharePoint/Teams content into a destination, again a ''pooled'' architecture unless deliberately restricted to metadata-only fields", "Managed Airbyte Cloud, if chosen instead of self-hosting to reduce operational burden, reintroduces a third-party vendor holding a copy of tenant data"]'::jsonb, 'For a technically comfortable owner who wants zero licensing cost and is willing to self-host, on the two commercial tenants, as a stepping stone before hand-building the equivalent against Graph directly.', 'Same residency mismatch as Fivetran (lands data by default) plus added operational burden for a one-owner shop; open-source transparency is a genuine plus if self-hosting is acceptable.', 'pooled', 'growing', 'https://docs.airbyte.com/integrations/sources/microsoft-sharepoint', '{"kind": "byo", "driver": "connections", "base_monthly": 7, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Free and self-hostable as open source, authenticating via standard Graph application permissions; Airbyte Cloud managed alternative starts around $10/month per third-party reporting, scaling with usage.", "caveat": "base_monthly is a placeholder self-host VM cost; Airbyte Cloud''s actual scaling rate beyond its ~$10/mo starting point is not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('m365-ingest:cdata-sync-m365', 'm365-ingest', 'CData Sync (Office 365 connectors)', 'CData Software', 'A commercial data-replication product with Office 365 drivers/connectors, sold as an annual license across cloud, self-hosted-cloud, and on-premises deployment options.', 'Standard license starts at $7,999/year for up to 5 standard connections and 100M rows/month (cloud, self-hosted-cloud, or on-premises all priced the same at the Standard tier); hourly marketplace pricing also exists ($2.74/hr Professional, $4.87/hr Enterprise on AWS/Azure).', '["Self-hosted / on-premises deployment option exists, which matters if a future compliance boundary requires the sync engine to run somewhere Bill controls directly", "Mature, established connector product with dedicated Office 365 drivers, not a general-purpose scraper", "Same license price across deployment models, so moving from cloud to on-prem later isn''t a repricing event"]'::jsonb, '["At $7,999/year minimum, this is by far the most expensive option in this entire set for a one-owner shop paying $7/month for its API host — the price alone likely rules it out", "No GCC High or government-cloud statement found in CData''s public pricing or product pages — unconfirmed for the IPTA tenant", "Designed for broad multi-source data replication (100+ connectors), most of which is irrelevant capability the Integrator would be paying for", "Still a ''land the data somewhere'' replication tool by design, same residency mismatch as Fivetran/Airbyte"]'::jsonb, 'Not recommended at this shop''s scale; would only make sense if the Integrator were replicating dozens of heterogeneous sources and Office 365 were just one of many, which is not the current shape of the problem.', 'Poor fit on cost alone before residency or GCC High questions are even considered.', 'pooled', 'mature', 'https://www.cdata.com/sync/pricing/', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "connections", "base_monthly": 666.58, "included": 5, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": true, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Standard license: $7,999/year for up to 5 standard connections and 100M rows/month, priced the same across cloud/self-hosted-cloud/on-premises.", "caveat": "Hourly marketplace alternative ($2.74/hr Professional, $4.87/hr Enterprise on AWS/Azure) not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('m365-ingest:nango-m365', 'm365-ingest', 'Nango (Microsoft/Outlook proxy integrations)', 'Nango', 'A developer-first integration platform (not a unified-schema API) offering raw-passthrough proxy access to hundreds of APIs including Microsoft/Outlook, priced by connections and compute usage.', 'Free tier at $0/month for testing; Starter from $50/month; Growth from $500/month; Enterprise custom. Usage-based line items for connections, proxy requests, compute time, sync storage and webhooks.', '["Raw-passthrough model (not a forced unified schema) means the Integrator keeps direct control over exactly which Graph fields it requests, closer to calling Graph directly than a black-box unified API", "Low-cost entry tier ($0 to prove the pattern, $50/mo to go further) fits a lean shop''s budget far better than Fivetran/CData/Merge-scale pricing", "Handles the OAuth/token-refresh plumbing for Microsoft integrations, removing some (not all) of the auth boilerplate the Integrator would otherwise write itself"]'::jsonb, '["Public documentation found during this research did not confirm which specific M365 surfaces (mail, calendar, Teams, SharePoint) are supported as pre-built Nango integrations versus requiring custom proxy configuration — needs a direct check against Nango''s own integration catalogue for ''Microsoft''/''Outlook'' before relying on it", "No GCC High or government-cloud mention found; unconfirmed for IPTA", "As a third party sitting between the Integrator and Graph, it is one more vendor holding OAuth tokens for three M365 tenants, a broader trust surface than calling Graph directly", "Smaller, less enterprise-proven than Fivetran or Merge specifically for M365 workloads"]'::jsonb, 'Worth a scoped trial for the two commercial tenants if the goal is to offload OAuth/token-refresh plumbing cheaply, before deciding whether to build the equivalent directly against Graph.', 'Middling fit — cheaper and more control-preserving than the warehouse-style ETL tools, but introduces a third party holding M365 credentials, which needs weighing against building directly on Graph for a system whose whole premise is being the one controlled doorway.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://nango.dev/pricing', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "connections", "base_monthly": 50, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free tier at $0/month for testing; Starter from $50/month; Growth from $500/month (this entry''s own pricing shape).", "caveat": "Usage-based line items for connections, proxy requests, compute time, sync storage and webhooks are not broken into per-unit rates in the reviewed material; Enterprise tier is custom."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('m365-ingest:apideck-filestorage-m365', 'm365-ingest', 'Apideck Unified File Storage API (SharePoint/OneDrive only)', 'Apideck', 'A unified File Storage API covering SharePoint and OneDrive drives/files/folders — deliberately narrow, covering only the files slice of M365, not mail, calendar, or Teams.', 'Not published on the pages reviewed; requires a direct quote.', '["Purpose-built unified schema specifically for file storage, which could simplify the SharePoint/OneDrive slice of the ingest problem if mail/calendar/Teams are handled separately", "Part of a broader Apideck unified-API suite, so if the Integrator later adopts Apideck for other categories (CRM, accounting) there may be integration-pattern reuse"]'::jsonb, '["Confirmed narrow scope: covers only files (drives, files, folders, upload sessions) — mail, calendar and Teams are explicitly not part of this product, so it can only ever be a partial answer to ''mail, calendar, files, Teams and org data''", "Pricing not published anywhere found, and no GCC High statement — both unverified", "Adds a second and third vendor relationship (one for files, others needed for mail/calendar/Teams) rather than one coherent ingest strategy — likely more integration complexity overall than calling Graph directly for everything"]'::jsonb, 'Only as a narrow, files-only stopgap if SharePoint/OneDrive access is needed faster than the rest of the M365 surface — not a standalone answer to this decision.', 'Poor fit as a primary option because it cannot cover mail/calendar/Teams/org data at all; would need to be paired with something else, which defeats the point of picking one vendor to simplify the problem.', 'pooled', 'growing', 'https://developers.apideck.com/apis/file-storage/sharepoint', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Not published on the pages reviewed; requires a direct quote.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('unified-api:nango', 'unified-api', 'Nango', 'Nango (Nango Inc.)', 'An open-source unified-API / integration platform: OAuth handling, a credential proxy, and optional data syncs across hundreds of SaaS APIs.', 'Free tier: $0/month, 10 connections. Starter: from $50/month, 20 connections then $1/connection. Growth: from $500/month, 100 connections then $1/connection. Enterprise: custom. Confirmed on nango.dev/pricing.', '["Genuinely open source (11.5k+ GitHub stars) with a real self-hosting path — ''run Nango in your own infrastructure with our support'', matching the small-shop preference to own critical infra where practical", "Free tier (10 connections) is enough to prove the pattern on 1-2 real integrations before spending anything", "Per-connection pricing model reads directly as rows in a config table — connections map naturally onto this project''s grant/connection model", "Its proxy design keeps secrets off the hub''s own servers by design, per its own docs, while still giving full traffic visibility"]'::jsonb, '["Self-hosting Nango is itself a service to operate (its own database, its own uptime) — the same ''daemon'' cost this project wants to avoid unless the hosted free/Starter tier is used instead", "$1/connection overage past the included count adds up quickly once several consumer companies each bring several client connections", "Newer/smaller company than Merge or Workato — some vendor-durability risk", "''Connections'' pricing can get expensive fast in a genuinely multi-tenant, multi-client model (the exact shape this project is building toward)"]'::jsonb, 'When the near-term need is 2-10 real integrations and the owner wants an option that can later move in-house without a rewrite, thanks to the open-source path.', 'The best philosophical fit of this set: free to start, transparent per-connection cost that maps onto rows, and a genuine self-host escape hatch if vendor risk ever needs to be eliminated.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://www.nango.dev/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "connections", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 1, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Nango''s published tiers: Free $0/mo (10 connections), Starter from $50/mo (20 connections), Growth from $500/mo (100 connections) (nango.dev/pricing).", "caveat": "Per-connection overage beyond each tier ($1/connection) and the custom Enterprise tier not modeled.", "tiers": [{"upto": 10, "monthly": 0}, {"upto": 20, "monthly": 50}, {"upto": 100, "monthly": 500}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('unified-api:unified-to', 'unified-api', 'Unified.to', 'Unified.to', 'A unified API covering many SaaS categories (CRM, HRIS, ATS, accounting, ticketing, and more), priced by API call volume rather than per connection.', 'Grow: $750+/month, 750,000 calls/month, $1.00/1,000 overage. Pro: $1,500+/month, 2,000,000 calls, $0.75/1,000 overage. Scale: $3,000+/month, 6,000,000 calls, $0.50/1,000 overage. Enterprise: custom. All paid tiers include unlimited customers and connections. Confirmed on unified.to/pricing, including a 30-day free trial.', '["Unlimited connections/customers on every paid tier means the pricing model doesn''t punish adding more consumer companies or clients the way per-connection pricing can", "30-day free trial gives real evaluation time before the $750/month floor kicks in", "Volume-based pricing is at least predictable once real call patterns are known", "Broad category coverage similar to Merge/Apideck"]'::jsonb, '["$750/month minimum is a serious commitment for a one-person shop''s current stage — roughly 100x the Render API hosting cost already budgeted", "During testing you can use Unified.to''s own OAuth credentials for some integrations, but going live requires switching to the hub''s own app credentials — an extra setup step per integration", "Secrets Manager / credential storage language on the pricing page suggests Unified.to holds credentials on its own infrastructure by default, which needs scrutiny against the ''no custody'' rule for anything regulated-adjacent", "No published free tier below the 30-day trial — cost starts immediately after"]'::jsonb, 'Once there are enough live integrations across enough consumer apps that a flat, unlimited-connections price beats per-connection billing — not yet, at this project''s current scale.', 'Workable as a land-in-consumer layer, but the $750/month floor is hard to justify before there''s real multi-app usage, and credential custody needs a direct question to the vendor before trusting it with anything regulated.', 'land-in-consumer', 'growing', 'https://unified.to/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "api_calls", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Unified.to''s published tiers: Grow $750+/mo (750,000 calls), Pro $1,500+/mo (2,000,000 calls), Scale $3,000+/mo (6,000,000 calls) (unified.to/pricing).", "caveat": "Enterprise tier is custom-quoted; overage rates ($1.00/$0.75/$0.50 per 1,000 calls beyond each tier) not modeled as per_unit; all tiers include unlimited customers/connections.", "tiers": [{"upto": 750000, "monthly": 750}, {"upto": 2000000, "monthly": 1500}, {"upto": 6000000, "monthly": 3000}, {"upto": null, "monthly": 0}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('unified-api:merge-dev', 'unified-api', 'Merge', 'Merge Technologies Inc.', 'One of the original ''unified API'' vendors, strongest in HR/ATS/CRM/accounting/file-storage/ticketing categories.', 'Launch (self-serve): free for the first 3 production linked accounts, then $650/month for up to 10 total accounts, $65/account after that. Professional and Enterprise are contract/quote-based, adding custom fields, extended scopes, and sandbox access. Confirmed on merge.dev/pricing.', '["Genuinely free to link the first 3 production accounts — a real zero-cost way to prove the pattern on a real integration, not just a sandbox", "Well-established, well-funded vendor with a long track record across its six supported categories", "Documentation and SDK quality is generally regarded as strong in this market", "Clear published self-serve pricing, unlike several competitors in this space"]'::jsonb, '["The jump from 3 free accounts to $650/month for up to 10 is steep — there''s no smooth middle tier for ''a handful more than 3''", "$65/account marginal cost after 10 adds up quickly in a genuinely multi-tenant, multi-client model", "Sandbox access requires the Professional (quote-based) tier — you can''t fully test before committing to a contract conversation", "Categories are fixed (accounting, ATS, CRM, file storage, HR/payroll, ticketing) — anything outside those six needs a different vendor anyway"]'::jsonb, 'When the actual need is 1-3 integrations, permanently, in Merge''s supported categories — the free Launch tier alone may be all that''s ever needed.', 'Reasonable land-in-consumer fit at very small scale thanks to the genuine free tier; becomes an expensive full step-function once past 3 linked accounts.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://www.merge.dev/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "connections", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 65, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Merge Launch plan: free for the first 3 production linked accounts, then $650/month for up to 10 total, $65/account after (merge.dev/pricing).", "caveat": "Professional/Enterprise tiers are custom-quoted and not modeled.", "tiers": [{"upto": 3, "monthly": 0}, {"upto": 10, "monthly": 650}, {"upto": null, "monthly": 0}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('unified-api:apideck', 'unified-api', 'Apideck', 'Apideck', 'A unified API with particular depth in accounting integrations, priced per ''consumer'' (end customer) rather than per call or per connection.', 'Launch: $599/month, 25-50 consumers. Scale: $1,299/month, 100-500 consumers. Enterprise: custom. 30-day free trial with full access on both self-serve tiers. Confirmed on apideck.com/pricing.', '["Per-consumer pricing maps cleanly onto this project''s own ''consumer app'' concept — a natural fit for how the Integrator already models access", "30-day full-access trial is generous for evaluation", "Particularly strong, per its own marketing, in accounting-category integrations specifically", "Self-serve signup with no sales call required to start"]'::jsonb, '["$599/month floor is still a real commitment relative to this project''s current $7/month total infra spend", "Per-consumer pricing penalizes exactly the multi-tenant, multi-client model this project is building toward, since each client company might count as its own consumer", "No free tier below the 30-day trial", "Category breadth outside accounting is less clearly a strength than Merge''s or Unified.to''s"]'::jsonb, 'If accounting-system integrations (QuickBooks-class) specifically become the priority connector, where Apideck''s stated specialty lines up directly.', 'Reasonable land-in-consumer fit if and when accounting integrations become the actual near-term need; premature otherwise.', 'land-in-consumer', 'growing', 'https://www.apideck.com/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "end_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Apideck published tiers, priced per ''consumer'': Launch $599/mo (25-50 consumers), Scale $1,299/mo (100-500 consumers) (apideck.com/pricing).", "caveat": "Enterprise tier is custom-quoted; 30-day free trial not modeled.", "tiers": [{"upto": 50, "monthly": 599}, {"upto": 500, "monthly": 1299}, {"upto": null, "monthly": 0}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('unified-api:paragon', 'unified-api', 'Paragon', 'Paragon (useparagon.com)', 'An embeddable integration/workflow platform (native integrations plus a visual workflow builder) usage-priced on connected tenants.', 'Usage-based, scaling on ''Connected Users'' (tenant orgs using integrations); a free self-serve tier exists (''Start for free'') but specific free-tier limits are not shown publicly. Self-host/forward-deploy is an Enterprise-only feature. Confirmed on useparagon.com/pricing.', '["Free self-serve tier with no sales call to begin evaluating", "Combines integration connectivity with a visual workflow/automation builder, which could reduce custom Express code for simple sync logic", "''Connected Users'' billing concept maps reasonably onto this project''s per-consumer-app model", "Real dashboard signup exists, confirmed via the pricing page''s own CTA"]'::jsonb, '["Actual dollar pricing is not published anywhere on the page — ''usage-based'' with no visible rate card means real cost is unknown until you''re inside the product", "Self-host/forward-deploy (the option that would let credentials stay in the hub''s own infrastructure) is Enterprise-only, which for most vendors in this space means a quote well above what a one-person shop budgets for", "Free-tier limits are unstated, so it''s unclear how much real evaluation is possible before hitting a paywall", "Less name-recognition/market signal than Merge or Nango"]'::jsonb, 'Worth a free-tier trial specifically to see real numbers, but budget-planning is impossible from public information alone.', 'Unclear until pricing is confirmed directly with the vendor; the Enterprise-only self-host gate is a real concern for anything regulated-adjacent.', 'land-in-consumer', 'growing', 'https://www.useparagon.com/pricing', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Usage-based on ''Connected Users''/tenants, but no rate card or free-tier limit is shown publicly on useparagon.com/pricing.", "caveat": "Self-host/forward-deploy is an Enterprise-only feature, not priced."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('unified-api:cyclr', 'unified-api', 'Cyclr', 'Cyclr Ltd.', 'An embedded iPaaS aimed at SaaS vendors who want to offer integrations inside their own product, with an explicit private-cloud/self-hosted deployment option.', 'Native Embedded iPaaS: from $1,595/month (PAYG), $2,595/month (Growth), $7,195/month (Scale). Service Embedded iPaaS: from $1,495/month (Shared), quote-based for Private Cloud. MCP PaaS: from $999/month (Shared), custom for Private Cloud. Confirmed on cyclr.com/pricing.', '["Explicitly offers a Private Cloud, self-hosted deployment (customer manages their own AWS/Azure account) — a real option for keeping infrastructure and credentials in-house if the price were justified", "Unlimited users and unlimited integrations per plan, so pricing doesn''t punish growth the way per-connection models do", "''MCP PaaS'' tier suggests some alignment with the MCP-based architecture this project already uses elsewhere", "Long-established embedded-iPaaS vendor with a mature product"]'::jsonb, '["Every published tier starts at $999-$7,195/month — an order of magnitude beyond what a one-person shop''s current infra budget ($7/month Render Starter) can justify", "Aimed at SaaS vendors selling integrations to their own customers, not at a small internal control-plane use case — the product''s whole framing is a mismatch", "Self-hosted Private Cloud tier is quote-only, so the one option that would fit the architecture''s credential-custody preference has no visible price at all", "No free or low-cost entry tier exists to prove the pattern cheaply first"]'::jsonb, 'If this project were itself selling integrations as a product feature to many external customers — not this project''s current situation.', 'Poor fit at this stage purely on cost; the self-hosted option is architecturally interesting but financially opaque and likely well above budget.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://cyclr.com/pricing', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 1595, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Cheapest published tier: Native Embedded iPaaS PAYG $1,595/month (cyclr.com/pricing).", "caveat": "Growth ($2,595/mo), Scale ($7,195/mo), Service Embedded and MCP PaaS tiers, and quote-based Private Cloud not modeled; no usage caps are specified per tier."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('unified-api:workato-embedded', 'unified-api', 'Workato Embedded', 'Workato', 'An enterprise integration/automation platform (iPaaS) with an embeddable product tier for vendors who want to offer Workato-powered integrations inside their own app.', 'No public self-serve pricing. Third-party contract-tracking data (Vendr) reports a median annual contract value of about $64,544/year across 340 tracked purchases, with typical ranges from roughly $25,000/year (small teams) to $150,000-$400,000+/year (enterprise), all consumption-based on automation task volume.', '["Extremely mature, broad-connector automation platform with strong enterprise credibility", "Consumption-based (task volume) pricing rather than flat seats, in principle scales with actual usage", "Embedded tier specifically targets vendors wanting to white-label integrations, which is architecturally close to this project''s ambitions"]'::jsonb, '["Enterprise sales only — no self-serve signup or public pricing exists at all", "Median annual contract of ~$64.5k is roughly 900x this project''s entire current Render hosting cost — wildly out of proportion to a one-person shop", "This is the textbook case of ''a daemon/operator/contract this small shop cannot justify even if the license were free'', except here the license itself is the expensive part", "Nothing about its published pricing model suggests any tier reachable below tens of thousands of dollars per year"]'::jsonb, 'For a well-funded company embedding integrations as a core product feature across many enterprise customers — not for this project at any point in its current trajectory.', 'Poor fit on cost alone; included here only because it was explicitly asked about, not because it is a realistic option for this hub.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://www.vendr.com/marketplace/workato', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 5378.67, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": true, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "No public self-serve pricing; Vendr third-party contract data reports a median annual contract value of $64,544/year across 340 tracked purchases.", "caveat": "Actual price is consumption-based on automation task volume and highly variable (roughly $25k/yr small teams to $150k-$400k+/yr enterprise); median used only as a rough placeholder."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('unified-api:build-direct-per-app', 'unified-api', 'Build it directly (one adapter per SaaS)', 'n/a — in-house', 'Skip a unified-API layer entirely: write a small, purpose-built Express route/service per SaaS integration, exactly as the AI-highlights and Apps & Access features were already built.', 'No vendor fee; cost is entirely engineering time per integration, using the same service-layer pattern already established in `api/src/services/`.', '["Zero new vendor, zero new monthly bill, zero new credential-custody question to answer — the integration''s data never leaves this project''s own infrastructure at all", "Matches the project''s own precedent exactly: every existing connector (highlights, AI gateway, consumer apps) was hand-built this way already", "Full control over exactly what''s stored and how, which matters most for anything IPTA-adjacent", "No risk of a vendor''s pricing, ownership, or terms changing out from under the integration"]'::jsonb, '["Does not scale past a handful of integrations — the entire point of a unified API is to avoid rebuilding OAuth/pagination/rate-limit handling per SaaS, and hand-building forfeits that entirely", "Every new SaaS the consumer apps want becomes a small development project rather than a config row, which cuts directly against ''everything variable is a row, everything invariant is code'' once the list of SaaS grows past a few", "All ongoing maintenance (API version changes, auth flow changes) becomes this one-person shop''s permanent burden per integration", "No credible answer for ''the long tail'' this research set was specifically asked to solve — this only works for the short list"]'::jsonb, 'Right now, for the one or two integrations that actually exist today — and as the default until real, y-shaped SaaS demand from Staffility''s job list justifies paying for a unified layer.', 'The clear correct choice at today''s connector count; revisit once the number of distinct SaaS integrations Staffility/IPTA actually need is known and larger than a handful.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://www.nango.dev/pricing', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "No vendor fee; cost is entirely engineering time per integration, using the existing api/src/services/ pattern.", "caveat": "Engineering time not priced; cost scales with number of integrations built, not a metered driver."}'::jsonb, 'shortlist', 'Implied by the same line: partner APIs called directly, no unified layer in the built system.', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('financial-aggregation:plaid', 'financial-aggregation', 'Plaid', 'Plaid Inc.', 'The default US account-aggregation API: bank login widget (Link) plus Auth/Balance/Identity/Transactions products.', 'Pay-as-you-go (month-to-month, no commitment) self-serve; Growth (12-month commitment, discounted) and Custom/enterprise tiers exist. Per-product costs are not published — Plaid discloses the pricing shape (one-time, subscription, and per-request products) but not rates; you request access and see pricing in-app. A free Limited Production tier allows up to 200 live-data calls per product for testing.', '["Self-serve signup with a real (if capped) production tier for a solo developer, confirmed on Plaid''s own pricing page", "Auth, Balance, Identity, and Transactions are on every tier including Pay-as-you-go", "Broadest US institution coverage of any option here, so consumer apps rarely hit a bank Plaid can''t reach", "Docs, SDKs, and sandbox are mature — least integration risk of the set"]'::jsonb, '["No public per-call/per-item rate card; real cost is only visible after requesting access, so budgeting is a guess until you''re in the dashboard", "Advanced products (Signal, Transfer, Layer, Protect) are locked behind Growth/Custom, pushing you toward a 12-month commitment as usage grows", "Historically the vendor most associated with lock-in complaints once an app is wired to Plaid''s Item model", "A 2019-era data-scraping lawsuit and periodic bank-side outages are the standard objections raised against it"]'::jsonb, 'When a consumer app needs broad, reliable US bank coverage now and the owner would rather pay Plaid''s premium than manage a second vendor relationship.', 'Good fit for a pass-through connector: Plaid returns data on request and the hub is not meant to store it, matching the ''never pooled'' rule — the risk is entirely about not letting Plaid tokens or pulled data linger in the hub''s own Postgres.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://plaid.com/pricing/', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Per-product rates not published; pay-as-you-go/Growth/Custom tiers exist but rates require in-app quote after requesting access.", "caveat": "Free Limited Production tier (200 live-data calls/product for testing) not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('financial-aggregation:teller', 'financial-aggregation', 'Teller', 'Teller.io', 'A smaller, developer-first US bank-data API using direct bank connections (not screen-scraping) for balances, transactions, verify, and identity.', 'Free ''Developer'' tier: unlimited sandbox plus up to 100 live production connections at no cost. Paid production is pay-per-use: Verify $1.50/account, Balance $0.10/API call, Transactions $0.30/enrollment/month, Identity $1.75/API call. Enterprise tier exists for scale; no published per-connection minimums.', '["100 free live connections is enough to prove the pattern before spending anything", "Transparent, published per-call pricing (rare in this set) makes cost easy to model up front", "Positions itself on direct, non-scraping bank connections rather than credential-sharing", "Priced and scoped for exactly this kind of small-shop pilot"]'::jsonb, '["Much smaller bank coverage than Plaid or MX — expect gaps at smaller regional/credit-union institutions", "Newer, less-funded company than Plaid/Mastercard/Envestnet — real vendor-durability risk over a multi-year horizon", "The page does not spell out the free-to-paid production transition process, so plan to ask directly before committing", "No public info here confirms enterprise-grade support SLAs"]'::jsonb, 'When the goal is to prototype the pass-through pattern cheaply and coverage of major/mid-size US banks is enough — i.e., exactly this project''s current stage.', 'Excellent fit philosophically: cheap self-serve start, pay only as real usage appears, and nothing about the product invites pooling data in the hub.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://teller.io/', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "connections", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 100, "per_unit": 0.3, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Teller''s published rate: Transactions $0.30/enrollment/month; free tier includes up to 100 live production connections.", "caveat": "Excludes one-time Verify ($1.50/account) and per-call Balance ($0.10) and Identity ($1.75) charges."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('financial-aggregation:stripe-financial-connections', 'financial-aggregation', 'Stripe Financial Connections', 'Stripe', 'Stripe''s own bank-linking product (built on the former Plaid-alternative ''Bridge'' technology it bought), for balances, account ownership, and transactions inside a Stripe account.', 'Pay-as-you-go, no free tier: instant bank account verification $1.50/account, Balances $0.10/successful call, Account Owners $1.50/successful call, Transactions $0.30 per institution per account holder per month. Custom pricing available at volume.', '["Zero new vendor relationship if the consumer app already touches Stripe for anything else", "Self-serve ''Start now'' link straight from an existing Stripe dashboard", "Pricing is fully published, unlike most of this list", "Backed by Stripe''s infrastructure and support reputation"]'::jsonb, '["Narrower feature set than a dedicated aggregator — it''s verification/balance/transaction-shaped, not a full aggregation platform", "No free tier at all, unlike Teller or Plaid''s limited free production", "Ties this connector''s fate to Stripe''s roadmap for a product that is not Stripe''s core business", "Less proven at high transaction-history volumes than Plaid/Envestnet"]'::jsonb, 'When Stripe is already the payments backbone for a consumer app and the ask is narrow (verify an account, check a balance) rather than full transaction history.', 'Fine for pass-through use; the per-call pricing actually discourages accidentally hoarding data since every pull costs money.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://stripe.com/financial-connections', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "connections", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.3, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Stripe''s published per-unit rate: Transactions $0.30 per institution per account holder per month; no free tier.", "caveat": "Excludes one-time bank verification ($1.50/account), Balances ($0.10/call) and Account Owners ($1.50/call) charges."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('financial-aggregation:mastercard-finicity', 'financial-aggregation', 'Mastercard Open Banking (Finicity)', 'Mastercard', 'Mastercard''s open-banking/account-aggregation platform, formerly Finicity, aimed at banks and larger fintechs.', 'Not published. No self-serve rate card was found on Mastercard''s developer or open-banking pages; pricing is quote-based through a sales/partner process.', '["Backed by Mastercard''s balance sheet and bank relationships — strong long-term vendor durability", "Full aggregation feature set (verification, transactions, income/employment, credit decisioning add-ons)", "Sandbox testing environment exists per Mastercard''s own support docs", "Strong reputation with banks and larger fintech partners"]'::jsonb, '["No public pricing anywhere — this is an enterprise sales motion, not a self-serve signup", "Unclear whether a one-person shop can even get a production agreement without a partner relationship", "Heavier compliance/onboarding overhead than this project''s operational-burden budget can likely absorb", "Overkill for a control plane whose job here is orchestration, not becoming a bank-grade aggregator itself"]'::jsonb, 'When a consumer app is itself bank-adjacent or needs Mastercard''s specific enterprise relationships — not a good starting point for a solo-operator hub.', 'Architecturally fine as a pass-through connector, but the sales-gated onboarding conflicts with the ''operational burden is first-class cost'' principle at this stage.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://developer.mastercard.com/product/open-banking/', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Not published; no self-serve rate card found on Mastercard''s developer/open-banking pages — quote-based through sales/partner process.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('financial-aggregation:mx', 'financial-aggregation', 'MX Platform API', 'MX Technologies', 'A full-featured account-aggregation and financial-data-enrichment platform competing directly with Plaid, marketed mainly to banks and larger fintechs.', 'Not published on MX''s product pages. No self-serve tier, sandbox signup, or rate card is exposed publicly — everything routes through a ''Request Demo'' flow.', '["Strong data enrichment (transaction categorization, spend insights) beyond raw aggregation", "Wide US/Canada DDA coverage per MX''s own marketing claims", "Tokenized connections designed to survive credential changes, per MX''s product page", "Well known and trusted in the banking/fintech space"]'::jsonb, '["Entirely sales-gated — no visible path for a solo developer to self-serve even a sandbox", "No public pricing at all, so it cannot be budgeted for without a sales call", "Docs/dashboard appear to be customer-only, unlike Plaid/Teller''s open developer portals", "Almost certainly the wrong first call for a one-person control plane"]'::jsonb, 'When a consumer app is a bank or credit union itself and wants MX''s enrichment layer specifically — not this project''s near-term case.', 'Would work as a pass-through connector in principle, but the enterprise-only access model is a poor match for the small-shop constraint.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://www.mx.com/products/api/', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Not published on MX''s product pages; no self-serve tier or rate card, everything routes through a Request Demo flow.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('financial-aggregation:yodlee', 'financial-aggregation', 'Envestnet | Yodlee', 'Envestnet', 'One of the oldest account-aggregation platforms (FastLink widget plus Account Aggregation/Verification APIs), now owned by wealth-management firm Envestnet.', 'Not published. developer.yodlee.com offers a ''Sign Up Now'' developer-portal registration, but pricing, contract minimums, and whether that registration leads anywhere near production access without a sales contract could not be confirmed from the public site.', '["Long operating history and very broad legacy bank coverage, including many smaller institutions", "Has a developer portal with real API documentation, not just a marketing page", "Strong presence in wealth-management/advisory use cases if that ever becomes relevant", "FastLink widget is a mature, well-tested integration pattern"]'::jsonb, '["Reputation (and this project''s own general industry knowledge) is that production access requires an enterprise/reseller contract, not pure self-serve — could not be confirmed either way from the public pages, so treat as likely gated", "No pricing transparency anywhere in the public docs", "UI/DX is generally regarded as dated next to Plaid or Teller", "Owned by Envestnet, a wealth-management company — product priorities may not track pure-aggregation needs"]'::jsonb, 'When integrating with an existing Envestnet/Yodlee relationship already in place elsewhere in the business — not a first choice for a fresh build.', 'Would sit as a pass-through connector like the others; the real question is whether onboarding is achievable at all at this company''s scale, which is unverified.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://developer.yodlee.com/', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Not published; developer.yodlee.com offers portal registration but pricing/contract minimums could not be confirmed from the public site.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('financial-aggregation:akoya', 'financial-aggregation', 'Akoya', 'Akoya LLC (bank-owned consortium)', 'A bank-permissioned data-access network — connections go through APIs the banks themselves operate, rather than a third party holding bank credentials.', 'Usage-tiered: ''Standard'' plan for under 10,000 monthly connections (rate not published on the pricing page itself), ''Enterprise'' for 10,000+ with custom pricing. A sandbox/test-data account is free. The page notes a setup/implementation fee ''may apply'' depending on integration needs.', '["Free sandbox with test data, no sales call needed to start evaluating it", "Direct bank-API model (no credential sharing) is a genuinely different, arguably lower-risk architecture than screen-scraping aggregators", "Backed/owned by a consortium of major US banks, which is a strong durability signal", "Standard-tier framing suggests it is meant to be reachable below enterprise scale"]'::jsonb, '["Coverage is limited to banks that have joined the Akoya network — smaller/regional banks may not be reachable at all", "Exact Standard-tier pricing is not published; a ''may apply'' setup fee is a real unknown for a solo shop''s budget", "Whether Standard-tier production activation is truly self-serve (vs. still needing a sales conversation) is not confirmed by the public page", "Less battle-tested in ordinary product integrations than Plaid/Teller"]'::jsonb, 'When the specific consumer app''s banks are large, Akoya-network institutions and true no-credential-sharing access matters more than universal coverage.', 'A genuinely strong match for pass-through: the network''s whole design point is that nobody but the bank and the end user ever holds the credential.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://akoya.com/pricing', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Usage-tiered (''Standard'' under 10,000 monthly connections, ''Enterprise'' 10,000+) but the Standard rate itself is not published on the pricing page; a sandbox is free.", "caveat": "A setup/implementation fee ''may apply'' depending on integration needs, amount unstated."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('financial-aggregation:gocardless-bank-account-data', 'financial-aggregation', 'GoCardless Bank Account Data', 'GoCardless', 'GoCardless''s PSD2 open-banking account-data API, covering EEA and UK banks only.', 'Not published for this product; GoCardless''s docs mention account verification is ''only offered to paying customers.'' More importantly: new signups for Bank Account Data are currently disabled — confirmed via GoCardless''s own new-signups-disabled landing page (blocked from full fetch by robots.txt but confirmed by its title and by a live GitHub issue reporting the same, opened Dec 2025).', '["Was historically a genuinely free/cheap way to reach EEA/UK banks, which is why hobbyist tools like Actual Budget integrated it", "Backed by an established payments company (GoCardless), not a fly-by-night provider", "Clear regulatory footing (PSD2 AISP registration) for EU coverage"]'::jsonb, '["US banks are not supported at all — this system''s near-term US-centric consumer apps get nothing from it", "New signups are disabled as of this research (Dec 2025 report, still unresolved) — cannot be provisioned today regardless of interest", "No US relevance and an availability freeze together make this a non-starter right now", "Actual Budget''s own docs already flag it as ''not accepting new accounts''"]'::jsonb, 'Only relevant if a future connector needs UK/EEA bank data and signups reopen — not usable today.', 'Would be pass-through in design, but is currently not obtainable, so fit is moot until GoCardless reopens it.', 'n/a', 'declining', 'https://github.com/adept/gocardless-to-csv/issues/4', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Not published for this product; new signups for Bank Account Data are currently disabled per GoCardless''s own landing page.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('financial-aggregation:build-direct', 'financial-aggregation', 'Build it directly (no aggregator)', 'n/a — in-house', 'Skip a middle vendor entirely: integrate one bank''s own developer API directly, or use manual OFX/CSV import, for the one or two connections actually needed today.', 'No vendor fee; cost is entirely engineering time plus whatever the bank itself charges for API access (usually free for basic read access, if the bank offers a public API at all).', '["Zero third-party vendor risk, no per-call fees, no credential-sharing with an aggregator at all", "Matches ''everything invariant is code'' when there really is exactly one bank/one connection to support", "No new vendor to vet for the DFARS/regulated angle since nothing leaves the two parties involved", "Full control over exactly what data is pulled and when"]'::jsonb, '["Does not scale — every additional bank is its own integration project, the opposite of the aggregator-led philosophy this project has already committed to", "Most US banks do not offer a public developer API at all, so this typically degrades to fragile screen-scraping or manual file import", "No vendor absorbing the ''bank changed their login flow'' maintenance burden — that lands entirely on the one-person shop", "Not a real answer once more than one or two banks are involved"]'::jsonb, 'Only as a stopgap for a single, specific bank that happens to expose its own clean API, or for pure manual CSV/OFX import while deciding on a real aggregator.', 'Technically the ultimate pass-through (nothing is even stored in a vendor''s system), but it directly contradicts the ''aggregator-led, thin-but-smart'' decision already made for this project.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://plaid.com/pricing/', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "connections", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "No vendor; cost is engineering time plus whatever the bank itself charges for API access, usually free for basic read access.", "caveat": "Some banks may charge for API access; engineering time itself is not priced."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 80),
  ('personal-finance-sync:tiller', 'personal-finance-sync', 'Tiller', 'Tiller Money', 'Feeds daily bank transactions and balances directly into a Google Sheet or Excel workbook, which then behaves like a queryable data source.', '$99/year (confirmed on tiller.com/pricing), 30-day free trial, no monthly option surfaced on the pricing page.', '["This project''s own environment already has a live Tiller MCP connector (list_spreadsheets / query_transactions / update_transactions), so integration cost is effectively already paid", "Because the destination is a normal spreadsheet, any tool that can read Sheets/Excel can read Tiller''s output — no proprietary API lock-in for the data itself", "$99/year is trivial next to Render''s $7/month API hosting cost already budgeted for this project", "Tiller''s own aggregator relationships (Plaid-class connections under the hood) mean bank-connection maintenance isn''t Bill''s problem"]'::jsonb, '["Tiller itself does not publish a general third-party REST API — programmatic access is via the spreadsheet, not a documented HTTP endpoint, so ''the API'' is really ''Google Sheets API against Tiller''s output''", "Categorization rules and feed configuration still require occasional manual spreadsheet upkeep", "Less structured than a real database — multi-user or multi-tenant use (several companies'' finances) means several separate spreadsheets to manage", "No native concept of access grants the way this project''s `consumer_apps` model expects"]'::jsonb, 'When the goal is exactly what this project already has running: bank data landing in a spreadsheet that other tools (including this hub, via Sheets access) can read.', 'Very strong practical fit precisely because it is already wired into this environment; the residency question becomes ''the spreadsheet is the land-in-consumer destination.''', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://tiller.com/pricing/', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 8.25, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": true, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "$99/year confirmed on tiller.com/pricing; no monthly option surfaced.", "caveat": "30-day free trial not modeled; billed per account, not per bank connection."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('personal-finance-sync:build-direct-to-postgres', 'personal-finance-sync', 'Build it directly (aggregator to your own Postgres)', 'n/a — in-house, using whichever financial-aggregation vendor is chosen', 'Skip a personal-finance app entirely: pull transactions straight from the chosen aggregator (Plaid/Teller/etc.) and write them into the Integrator''s own Postgres or a consumer app''s store.', 'No separate vendor fee beyond the aggregation-connector cost already chosen in the financial-aggregation set; cost is engineering time to write the sync job.', '["Matches this project''s own stated architecture exactly — ''land-in-consumer is the default, not a concession'' — with no intermediary app to reconcile against", "Full schema control: store exactly the fields needed, nothing more, which also minimizes what a breach could expose", "No second vendor relationship, no second bill, no second ToS to track", "Reuses whatever aggregator relationship is already built for other connectors"]'::jsonb, '["All the categorization, budgeting-rule, and reconciliation logic that Tiller/YNAB/Lunch Money already built has to be re-built or done without", "This is real engineering work, not configuration — the opposite of ''everything variable is a row'' unless the ingest is generalized well", "No polished UI for Bill to review the data by hand day-to-day; that has to be the admin''s Highlights-style screen or a spreadsheet export", "Duplicates effort already available cheaply from Tiller"]'::jsonb, 'Once there''s a second or third consumer app that needs its own copy of transaction data with no vendor in between — not needed while Tiller already does the job for one user.', 'The purest architectural fit of any option in this set — it is literally the pattern the project has already chosen for AI highlights, applied to finance.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://plaid.com/pricing/', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "No separate vendor fee beyond whichever financial-aggregation connector is chosen; cost is engineering time to write the sync job.", "caveat": "Actual data cost accrues to the financial-aggregation vendor selected separately."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('personal-finance-sync:lunch-money', 'personal-finance-sync', 'Lunch Money', 'Lunch Money (Yuting Cai)', 'A budgeting web app built API-first, with a fully documented public REST API for reading and writing budgets, transactions, and categories.', '$10/month or $100/year (confirmed on lunchmoney.app/pricing), 30-day free trial, price-lock guarantee on whatever plan/rate a user first joins on.', '["Rare in this space: a real, documented developer API (lunchmoney.dev) meant for third-party use, not reverse-engineered", "Read AND write access, unlike YNAB''s mostly-read API", "Small, indie-run company that has historically been developer-friendly (they maintain a public developers page)", "Price is comparable to Tiller and easy to justify"]'::jsonb, '["Newer and smaller than Tiller/YNAB — less proven at scale, single-maintainer-adjacent risk", "It''s a budgeting app UI first; using it purely as a data pipe means paying for features (budgets, goals) that may go unused", "No enterprise/team tier visible — built for individuals, not multi-tenant business use", "Bank-connection reliability depends on Lunch Money''s own aggregator partnerships, which is one more layer of indirection versus a direct Plaid pull"]'::jsonb, 'When Bill (or another individual user) wants a real budgeting UI in addition to API access, and the data need is per-person rather than multi-tenant.', 'Good fit for a per-user, land-in-consumer connector; its real API means the hub could genuinely read from it rather than working around it.', 'land-in-consumer', 'growing', 'https://lunchmoney.app/pricing', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 10, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "$10/month (or $100/year) confirmed on lunchmoney.app/pricing.", "caveat": "30-day free trial and price-lock guarantee on first-joined rate not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('personal-finance-sync:ynab', 'personal-finance-sync', 'YNAB (You Need A Budget)', 'YNAB', 'A well-known zero-based-budgeting app with an official, documented public API for reading (and limited writing of) budget data.', 'Requires an active YNAB subscription (subscription price not on the API page itself); the API itself has no extra fee. Personal access tokens or OAuth apps; OAuth apps start in a 25-user ''Restricted Mode'' and need a 2-4 week review to lift that cap.', '["Official, documented, genuinely free-to-use API layered on top of a paid subscription — confirmed at api.ynab.com", "Supports OAuth for building a real third-party integration, not just personal scripts", "Mature product with a long track record and an established user base", "Rate limit (200 requests/hour) is generous enough for a periodic sync job"]'::jsonb, '["Primarily read-only — most endpoints are GET; write support is limited to a handful of resources", "OAuth apps are capped at 25 authorizations until a manual review clears them — a real bottleneck if this were ever offered to multiple client companies", "Budgeting-methodology-specific (zero-based envelopes) — awkward fit if the need is just ''raw transactions,'' not budget categories", "Requires an active paid subscription per user connected, on top of any hub cost"]'::jsonb, 'When the actual need is envelope-style budgeting data for one or a few individual users, not a general transaction feed for many tenants.', 'Reasonable land-in-consumer fit for a single user''s data; the OAuth review requirement makes it a poor fit if this needed to scale to many consumer companies quickly.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://api.ynab.com/', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Subscription price is not stated on YNAB''s own API page (''requires an active YNAB subscription''); the API itself has no extra fee.", "caveat": "OAuth apps start in a 25-user Restricted Mode requiring a 2-4 week review to lift."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('personal-finance-sync:actual-budget', 'personal-finance-sync', 'Actual Budget', 'Actual Budget (open source)', 'A self-hosted, open-source YNAB-style budgeting app; bank sync is optional and goes through third-party providers you configure yourself.', 'Free and open source. Hosting is on you (a small VM or container). Its supported bank-sync providers (GoCardless BankAccountData for EU, SimpleFIN Bridge for North America, Akahu for NZ, Enable Banking for Europe, Pluggy.ai for Brazil) each carry their own separate cost/availability — e.g. GoCardless''s EU option is currently closed to new signups (see financial-aggregation set), and SimpleFIN is a separate paid third-party bridge for US/Canada.', '["Genuinely free software with no vendor lock-in — the actual credible open-source option in this category", "Confirmed to expose its own API, which the self-hosting owner can call directly (`actual-server`)", "Bank sync is manually triggered, not automatic polling, which is arguably a feature for a low-traffic personal-finance use case", "Multiple regional bank-sync providers give some flexibility depending on where accounts are held"]'::jsonb, '["Requires running and maintaining `actual-server` yourself — exactly the ''daemon to operate'' cost this project explicitly wants to avoid for a one-person shop", "US/Canada bank sync depends on SimpleFIN, a separate paid service not evaluated in this research pass", "EU bank sync depends on GoCardless Bank Account Data, which per this research is currently closed to new signups", "More moving parts (self-hosted app + third-party bank-sync bridge + your own ops) than any hosted option in this set"]'::jsonb, 'For a technically comfortable owner who wants zero subscription cost and is willing to run one more small service, and whose banks are covered by SimpleFIN or one of the regional bridges.', 'Philosophically the closest thing to ''own everything'', but it directly conflicts with the stated small-shop rule that any daemon/operator burden is expensive here.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://actualbudget.org/docs/advanced/bank-sync/', '{"kind": "byo", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 5, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Free and open source app with no license fee; base_monthly is a placeholder small-VM/container hosting cost, not a licence fee.", "caveat": "Operator time not priced; bank-sync provider costs (GoCardless, SimpleFIN, Akahu, Enable Banking, Pluggy.ai) are separate and not included."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('personal-finance-sync:monarch-money', 'personal-finance-sync', 'Monarch Money', 'Monarch Money, Inc.', 'A popular modern budgeting/net-worth app (a common Mint replacement) with a polished consumer UI.', 'Not independently re-verified in this pass; widely reported around $99-$150/year based on prior public pricing, but not confirmed from Monarch''s own pricing page in this session.', '["Strong, actively developed consumer product with broad account-type support (investments, real estate, budgets, not just checking/savings)", "Large enough user base that community tooling around it is active and reasonably well maintained", "Frequently cited as the leading post-Mint-shutdown replacement, so long-term viability looks reasonable"]'::jsonb, '["No officially documented, supported public API for third-party developers was found — status.monarch.com is an internal service-status page, not a developer portal", "Every third-party client found (Python/Node packages on GitHub/PyPI) is an unofficial, reverse-engineered wrapper around Monarch''s internal GraphQL API, which can break without notice on any Monarch release", "Using an unofficial API risks violating Monarch''s Terms of Service — a real risk for a business-adjacent integration, not just a hobby script", "No self-serve developer program, keys, or rate-limit documentation exists to plan around"]'::jsonb, 'As a personal budgeting app for Bill to use standalone — not as something the Integrator should build an automated connector against.', 'Poor fit for this hub: there is no supported integration surface, so any connector here would be brittle and outside the vendor''s own terms.', 'n/a', 'growing', 'https://status.monarch.com/public-api', '{"kind": "flat", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 10.38, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": true, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Not independently re-verified this session; widely reported (third-party) range of $99-150/year — midpoint ($124.50/year) used.", "caveat": "Not confirmed from Monarch''s own pricing page."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('personal-finance-sync:quicken-simplifi', 'personal-finance-sync', 'Quicken Simplifi', 'Quicken Inc.', 'Quicken''s modern cloud budgeting/cash-flow app, aggregating over 14,000 institutions per its own marketing.', 'Consumer subscription pricing (not independently re-verified here); no separate API pricing exists because there is no official API product.', '["Backed by Quicken''s long institutional history and very broad bank coverage claim (14,000+ institutions)", "OAuth-based bank-connection flow (per Quicken''s own support docs) is a modern, credential-safe pattern for the end user", "Reasonable choice for a non-technical individual user who just wants cash-flow visibility"]'::jsonb, '["No official developer API was found — only an ''unofficial API for Quicken Simplifi'' exists on GitHub, reverse-engineered and unsupported by Quicken", "Same ToS and breakage risk as Monarch: nothing here is sanctioned by the vendor for third-party integration", "No path found for the Integrator to read Simplifi data in a supported way at all"]'::jsonb, 'As a personal cash-flow app in its own right — not as an integration target for this hub.', 'Poor fit: with no supported API, any connector would sit entirely outside the vendor''s terms and could break at any time.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://support.simplifi.quicken.com/en/articles/6997452-new-and-improved-way-to-connect-to-your-financial-institution-oauth-api', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Consumer subscription pricing not independently re-verified in this pass and no figure or range was obtained; no official API product exists either.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('personal-finance-sync:copilot-money', 'personal-finance-sync', 'Copilot Money', 'Copilot Money, Inc.', 'A design-forward budgeting/net-worth app, Apple-ecosystem-first (iOS/Mac/iPad, plus web).', 'Not verified in this session — a fetch of copilot.money was blocked by a permission timeout, so pricing and API existence could not be confirmed from a primary source this pass.', '["Well-regarded UI/UX in its market segment, per general industry reputation", "Apple-ecosystem-native experience if Bill''s daily driver is Apple hardware"]'::jsonb, '["No evidence of a public developer API was found in search results — nothing resembling lunchmoney.dev or api.ynab.com surfaced for Copilot", "Apple-first design likely means weaker or no support for the non-Apple parts of a multi-tenant admin stack", "Could not verify current pricing this session — treat any number quoted elsewhere as unconfirmed until checked directly"]'::jsonb, 'As a personal app for an Apple-first individual user, not as an integration target — same caveat as Monarch/Simplifi.', 'Likely poor fit given no confirmed API surface, but this is the weakest-evidenced entry in the set and deserves a direct check before ruling it out entirely.', 'n/a', 'growing', 'https://lunchmoney.app/pricing', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Not verified in this session — a fetch of copilot.money was blocked by a permission timeout, so pricing and API existence could not be confirmed.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('transcript-capture:fireflies-ai', 'transcript-capture', 'Fireflies.ai', 'Fireflies.ai, Inc.', 'A meeting bot that joins calls, transcribes them, and exposes a GraphQL API to pull transcripts, summaries, and action items back out.', 'Free forever; Pro $10/seat/mo, Business $19/seat/mo, Enterprise $39/seat/mo (all annual billing) as of Aug 2026.', '["API access is listed on every tier including Free — no enterprise contract needed to get data out programmatically", "Cheapest paid tier at $10/seat/mo undercuts every other meeting-bot vendor researched here", "GraphQL API explicitly supports both fetching data and uploading audio, so it can also take capture the hub doesn''t own"]'::jsonb, '["\"Custom data retention\" (i.e., a short, enforced window) is an Enterprise-only feature per their own pricing page — Free/Pro/Business retain by default until manually deleted", "Docs admit the API is still being expanded (\"actively working to expose more functionality\"), so some fields may not be stable", "A cloud recorder-bot model at all — audio still transits and lands on a third party''s servers regardless of plan"]'::jsonb, 'When the hub needs a cheap, always-available API to pull transcripts and is willing to handle its own deletion cadence rather than relying on the vendor''s retention controls.', 'Good API fit (matches control-plane philosophy — a row/config, not a bespoke integration) but the hub would need to actively delete source recordings itself since automatic short retention is Enterprise-gated.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://fireflies.ai/pricing', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "end_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 10, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": true, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Pro tier: $10/seat/month (annual billing) is the cheapest paid tier (Fireflies pricing, Aug 2026); a Free forever tier also exists.", "caveat": "Business ($19/seat) and Enterprise ($39/seat) tiers, and the free tier''s feature/minute limits, not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('transcript-capture:tldv', 'transcript-capture', 'tl;dv', 'tl;dv (Salesroom / Codex Solutions)', 'A meeting recorder with a REST API scoped per-meeting for transcripts, AI notes, and time-limited recording download links.', 'Free plan exists; API access requires Pro or Business plan (not Free, not Enterprise) — official support docs, Aug 2026; per-seat price not published in the fetched source.', '["Clean, purpose-built REST endpoints — /meetings/{id}/transcript, /notes, /download — rather than a generic query language", "Recording download links are pre-signed and short-lived (6 hours), which is a sane default for not leaving audio sitting in a URL", "API access sits on the mid-tier (Pro/Business), not gated all the way up to Enterprise like Otter or MS Teams"]'::jsonb, '["API access explicitly excludes both the Free plan AND the Enterprise plan — an unusual gate that needs re-checking before committing", "Access depends on the meeting organizer''s own subscription status, not just who can see the meeting in the app — a shared/team account nuance", "No retention-period specifics found in their published security page — has to be asked directly"]'::jsonb, 'When a small team wants a real API without paying for the top Otter/Fathom-style Enterprise tier, and the meeting-organizer-owns-access model fits how the team actually runs meetings.', 'Reasonable: API is affordable and specific, but retention policy is unpublished, so it can''t yet be verified as compatible with a highlights-only, no-transcript-storage philosophy.', 'land-in-consumer', 'growing', 'https://intercom.help/tldv/en/articles/11583137-api', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "API access requires Pro or Business plan (not Free, not Enterprise), but the per-seat price is not published in the fetched source (tl;dv support docs, Aug 2026).", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('transcript-capture:meetily', 'transcript-capture', 'Meetily', 'Zackriya Solutions (open source, MIT)', 'A 100%-local, open-source meeting assistant that transcribes with Whisper and summarizes with a pluggable LLM, entirely on the machine running it.', 'Community Edition: free, MIT-licensed. Optional Pro $10/user/mo (billed annually) and custom Enterprise for hosted/admin features.', '["Audio and transcript never leave the device by default — the single cleanest answer to the DFARS/CUI residency worry of any option here", "No vendor relationship, no recurring bill, no retention clock running anywhere but on Bill''s own disk, for the Community Edition", "RESTful API + webhooks are documented for custom integration, which fits the \"everything variable is a row\" MCP-push pattern"]'::jsonb, '["It is a real piece of software to install, update, and keep working — exactly the kind of standing local dependency a one-person shop pays for in attention, not dollars", "29.7k GitHub stars is healthy but this is still a much younger, smaller-team project than Otter/Fathom/Fireflies — expect rougher edges", "Bot-free capture means it only sees audio on the device it runs on — no built-in way to join a remote call the way Recall/Fathom bots do"]'::jsonb, 'When the meeting is exactly the kind IPTA''s DFARS obligations make radioactive to send to any cloud recorder — the local pipeline earns its keep on that one property alone.', 'Excellent residency fit (pass-through by construction, nothing pooled), but it''s the one option here with real setup/maintenance cost rather than a subscription — a fair trade only for the meetings that actually need it.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://meetily.ai/', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "end_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 10, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": true, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Optional Pro tier: $10/user/month billed annually; the Community Edition is free and MIT-licensed, fully local.", "caveat": "Custom Enterprise (hosted/admin features) tier not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('transcript-capture:plaud', 'transcript-capture', 'Plaud', 'Plaud Inc.', 'A hardware voice recorder (pin/card) plus app and cloud service that transcribes and summarizes, with an OAuth API and MCP/CLI to pull notes back out.', 'Starter free (300 min/mo); Pro $8.33–17.99/mo (1,200 min); Unlimited $19.99–29.99/mo; Team $20–28/user/mo, as of Aug 2026.', '["Genuine on-device option: with Cloud Sync off, \"recordings stay on-device; cloud used only on-demand; no persistent server copy retained\" per their own security docs", "When cloud sync IS used, deletion is explicit and propagates to backups — not just a soft-delete flag", "Documented MCP & CLI tooling for retrieving transcripts/notes fits directly into the hub''s existing MCP-push pattern"]'::jsonb, '["Default cloud behavior retains recordings, transcripts, and summaries indefinitely until a person manually deletes them or cancels the account — opt-in, not default-off", "Full local capture flow (\"Plaud Desktop\") appears to require owning the physical Plaud hardware, not just the app", "Regional cloud storage spans AWS Frankfurt/Oregon/Singapore/Japan — a residency detail worth confirming against any client contract before use"]'::jsonb, 'When Bill personally wants a wearable recorder for his own working sessions and is comfortable toggling Cloud Sync off for anything sensitive.', 'Good fit for Bill''s own low-sensitivity capture (the exact use case the hub''s session-highlights feature already targets), less clear for anything IPTA-adjacent unless run fully on-device.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://support.plaud.ai/hc/en-us/articles/57744166222489-Plaud-s-security-and-compliance', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "end_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 24, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Team plan: $20-28/user/month (published range, Aug 2026); midpoint ($24) used as the per-user rate.", "caveat": "Starter (free, 300 min/mo), Pro ($8.33-17.99/mo, 1,200 min) and Unlimited ($19.99-29.99/mo) individual-tier options not modeled; the Team figure itself is a published range, not a single number."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('transcript-capture:recall-ai', 'transcript-capture', 'Recall.ai', 'Recall.ai, Inc.', 'Developer infrastructure — a meeting-bot API and desktop-recording SDK — for building a custom capture integration rather than adopting a consumer note-taking app.', 'Usage-based as of their 2026 repricing: $0.50/recording hour (bot or SDK), $0.15/hour built-in transcription, storage free 7 days then $0.05/media-hour per extra 30 days; Calendar API included free.', '["No seats, no platform fee — pay only for the hours actually recorded, which suits a low-volume, one-person operation better than any per-seat SaaS here", "One API surface across Zoom, Meet, Teams, Webex, Slack huddles, and GoTo — avoids building N separate platform integrations", "Storage has an explicit short free window (7 days) before any charge accrues, which nudges toward not hoarding recordings by default"]'::jsonb, '["This is infrastructure, not a finished product — the hub''s own code has to do everything a Fireflies/Fathom UI already does, real build effort for a one-person shop", "No self-hosted or on-device deployment found — it is a cloud bot service, so the same residency questions as any other recorder-bot vendor apply", "Costs scale with usage in a way that''s easy to reason about at low volume but needs a cap/alert before it''s wired into anything automatic"]'::jsonb, 'When the hub wants to OWN the capture integration (matching "everything variable is a row") rather than depend on a third-party app''s UI and roadmap, and is willing to write the glue code.', 'Philosophically the closest match to a control-plane''s own connector model, but it trades a finished product for engineering time the shop may not want to spend on this vertical yet.', 'pass-through', 'growing', 'https://www.recall.ai/blog/new-recall-ai-pricing-for-2026', '{"kind": "usage_passthrough", "driver": "meetings", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.65, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "derived", "basis": "Usage-based: $0.50/recording hour (bot or SDK) + $0.15/hour built-in transcription = $0.65 combined, per Recall.ai''s 2026 repricing.", "caveat": "Assumes roughly 1 hour per meeting mapped onto the ''meetings'' driver; storage ($0.05/media-hour after 7 free days) and the free Calendar API not included."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('transcript-capture:fathom', 'transcript-capture', 'Fathom', 'Fathom Video, Inc.', 'A free, popular meeting recorder/summarizer with a REST API for meeting summaries, transcripts, and action items.', 'Free tier is generous on summaries; a paid API-key quickstart exists but no plan requirement was confirmable from official docs, and retention rules are Enterprise-only.', '["Free tier is real and widely used, so a first integration can be prototyped at zero cost before deciding whether to pay for anything", "API explicitly exposes summaries, transcripts, and action items — the shape the hub actually wants", "Rate limit (60 calls/min across all of an account''s keys) is generous for a low-volume single-owner use"]'::jsonb, '["Retention policies — the ability to auto-delete recordings after N days — are an Enterprise-plan feature and \"not self-serve yet\" even there; you email support to set one up", "Official docs would not confirm which paid tier (if any) is required for API access, which has to be verified directly with Fathom before relying on it", "API keys are scoped to meetings the user recorded or that were shared to their team — fine for one owner, a friction point if this ever grows past one seat"]'::jsonb, 'For quick prototyping against a free, well-documented API before committing spend, accepting that retention has to be handled by manually deleting recordings rather than by policy.', 'Workable as a data source, but the mismatch between "free tier for recording" and "Enterprise-only for retention control" means the hub inherits indefinite storage on Fathom''s side unless someone remembers to clean up.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://help.fathom.video/en/articles/6057089', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "Free tier is generous for summaries, but no plan requirement was confirmable for API access from official docs, and retention rules are Enterprise-only (unpriced).", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('transcript-capture:otter-ai', 'transcript-capture', 'Otter.ai', 'Otter.ai, Inc.', 'One of the original AI meeting-note apps, with a public API gated to its top plan.', 'Basic free; Pro $8.33–16.99/mo; Business $19.99–30/mo; Enterprise custom — API is Enterprise-only per Otter''s own help center, Aug 2026.', '["Very mature product with broad platform recognition and long track record", "API, once available, is comprehensive: channels, conversations, transcripts, audio, action items, insights, outlines", "Business tier already adds custom export formats and admin export controls, useful even without the API"]'::jsonb, '["\"Otter''s Public API is available for all Enterprise workspaces\" — full stop, no path to programmatic access below the top, custom-priced tier", "\"Custom data retention\" is likewise Enterprise-only — the two things this system cares about most (API + retention control) both require the plan a one-person shop is least likely to buy", "Only 2 API keys per user and a 10 req/s cap even at Enterprise — modest limits for the price tier required to get them"]'::jsonb, 'For an organization already paying for Otter Enterprise for other reasons, where the API comes bundled rather than being the reason to buy.', 'Poor fit for a solo shop: both features this evaluation cares about sit behind the one tier priced for teams, not individuals.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://help.otter.ai/hc/en-us/articles/36130822688279-Otter-ai-Public-API', '{"kind": "quote", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "unknown", "basis": "API access is gated to the Enterprise plan, which is custom-quoted per Otter''s own help center (Aug 2026); Basic/Pro/Business consumer tiers do not include API access.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('transcript-capture:ms-teams-copilot', 'transcript-capture', 'Microsoft Teams (native transcription + Copilot Meeting Insights API)', 'Microsoft', 'Teams'' built-in call transcription, readable programmatically only through the Graph "Meeting AI Insights" API, which itself requires a Microsoft 365 Copilot license per user.', 'Native transcription is included in most Teams/M365 plans; the Graph API that reads AI insights requires a Microsoft 365 Copilot add-on license (~$30/user/mo list, evaluation mode not applicable) per Microsoft Learn.', '["If LFODIE is already an M365 shop, transcription itself needs no new vendor or contract — it''s a Teams admin setting", "Transcripts/recordings land in the org''s own OneDrive/SharePoint by default, not a third party''s cloud — a residency plus for IPTA-adjacent meetings run on Teams", "Retention is controllable through standard Microsoft Purview retention labels (1-day minimum) — same tooling as everything else in a Microsoft tenant"]'::jsonb, '["Reading the transcript/insight programmatically via Graph requires a Copilot license on the specific user, purchased per-seat regardless of whether Copilot chat is ever used — a real ongoing cost just to unlock the API", "No default automatic deletion — an org has to actively configure a Purview retention label or the recording behaves like any other OneDrive file, i.e., forever", "Heaviest operational/compliance surface of any option here: Graph app registration, admin consent, Purview policy — the opposite of \"a row, not code\" for a one-person shop"]'::jsonb, 'When the org is already deep in M365, already pays for Copilot seats, and wants transcript retention to live inside the same compliance tooling as the rest of its Microsoft data.', 'Worst operational fit for a small shop of this shape: the API is locked behind a specific paid add-on that has nothing to do with the hub''s actual need, and the compliance setup is real engineering work.', 'land-in-consumer', 'mature', 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/microsoftteams/platform/graph-api/meeting-transcripts/meeting-insights', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "end_users", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 30, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "estimated", "basis": "Microsoft 365 Copilot add-on required to read AI insights via Graph: approximately $30/user/month list price (Microsoft Learn, Aug 2026).", "caveat": "Native Teams/M365 transcription itself is included at no extra cost; only the Graph-readable AI-insights layer requires the Copilot license; no evaluation mode applies."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('notify-publish:azure-communication-services', 'notify-publish', 'Azure Communication Services Email', 'Microsoft', 'Microsoft''s transactional email sending service inside the Communication Services family, billed per email sent plus data transferred.', 'As of Aug 2026, confirmed via the Azure Retail Prices API: $0.00025 per email sent (Basic tier, per recipient) plus $0.00012 per MB of data transferred (headers + body + attachments, per recipient). No published free monthly grant for either meter. A High Volume Email SKU exists at $0.000042 per recipient for internal/high-throughput scenarios.', '["Sub-cent per-email pricing at small volume; no platform/base fee to send at all", "Azure-managed domain (`*.azurecomm.net`) works immediately with zero DNS setup for dev/test", "Same ARM resource model as the rest of an Azure estate — one bill, one IAM surface"]'::jsonb, '["No free tier/grant found on either the per-email or per-MB meter", "Production sending requires a custom verified domain with SPF and DKIM DNS records — real setup burden, not turnkey", "Azure-managed domain sender address is an ugly GUID subdomain, unsuitable for a real from-address", "Deliverability reputation and domain warm-up are the sender''s problem, same as any raw SMTP relay"]'::jsonb, 'An Azure-native shop sending low-to-moderate transactional email volume that already owns DNS for its sending domain and wants one Azure invoice instead of a separate SendGrid/Postmark account.', 'A reasonable Render/Netlify-era email swap-in once the app lives in Azure, but the domain+SPF+DKIM setup is real work the current stack doesn''t require — budget an afternoon, not a config flag.', 'pass-through', 'mature', 'https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/communication-services/concepts/email-pricing', '{"kind": "per_unit", "driver": "emails", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.00025, "tiers": [], "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Azure Retail Prices API meter ''Basic Sent Email'', product ''Email'', SKU ''Basic'': $0.00025 per email sent (per recipient).", "caveat": "Excludes the separate $0.00012/MB data-transfer meter, which adds on top for anything beyond a trivial plain-text message; no free grant confirmed on either meter."}'::jsonb, 'chosen', 'Josh 2026-08-05: sends the referral invite. Every attempt logged, with a hard safety list so a test environment cannot email a real candidate.', 'catalogue', 0),
  ('notify-publish:discord-webhook', 'notify-publish', 'Discord Incoming Webhook', 'Discord Inc.', 'A URL you POST a JSON message to and it appears in a channel — no account, no SDK, no auth flow beyond the URL itself.', 'Free. No paid tier gates webhook posting.', '["Genuinely zero cost and zero setup beyond creating the webhook URL in a channel''s settings", "No daemon, no library, no auth token to rotate — a single POST from anywhere the hub already runs (Render, a cron job, MCP)", "Bots overall are capped at 50 requests/second per Discord''s own rate-limit docs — far beyond what a control-plane''s notification volume will ever need"]'::jsonb, '["Discord is a consumer/community platform first — using it as the business''s alert channel is a cultural mismatch for a company that runs on Slack/Teams/email", "No delivery guarantees, retries, or read receipts — it''s fire-and-forget, fine for a nice-to-have ping, not for anything that must be acknowledged", "Message formatting (embeds) is Discord-specific and doesn''t translate if the hub later wants a channel-agnostic notification abstraction"]'::jsonb, 'For Bill''s own personal alerting (e.g., "weekly-ops-brief just ran") where the lowest possible setup cost matters more than looking professional.', 'Excellent fit for the hub''s actual scale today: a control plane run by one person needs a ping, not a notification platform.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://docs.discord.com/developers/topics/rate-limits', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free. No paid tier gates webhook posting.", "caveat": ""}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 10),
  ('notify-publish:slack', 'notify-publish', 'Slack (Incoming Webhooks / Slack apps)', 'Slack Technologies (Salesforce)', 'The business-grade equivalent of a Discord webhook — post a message to a channel via a webhook URL, or use a full Slack app for two-way interaction later.', 'Slack itself has a free tier (message-history-limited); posting via an Incoming Webhook has no separate charge. Rate limits are documented per-method, not flatly stated as a single number.', '["If LFODIE or a consumer app''s team is already on Slack, this is the channel people actually watch — unlike Discord", "Same one-POST-to-a-URL simplicity as Discord''s webhook, so the integration code is nearly identical if the hub wants to support both", "Room to grow into a real Slack app later (slash commands, interactive buttons) without switching platforms"]'::jsonb, '["Slack''s own rate-limit documentation is fragmented across method-specific tiers rather than one clear number — worth load-testing before relying on it for anything bursty", "Free-tier Slack workspaces cap message retention/searchability, which matters if the notification itself is meant to be an audit trail, not just a ping", "Requires whichever team is receiving notifications to actually be a Slack shop — a real dependency if that''s not already true"]'::jsonb, 'When the audience for the notification is a team that already lives in Slack — the highest chance any given alert actually gets seen.', 'Strong fit if LFODIE''s own ops already run through Slack; otherwise no better than Discord for a solo recipient, just more setup.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://docs.slack.dev/apis/web-api/rate-limits/', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Posting via an Incoming Webhook has no separate charge; Slack itself has a free tier (message-history-limited).", "caveat": "Rate limits are documented per-method, not as a single number; full paid Slack plans for other features not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 20),
  ('notify-publish:resend', 'notify-publish', 'Resend', 'Resend, Inc.', 'A modern transactional email API built for developers, with a generous free tier and a clean REST/SDK surface.', 'Free: 3,000 emails/mo (100/day cap), 3 domains. Pro $20/mo for 50,000/mo, 10 domains. Scale $90/mo for 100,000/mo. Overage $0.90/1,000 emails. Aug 2026.', '["Free tier alone (3,000/mo) comfortably covers a one-person shop''s notification volume indefinitely", "Pricing and limits are published plainly with no \"contact sales\" wall at the entry tier, unlike most of the OCR/enterprise vendors researched elsewhere", "Built specifically for transactional sending from application code — matches the hub''s Node/TypeScript stack well"]'::jsonb, '["100 emails/day daily cap on the free tier could bite if a burst of agent runs all fire notifications at once", "Newer company than Postmark/SES/SendGrid — shorter track record for deliverability reputation at scale", "Domains beyond the plan''s included count are a paid add-on ($20/mo for 100 more) — a cost cliff to watch if the hub grows to serve multiple consumer apps'' own domains"]'::jsonb, 'As the default transactional-email connector for a small, code-first shop that wants generous free usage and a modern API without an enterprise sales conversation.', 'Very good fit — free tier size and API shape both suit a control plane with one owner and low notification volume.', 'n/a', 'growing', 'https://resend.com/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "emails", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.0009, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Resend published tiers: Free 3,000 emails/mo (100/day cap); Pro $20/mo for 50,000/mo; Scale $90/mo for 100,000/mo; overage $0.90/1,000 (Aug 2026).", "caveat": "3-domain / 10-domain limits per tier not modeled.", "tiers": [{"upto": 3000, "monthly": 0}, {"upto": 50000, "monthly": 20}, {"upto": 100000, "monthly": 90}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 30),
  ('notify-publish:postmark', 'notify-publish', 'Postmark', 'ActiveCampaign', 'A transactional-email specialist known for deliverability, with a small permanent free tier and inbound email processing on paid plans.', 'Free: 100 emails/mo, no card required, doesn''t expire, no overages allowed. Paid from $15–18/mo for 10,000 emails/mo plus per-1,000 overage ($1.20–1.80).', '["Free tier genuinely never expires (unlike SendGrid''s, which became a 60-day trial) — good for infrequent, low-volume alerting that should just keep working", "Strong, long-standing reputation specifically for transactional deliverability (not bulk/marketing mail), which is exactly this use case", "Inbound email processing is available on paid tiers if the hub ever wants two-way email, not just outbound notification"]'::jsonb, '["100 emails/mo free cap is tight if the hub starts sending per-agent-run notifications rather than daily digests", "Entry paid tier ($15/mo) costs more than Resend''s equivalent volume for no extra capability the hub needs today", "No mention of a self-hosted or on-prem option — pure SaaS, fine here since nothing sensitive is in a notification, but worth naming"]'::jsonb, 'When deliverability track record matters more than price and volume stays genuinely low (under 100/mo) or a paid tier is already justified.', 'Solid alternative to Resend; slightly worse economics at the free tier''s edge, better brand reputation for inbox placement.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://postmarkapp.com/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "emails", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.0012, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Postmark: Free 100 emails/mo (no card, doesn''t expire, no overage allowed); Paid from $15/mo for 10,000 emails/mo, overage from $1.20/1,000 (Aug 2026).", "caveat": "Uses the low end of the published $15-18/mo and $1.20-1.80/1,000 ranges; the free tier does not permit any overage at all (hard cap).", "tiers": [{"upto": 100, "monthly": 0}, {"upto": 10000, "monthly": 15}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 40),
  ('notify-publish:ms-teams-workflows', 'notify-publish', 'Microsoft Teams (Workflows / Power Automate webhook)', 'Microsoft', 'Teams'' current way to post a message to a channel from a webhook URL, replacing the old Office 365 Connectors that Microsoft has now shut off.', 'Free to create a Workflow-based webhook in a channel; Power Automate itself has its own separate licensing tiers for anything beyond the basic "post to Teams" template.', '["If LFODIE/a consumer app already lives in Teams, this reaches the same audience Slack would for a Microsoft-first org", "The new Workflows trigger supports Adaptive Cards, richer formatting than the old MessageCard format ever offered", "Comes free bundled with any Teams tenant — no separate notification-service subscription needed"]'::jsonb, '["CRITICAL: the old Office 365 Connectors mechanism — what most existing \"Teams incoming webhook\" tutorials and code samples describe — completed its retirement rollout May 18–22, 2026. Any naive integration built from older docs or existing code is now dead.", "The replacement is built on Power Automate/Workflows, which is a heavier, more Microsoft-specific abstraction than a plain webhook URL — more moving parts to learn for a one-line notification", "Bot icon/name customization and MessageCard-style interactive cards are explicitly unsupported in the new webhook flow — a step down in flexibility, not up"]'::jsonb, 'For a Microsoft-centric consumer app (this fits Bills-Master-Plan''s ecosystem) that specifically needs Teams-native alerts and is willing to rebuild any pre-2026 webhook code.', 'Situational — only worth the Workflows setup cost if the audience is already Teams-only; otherwise Slack or email get there with less Microsoft-specific plumbing, and any legacy integration MUST be checked against the May 2026 shutdown.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://devblogs.microsoft.com/microsoft365dev/retirement-of-office-365-connectors-within-microsoft-teams/', '{"kind": "free", "driver": "none", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Free to create a Workflow-based webhook in a Teams channel.", "caveat": "Power Automate itself has separate licensing tiers for anything beyond the basic ''post to Teams'' template; not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 50),
  ('notify-publish:aws-ses', 'notify-publish', 'AWS SES', 'Amazon Web Services', 'Amazon''s raw, low-level transactional email sending service — cheapest at volume, least polished developer experience.', 'Pay-as-you-go from $0.10/1,000 emails; "Essentials" plan $0.16/1,000 (0–10M/mo); no permanent SES-specific free tier as of Aug 2026 (only a general $200 AWS trial credit for 6 months).', '["Cheapest per-email cost of any option here by a wide margin once volume exists — irrelevant at a control plane''s tiny scale, but future-proof", "Sits inside AWS if the hub or a consumer app ever needs AWS-native plumbing (SNS, Lambda) for delivery events", "No per-domain limits or feature gates the way the boutique providers have"]'::jsonb, '["No meaningful permanent free tier for this system''s shape (Bill isn''t running SES from EC2, where the historic free tier applied) — starts costing from message one, unlike Resend/Postmark''s free tiers", "Raw API and IAM/SES sending-authorization setup is real yak-shaving for a one-line \"notify me\" use case — the opposite of a low-operational-burden pick", "Deliverability reputation must be self-managed (warm-up, complaint handling) far more than with Postmark/Resend, which handle more of this by default"]'::jsonb, 'Only once volume is genuinely large enough that per-email cost matters, or the hub is already deeply embedded in AWS for other reasons — neither is true here today.', 'Poor fit for a one-person shop''s low-volume notification need — the setup cost outweighs the marginal savings at this scale.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://aws.amazon.com/ses/pricing/', '{"kind": "usage_passthrough", "driver": "emails", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0.0001, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Pay-as-you-go from $0.10 per 1,000 emails (AWS SES pricing, Aug 2026).", "caveat": "Essentials plan alternative ($0.16/1,000, 0-10M/mo) and the temporary general $200 AWS trial credit (6 months) not modeled."}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 60),
  ('notify-publish:sendgrid', 'notify-publish', 'SendGrid (Twilio)', 'Twilio Inc.', 'One of the original transactional-email APIs, now fully owned and priced under the Twilio brand.', 'No permanent free plan — as of the July 26, 2025 change, new signups get only a 60-day trial (100 emails/day); cheapest ongoing paid plan is ~$19.95/mo for 50,000 emails/mo.', '["Long-established deliverability infrastructure and broad ecosystem integrations (Zapier, most SaaS platforms ship a SendGrid connector out of the box)", "50,000 emails/mo at the entry paid tier is generous headroom if the hub ever needs to email more than one recipient per event", "Under Twilio now, so it benefits from Twilio''s broader infrastructure and support org"]'::jsonb, '["FLAG: the free tier that made SendGrid a default recommendation for years is gone — it is now a 60-day trial, not a permanent option, as of mid-2025", "Cheapest paid plan ($19.95/mo) costs more than Resend''s equivalent-or-better free tier for a one-person shop''s actual volume", "Merged into Twilio''s broader pricing/branding, which has made the standalone SendGrid pricing page redirect and made current terms harder to pin down than competitors''"]'::jsonb, 'For a team already using Twilio for SMS/voice that wants email under the same billing relationship and vendor.', 'Weak fit here specifically because the free tier this system needs (near-zero volume, indefinite) no longer exists — Resend or Postmark''s free tiers do the same job for less.', 'n/a', 'mature', 'https://www.twilio.com/en-us/products/email-api/pricing', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "emails", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "No permanent free plan as of the July 26, 2025 change (60-day trial only, 100 emails/day); cheapest ongoing paid plan is ~$19.95/mo for 50,000 emails/mo (Twilio SendGrid, Aug 2026).", "caveat": "The 60-day trial period is not modeled as an ongoing free tier.", "tiers": [{"upto": 50000, "monthly": 19.95}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 70),
  ('notify-publish:novu', 'notify-publish', 'Novu', 'Novu Inc. (open source + hosted)', 'A dedicated notification-infrastructure platform (multi-channel: email/SMS/push/in-app/chat) with a workflow builder, offered as hosted SaaS or a self-hosted Community Edition.', 'Hosted: Free (10,000 workflow runs/mo, 3 members); Pro from $30/mo; Team from $250/mo. Self-hosted Community Edition exists separately (open source, MongoDB+Redis+S3-backed).', '["Genuinely useful if the hub ever needs to route the SAME event to multiple channels per-recipient with user-managed preferences — a real capability the webhook options above don''t have", "Community Edition is open source and self-hostable without an Enterprise contract, per Novu''s own self-hosting docs", "10,000 free hosted workflow runs/mo is far more than a one-person shop needs, so the SaaS free tier alone might suffice without self-hosting at all"]'::jsonb, '["Self-hosting Community Edition requires running MongoDB, Redis, S3-compatible storage, and multiple Novu services (API/Worker/WS/Dashboard) — exactly the kind of standing multi-service daemon stack that is expensive here even at $0 licence cost", "This is notification infrastructure sized for a product with many end users and preference management, not a control plane with one owner pinging himself — a scale mismatch", "Novu''s own pricing page frames \"self-hosted deployment options\" under Enterprise, which conflicts with the separately documented free Community Edition — worth re-confirming directly before relying on either claim"]'::jsonb, 'If the hub grows into serving many consumer apps'' end users, each with their own notification preferences across multiple channels — not the shape of the problem today.', 'Poor fit right now: it solves a multi-tenant, multi-channel routing problem the hub doesn''t have, at an operational cost (self-hosted) or platform lock-in (hosted) neither of which beats a plain webhook for a single recipient.', 'n/a', 'growing', 'https://docs.novu.co/community/self-hosting-novu/overview', '{"kind": "tiered", "driver": "emails", "base_monthly": 0, "included": 0, "per_unit": 0, "markup_pct": 0, "min_monthly": 0, "annual_only": false, "setup_usd": 0, "confidence": "published", "basis": "Novu hosted: Free 10,000 workflow runs/mo (3 members); Pro from $30/mo (Aug 2026).", "caveat": "Team ($250/mo) tier and the separately-infra-costed self-hosted Community Edition not modeled; workflow runs span email/SMS/push/in-app/chat, not email alone.", "tiers": [{"upto": 10000, "monthly": 0}, {"upto": null, "monthly": 30}]}'::jsonb, 'candidate', '', 'catalogue', 80)
